window.AWD_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Clone the starting checkpoint","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#workspace","excerpt":"Get the starter project onto your computer and create your own branch to save your work.","body":"Choose the starting version The starting tag contains Laravel 12, the Docker configuration and one route for the welcome page. A tag names a particular version of the files. You can browse the finished application to see where the course leads.      Open a terminal on your computer You will need Git, Docker Desktop and a code editor. Start Docker Desktop and wait for the engine to be ready. In your terminal, move to the folder where you usually keep your projects.       git clone -c core.autocrlf=input --branch lesson-01-topic-01 https://github.com/john-dempsey/book-reviews-student.git book-reviews-learning\ncd book-reviews-learning Terminal · your computer     git clone -c core.autocrlf=input --branch lesson-01-topic-01 https://github.com/john-dempsey/book-reviews-student.git book-reviews-learning\ncd book-reviews-learning Make the checkpoint your own Git may report a “detached HEAD” after cloning a tag. Create a branch now so your commits have a name. The clone command sets line endings for this project, which keeps the Docker shell script usable on Windows.       git switch -c my-book-reviews\ngit branch --show-current\ngit describe --tags --exact-match HEAD Terminal · book-reviews-learning     git switch -c my-book-reviews\ngit branch --show-current\ngit describe --tags --exact-match HEAD  The last two commands print my-book-reviews and lesson-01-topic-01, respectively. Open book-reviews-learning in your editor. You should see README.md, compose.yaml, docker/ and src/.       If Git says the destination already exists, use a new folder name in the clone and cd commands. If you already completed this step, return to your existing working folder and run git status to see your current branch.      Get the starter project onto your computer and create your own branch to save your work.    The tag identifies the shared starting point. Your branch moves forward as you commit your work.  ","label":"WORKSPACE"},{"title":"Start the Docker workspace","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#install","excerpt":"Start the Docker containers and open the workspace where you will run Laravel commands.","body":"Open the README in your editor Find Docker Setup and First-time setup . These are the instructions for the version you just cloned. You can also read the tagged README on GitHub . Open compose.yaml alongside it.       docker compose up -d --build\ndocker compose ps Terminal · book-reviews-learning     docker compose up -d --build\ndocker compose ps Read the service names Look for apache-php-container , which serves your app on port 8080, and mysql-container , which stores its data. phpmyadmin-container gives you a browser view of that database on port 8081. The fourth service, workspace-container , gives you PHP, Composer and npm in a terminal. The first build can take several minutes.       docker compose exec workspace-container bash Terminal · book-reviews-learning     docker compose exec workspace-container bash  pwd\nls Terminal · workspace container     pwd\nls  pwd prints /var/www/html and ls includes artisan and composer.json. Compare this listing with src/ in your editor. Docker mounts that folder into the container, so both views refer to the same files. Leave this shell open for the next step.       If Compose cannot find a configuration file, check that your host terminal is in book-reviews-learning beside compose.yaml. If a port or container name is already in use, stop the conflicting local project and run the start command again.      Start the Docker containers and open the workspace where you will run Laravel commands.    The workspace supplies Composer and opens in the mounted Laravel project, beside composer.json.  ","label":"INSTALL"},{"title":"Run the starter application","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#database","excerpt":"Install the packages, set up the database and check that Laravel opens in your browser.","body":"Install the project dependencies You now have the Docker workspace running. Next, install the PHP dependencies that the application needs. Run the commands below inside the workspace-container shell opened in the previous activity. Composer reads composer.lock and installs the required PHP packages into vendor/ .       composer install Terminal · workspace container     composer install Build the browser assets Laravel's Blade templates use Tailwind CSS classes such as grid-cols-3 and h-48 . Those class names only work when Tailwind has generated the corresponding CSS. Vite prepares that CSS and the JavaScript for the browser. First install the npm packages, then run npm run build to create the files in public/build/ . For this Docker-based walkthrough, use npm run build as the default. The alternative, npm run dev , starts a separate Vite server which must remain running and reachable from the browser. You do not need it for these lessons.       npm install --ignore-scripts && npm run build Terminal · workspace container     npm install --ignore-scripts && npm run build  Styles missing? If the page looks unstyled, the books appear in one long column, or book covers are enormous later in the course, the generated Tailwind CSS may be missing or stale. From the Laravel project directory inside the workspace run npm run build , then refresh the browser. If the page still looks wrong, confirm the command completed without errors and that public/build/ exists. If you previously used npm run dev , stop it first and check for a stale public/hot file, which tells Laravel to load assets from the development server instead of the build. Only remove that file when the dev server is no longer running.      Prepare the database Laravel uses migrations to create and update database tables. A migration is a PHP file that describes a change to the database structure, such as creating a table or adding a column. Keeping these instructions in the project means everyone can build the same database structure without creating tables manually in phpMyAdmin. The starting project includes migrations for Laravel's users , cache and jobs tables. The command below runs any migrations that have not already been applied. Laravel records which ones have run, so running it again does not recreate the same tables. migrate:status shows you which migrations have been completed. The project includes database connection settings in its .env files. Leave those settings as supplied.       php artisan migrate\nphp artisan migrate:status Terminal · workspace container     php artisan migrate\nphp artisan migrate:status  Open http://localhost:8080 in your browser. You should see Laravel’s default welcome page. Next, open http://localhost:8081 (phpMyAdmin), select the book_reviews database and find the users , cache and jobs tables. In the terminal, php artisan migrate:status should show these migrations as Ran . You will create the catalogue tables later.      Find the page you just opened In your editor, open src/routes/web.php . The route for / returns view('welcome') , which renders src/resources/views/welcome.blade.php . You have now seen the connection between a URL, a Laravel route and the page displayed in the browser. In the next activity, you will add your own route.       If php artisan migrate reports a connection error on first launch, allow MySQL to finish starting and retry. For an access-denied error, see the troubleshooting section in the tagged README. Its database reset clears local records. For a missing vendor/autoload.php, check that composer install finished successfully.      Return to the project next time To leave the container shell, type exit . From the host project folder, use docker compose stop at the end of a session and docker compose up -d next time. Re-enter the workspace shell before running Artisan commands.      Install the packages, set up the database and check that Laravel opens in your browser.    At this tag, the migrations set up Laravel’s initial tables. Books, authors and reviews will each need migrations as you build the catalogue.  ","label":"DATABASE"},{"title":"Add your first route","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#route","excerpt":"Add a new web address to your app and check that Laravel recognises it.","body":"Follow a request When you visit /books , Laravel looks for a matching route. For now, the route will return plain text. Later it will ask a controller for records and render a view.       Route::get('/books', function () {\n    return 'Your book catalogue starts here.';\n}); src/routes/web.php     Route::get('/books', function () {\n    return 'Your book catalogue starts here.';\n}); Read the code get handles a browser GET request. /books is its URL path. The function runs when that route matches; its return value becomes the response. Keep the existing use Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Route; import at the top.       Visit http://localhost:8080/books. You should see “Your book catalogue starts here.” The existing / page still has its own route.       php artisan route:list --path=books Terminal · workspace container     php artisan route:list --path=books Confirm Laravel recognises your route The route:list command displays routes registered with Laravel. The --path=books option filters the list to routes containing books . Check that you can see a GET route with the URI books . This verifies that Laravel has registered your new route, independently of what the browser displays. If it is missing, check the file you edited and the spelling of the URL.       404? Check the spelling of /books and that you edited src/routes/web.php. A PHP syntax error? Check semicolons, quotes and the closing });. Do not add a second opening <?php tag.      Add a new web address to your app and check that Laravel recognises it.    The path determines which URL matches. Unless another route exists, /books will now return a 404.  ","label":"ROUTE"},{"title":"Build your first book page","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#view","excerpt":"Make a Blade page for your new route instead of displaying plain text.","body":"Separate the page from its route A view holds the HTML. The route chooses which view to return. Create a books folder inside src/resources/views , then create index.blade.php inside it.       Book Reviews Book Reviews A home for your next good read. src/resources/views/books/index.blade.php     <!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n    <title>Book Reviews</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>Book Reviews</h1>\n    <p>A home for your next good read.</p>\n</body>\n</html>  Route::get('/books', function () {\n    return view('books.index');\n}); src/routes/web.php     Route::get('/books', function () {\n    return view('books.index');\n}); A name that maps to a file books.index resolves to resources/views/books/index.blade.php inside Laravel. The dot represents a directory separator. You leave off .blade.php when calling view() .       Refresh /books. You should see a “Book Reviews” heading and the new paragraph. The browser tab should also say “Book Reviews”.       “View [books.index] not found”: check the file is in src/resources/views/books/, is named index.blade.php exactly, and has been saved. Case matters inside Linux containers. It must not be index.blade.php.txt.      Make a Blade page for your new route instead of displaying plain text.    Dot notation maps nested view folders to a short name. The Blade filename is show.blade.php inside books/.  ","label":"VIEW"},{"title":"Pass data into the page","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#data","excerpt":"Send information from a Laravel route to a Blade page and display it.","body":" Route::get('/books', function () {\n    $heading = 'The reading room';\n\n    return view('books.index', ['heading' => $heading]);\n}); src/routes/web.php     Route::get('/books', function () {\n    $heading = 'The reading room';\n\n    return view('books.index', ['heading' => $heading]);\n});  {{ $heading }} src/resources/views/books/index.blade.php     <h1>{{ $heading }}</h1> Connect the two sides The array key heading becomes $heading in the view. Blade’s double braces print the value and escape HTML. The route currently supplies a fixed string; later, controllers will supply records from the database.       Refresh /books. The heading now reads “The reading room”. Change the string in the route and refresh again: the template stays the same.      Try it yourself Pass a second value called description to the view. Use it in the paragraph instead of the fixed text. Aim to edit only the route data and that paragraph.       Hint: put a second key/value pair in the array passed to view(). In the template use {{ $description }}. An “Undefined variable” error means the name was not passed, or its spelling differs.      Send information from a Laravel route to a Blade page and display it.    Escaping means text containing HTML is displayed as text rather than interpreted as markup. Keep this default for user-supplied content.  ","label":"DATA"},{"title":"Check your understanding","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#checkpoint","excerpt":"Build one more page on your own to practise routes, templates and data.","body":"Your challenge Create an /about route and a resources/views/about.blade.php template. Pass a courseName value from the route and display it in a heading. Include a normal HTML link back to /books .       Open /about directly. Your heading comes from the route, the link opens /books, and both pages still work after a refresh.       A possible route: Route::get('/about', function () { return view('about', ['courseName' => 'Book Reviews']); }); In the view, use {{ $courseName }} and Browse books inside a full HTML document.      Trace what you have built The browser requests a URL. Laravel matches a route. Your PHP passes data to a Blade view. Blade produces HTML, which the browser displays. Module 2 will introduce the database records that eventually feed these pages.      Before you move on You should be able to explain where routes live, how a view name maps to a file, and how a PHP value reaches the page. Mark steps complete when you have checked the result, not just copied the code.      Keep exploring Official references: Laravel routing ↗ and Laravel views ↗ . These lesson examples are deliberately simpler than the final application’s authenticated controller routes.      Build one more page on your own to practise routes, templates and data.    Check that the route passes a courseName key and the template uses the same spelling. No database is involved in this page yet.  ","label":"CHECKPOINT"},{"title":"Understand the application and its files","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m02-s01","excerpt":"See how the different parts of a Laravel project work together.","body":"What you will build Your task: Explore the folder structure of the Laravel project, find the routes and views you used in Module 01, and identify where the database models will be created.      Further reading Read the related tutorial topic: project setup/orientation .      Explore the project Before building the catalogue database, get to know the application you have started. You will identify the main parts of Laravel and see how they work together to respond to a browser request.      The domain model The app is a book review catalogue, built around four things: A User can write many Reviews . A Book can have many Reviews — but at most one Review per User per\nBook . That's not just a rule the app remembers to enforce; it's backed\nby a unique constraint in the database itself, so it holds even if a bug\nelsewhere tries to violate it. You'll build that constraint in this\nlesson's migrations topic. A Book can have many Authors , and an Author can have written many\nBooks — a many-to-many relationship, which needs a join table ( author_book )\nin between. If you've drawn an entity-relationship diagram for a\nmany-to-many before, this is exactly that shape. A real catalogue would usually give a Book many Editions (different\nISBNs, publishers, print runs). This app doesn't — a book's edition\ndetails (ISBN, publisher, price, edition number) live directly on the books table, so every Book has exactly one implicit edition. That's a\ndeliberate simplification to keep the schema to a size this course's six\nweeks can comfortably cover, not an oversight. In short:       User ──< Review >── Book ──< author_book >── Author\n        (1 per user            (many-to-many)\n         per book) Diagram     User ──< Review >── Book ──< author_book >── Author\n        (1 per user            (many-to-many)\n         per book) Authentication and permissions The application will let people register and sign in. Authentication checks who a user is; later, Laravel Breeze will provide the registration and login features. Authorisation controls what a signed-in user is allowed to do. For example, when you build reviews, users will be able to edit and delete their own reviews. The application does not introduce separate administrator and regular-user roles.      What is Eloquent? Eloquent is Laravel's object-relational mapper (ORM). It lets you represent database records as PHP objects, called models . Instead of writing SQL for every task, you can use expressions such as Book::find(3) to look up a book and $book->authors to access its authors. You will create these models and relationships in 02-03.      The stack, and what's already running If you haven't already worked through the First-time setup steps in\nthis project's README.md , do that now — the rest of this lesson assumes docker compose up -d --build has been run at least once, composer install and npm install --ignore-scripts && npm run build have completed inside the\nworkspace container, and php artisan migrate has run without error. That setup brings up four Docker Compose services, defined in compose.yaml and the docker/ folder alongside it: Service What it is URL apache-php-container Serves the Laravel app http://localhost:8080 mysql-container MySQL 8, holding the app's database localhost:3306 phpmyadmin-container phpMyAdmin, for browsing that database http://localhost:8081 workspace-container A terminal for php artisan , composer , npm — This is the environment you brought up during setup, so none of it\nshould be unfamiliar: one container serves the app, one runs the database,\none gives you a browser-based way to look inside that database. The one\naddition is workspace-container — it doesn't serve anything itself, it's\njust a shell ( docker compose exec workspace-container bash ) with PHP,\nComposer, and Node installed, so you have somewhere to run command-line\ntools without needing them inside the container that's serving live\ntraffic. Right now, with first-time setup done, you should be able to see: The Laravel app , at http://localhost:8080 — Laravel's own default\nwelcome page. The catalogue tables and models are the next additions\nbuilding. phpMyAdmin , at http://localhost:8081 — log in and you'll find a book_reviews database already containing a handful of tables\n( users , sessions , cache , jobs , and a few others) that Laravel\ncreates by default. No books , authors , or reviews tables yet —\nthose come from migrations you're about to write. The workspace , reachable only via docker compose exec workspace-container bash — no URL, since it isn't a web server. This is\nwhere every php artisan and composer command in this lesson gets\nrun.      Where the Laravel app itself came from The project in src/ already exists, so you won't run this yourself — but\nit's worth knowing what created it, because you'll see this command again\non your own projects. laravel new . scaffolds a fresh Laravel project\ninto the current directory, and asks a handful of setup questions along\nthe way. This project answered: No starter kit — no built-in auth scaffolding yet. That's what\nYou will install Breeze in Module 03. PHPUnit for testing, not the alternative Pest. MySQL as the database, matching the mysql-container service above. What you get back is a bare Laravel installation: the framework, its\ndefault folder structure, and three default migrations (for users , cache , and jobs ) — but no Breeze, no app-specific models or\nmigrations. Laravel does scaffold one model and one factory by default,\nthough: User ( app/Models/User.php ) and UserFactory ( database/factories/UserFactory.php ), both generic and both there\nbefore you write a single line of app-specific code. That is the starting database and model structure — users , cache , and jobs tables, a User model, a UserFactory , and nothing yet for books , authors , or reviews — and exactly what the rest of this lesson builds on top of.      What this lesson does Over the next activities, you will: Explore how Laravel routes, views and models fit together. Write migrations for books , authors , author_book and reviews . Create Eloquent models and define their relationships. Write tests to check the models and database constraints. Authentication is introduced in Module 03, once the models and basic database structure are in place.       Read the related tutorial topic: project setup/laravel application structure .      Tour the Laravel folders Before writing any app-specific code, it's worth knowing the shape of what laravel new . already gave you, and how a request actually travels\nthrough it. You have already added a simple route and view in Module 01. Now you will explore the framework files that support them.      How requests reach Laravel In the prior PHP module, a URL usually pointed at a real .php file on\ndisk — /about.php served about.php . Laravel doesn't work that way.\nApache's document root points at src/public/ , and that folder contains\nan .htaccess file with a rewrite rule at the bottom of it:       RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d\nRewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f\nRewriteRule ^ index.php [L] Code example     RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d\nRewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f\nRewriteRule ^ index.php [L] Read the rewrite rule Read as English: if the requested path isn't a real directory and isn't a\nreal file, rewrite it to index.php instead. So /books , /books/3 , and /anything-that-doesn't-exist-as-a-file all end up running the exact same\nPHP file — public/index.php , Laravel's front controller . Its actual\njob is short:       $app = require_once __DIR__.'/../bootstrap/app.php';\n\n$app->handleRequest(Request::capture()); public/index.php     $app = require_once __DIR__.'/../bootstrap/app.php';\n\n$app->handleRequest(Request::capture()); The front controller It boots the framework, hands it the incoming request, and lets Laravel\ndecide what to do with it. Nothing about which code runs is decided by\nthe URL rewriting itself — that's the router's job, covered next. The\nrewrite rule's only job is making sure every request that isn't a real\nfile gets to Laravel at all.      Models, views and controllers Laravel is a Model-View-Controller framework — not by forcing rigid\nseparation, but by giving each part its own folder and convention: A Model represents one thing your data cares about (a Book , a Review ) and knows how to load and save its own rows. You will create those models in this module. A View turns data into the actual HTML response — a Blade template here, not plain PHP echo statements. A Controller sits between the two: a request arrives, the\nController asks a Model for whatever data it needs, then hands that\ndata to a View to render. Strung together, one request looks like this:       Browser → routes/web.php → Controller → Model (talks to the database)\n                                ↓\n                             Blade View → HTML back to the browser Diagram     Browser → routes/web.php → Controller → Model (talks to the database)\n                                ↓\n                             Blade View → HTML back to the browser Follow the request The router decides which Controller (or closure) handles a given URL —\nthat's the piece still missing from the picture above.      Find the welcome route routes/web.php is where a URL pattern gets matched to the code that\nhandles it. You have already added a /books route in Module 01. The starter route for the welcome page looks like this:       Route::get('/', function () {\n    return view('welcome');\n}); Code example     Route::get('/', function () {\n    return view('welcome');\n}); Read the route definition Route::get() handles GET requests to the path / . Here, it returns the welcome view. In Module 01 you also registered a route for /books ; later you will connect that address to a controller method instead of returning a view directly.      Where things live Folder What lives there What's already there app/Models/ Eloquent model classes, one per database table User.php app/Http/Controllers/ Controller classes Controller.php — an empty base class every controller will extend resources/views/ Blade templates ( .blade.php ) welcome.blade.php routes/web.php Maps URLs to closures or controller methods The one route above database/migrations/ One file per schema change, applied in order users , cache , jobs config/ One PHP file per subsystem, each reading its values from .env database.php , among many others public/ Anything Apache serves directly index.php , plus the static content below artisan Laravel's command-line entry point — a single file, not a folder Every php artisan ... command runs through it Almost all of the code you write for the rest of this course lands in the\nfirst four rows.      The artisan CLI artisan is the file behind every command-line interaction you'll have\nwith Laravel this course: php artisan migrate (mentioned at the end of\nthe last topic), php artisan make:model , php artisan tinker for\npoking at models interactively, and more as the course goes on. It isn't\na folder holding code you'll write yourself — it's the command-line\ninterface, dispatching to code that already exists inside the framework\nor your own app. You'll always run it from the workspace container's\nterminal, never from your own machine directly.      The public folder and static content public/ is the folder that the web server serves directly. index.php is Laravel's entry point; files such as favicon.ico , robots.txt and generated CSS or JavaScript assets can be served without going through a Laravel route. By contrast, a URL such as /books normally reaches public/index.php , and Laravel chooses which route should handle it.      Migrations and seed data database/migrations/ is where the migrations mentioned at the end of the\nlast topic actually live — right now, just the three Laravel ships by\ndefault ( users , cache , jobs ). database/factories/ and database/seeders/ exist too, holding only the default UserFactory for\nnow; you will use these in Module 03. Laravel reads its database connection details from .env , not from a\nconfig file you'd edit directly:       DB_CONNECTION=mysql\nDB_HOST=mysql\nDB_DATABASE=book_reviews\nDB_USERNAME=laravel Environment settings     DB_CONNECTION=mysql\nDB_HOST=mysql\nDB_DATABASE=book_reviews\nDB_USERNAME=laravel Database connection settings config/ is where those .env values actually get consumed — one PHP\nfile per subsystem ( app.php , database.php , session.php , and more),\neach reading env() calls into plain PHP arrays that the rest of Laravel\nuses. config/database.php specifically is the bridge for the block\nabove, and it's why changing a DB_* value in .env changes which\ndatabase php artisan migrate talks to, with no other file to edit. DB_HOST=mysql here is the same mysql-container you saw running in\nphpMyAdmin in the last topic — this .env is how Laravel knows to look\nfor it there instead of on your own machine.      Testing tests/ splits into tests/Unit/ and tests/Feature/ , both extending\nthe shared tests/TestCase.php . Unit tests check one class in isolation —\na model's relationships, for example; feature tests make real HTTP\nrequests against a route and check the response, closer to how a browser\nwould actually use the app. Both run against the same MySQL database the\napp itself uses — there's no separate, lighter test database here — with\nthe RefreshDatabase trait resetting it between tests so one test's data\nnever leaks into the next. Right now tests/ is essentially empty — you will write model tests in activity 02-04, once there's an actual schema and actual models\nworth testing.       You can point to public/index.php, routes/web.php, resources/views and app/Models and describe each responsibility.       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      See how the different parts of a Laravel project work together.    A route chooses how a request is handled; the controller or closure chooses a response, often a Blade view.  ","label":"M02-S01"},{"title":"Design the four catalogue tables","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m02-s02","excerpt":"Create the database tables that will hold books, authors and reviews.","body":"What you will build Your task: Generate each migration in dependency order. Add the columns and foreign keys shown, then run migrate:status. Your migration filenames will contain timestamps generated on your machine.      Further reading Read the related tutorial topic: project setup/migrations .      Plan the new tables You will add four migrations: books , authors , author_book (which links books to their authors) and reviews . Each migration is a PHP file describing the structure of one table. Laravel applies migrations in filename order and records which ones have already run.      Generate the migration files Migration filenames begin with timestamps. Laravel uses these to determine the order in which changes run. This matters here: books and authors must exist before author_book can reference them; books and the existing users table must exist before reviews . From the workspace container, generate the four files in the order shown below. Check the resulting filenames in src/database/migrations/ : the timestamp portion should put books , authors , author_book and reviews in that order. If two files get the same timestamp because they were generated in the same second, adjust the filename timestamps so the dependencies are created first.       php artisan make:migration create_books_table\nphp artisan make:migration create_authors_table\nphp artisan make:migration create_author_book_table\nphp artisan make:migration create_reviews_table Terminal     php artisan make:migration create_books_table\nphp artisan make:migration create_authors_table\nphp artisan make:migration create_author_book_table\nphp artisan make:migration create_reviews_table Examine a generated migration Open the newly generated create_books_table file. Its up() method creates the table, while down() defines how to undo that change. Replace the Schema::create(...) body with the version shown in the next step. Your timestamped filenames will differ from the examples below. Use the filenames generated on your computer, not the dates in the example paths.       Schema::create('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); Code example     Schema::create('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); Add the table definitions Update the up() method in each migration with the relevant Schema::create(...) code below. Keep the generated <?php , use imports, class declaration and down() method intact. Each down() method should use Schema::dropIfExists() for that table.      Define the books table Start with the books migration. The table stores the title, publication year, optional description and cover image, plus basic edition details.       Schema::create('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->string('title');\n    $table->text('description')->nullable();\n    $table->year('year');\n    $table->string('image')->nullable();\n\n    // Part 1 keeps one implicit edition per book, rather than a\n    // separate editions table - these four fields describe that\n    // one edition directly.\n    $table->string('isbn')->nullable();\n    $table->string('publisher')->nullable();\n    $table->string('edition_number')->nullable();\n    $table->decimal('price', 8, 2)->nullable();\n\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); database/migrations/2026_09_12_065917_create_books_table.php     Schema::create('books', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->string('title');\n    $table->text('description')->nullable();\n    $table->year('year');\n    $table->string('image')->nullable();\n\n    // Part 1 keeps one implicit edition per book, rather than a\n    // separate editions table - these four fields describe that\n    // one edition directly.\n    $table->string('isbn')->nullable();\n    $table->string('publisher')->nullable();\n    $table->string('edition_number')->nullable();\n    $table->decimal('price', 8, 2)->nullable();\n\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); Understand the book columns Most of this reads directly off your SQL knowledge: string is VARCHAR(255) , text is TEXT , decimal(8, 2) is exactly the SQL type\nof the same name. year() is one you might not have reached for by\nhand — MySQL has a dedicated YEAR type, and this is the column it's\nfor. $table->id() is the one genuinely new piece: an auto-incrementing BIGINT UNSIGNED primary key named id , which is also the column every foreignId() below assumes it's pointing at.       Schema::create('authors', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->string('name');\n    $table->string('image')->nullable();\n    $table->text('bio')->nullable();\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); database/migrations/2026_09_12_065936_create_authors_table.php     Schema::create('authors', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->string('name');\n    $table->string('image')->nullable();\n    $table->text('bio')->nullable();\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); Why authors have their own table No foreign keys yet — an Author doesn't belong to anything else. The\nrelationship between books and authors lives in the next table instead.       Schema::create('author_book', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->foreignId('book_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->foreignId('author_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); database/migrations/2026_09_12_065948_create_author_book_table.php     Schema::create('author_book', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->foreignId('book_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->foreignId('author_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->timestamps();\n}); Link books and authors A Book can have many Authors, and an Author can have written many Books —\na many-to-many relationship, which needs a table in between holding pairs\nof IDs. author_book is exactly that join table, named the way Laravel\nexpects: the two related table names, singular, alphabetical, joined with\nan underscore. $table->foreignId('book_id') on its own just adds a BIGINT UNSIGNED column named book_id ; ->constrained() is what turns\nit into a real foreign key — it works out, from the column's own name,\nthat book_id should reference books.id . Running SHOW CREATE TABLE author_book afterward shows exactly the constraint you'd expect to have\nwritten by hand:       CONSTRAINT `author_book_book_id_foreign` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `books` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE,\nCONSTRAINT `author_book_author_id_foreign` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `authors` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE SQL example     CONSTRAINT `author_book_book_id_foreign` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `books` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE,\nCONSTRAINT `author_book_author_id_foreign` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `authors` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE Cascade deletes ->onDelete('cascade') on both means deleting a Book or an Author also\ndeletes the pairing rows that mentioned it — without it, that delete\nwould simply fail with a foreign-key-constraint error instead, since a\ndangling author_book row referencing a Book that no longer exists isn't\nallowed.       Schema::create('reviews', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->foreignId('book_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->foreignId('user_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->unsignedTinyInteger('rating');\n    $table->text('comment')->nullable();\n    $table->timestamps();\n\n    // At most one review per user per book - enforced here, not\n    // just in application code.\n    $table->unique(['book_id', 'user_id']);\n}); database/migrations/2026_09_12_070000_create_reviews_table.php     Schema::create('reviews', function (Blueprint $table) {\n    $table->id();\n    $table->foreignId('book_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->foreignId('user_id')->constrained()->onDelete('cascade');\n    $table->unsignedTinyInteger('rating');\n    $table->text('comment')->nullable();\n    $table->timestamps();\n\n    // At most one review per user per book - enforced here, not\n    // just in application code.\n    $table->unique(['book_id', 'user_id']);\n}); Prevent duplicate reviews rating only ever needs to hold 1–5, so unsignedTinyInteger — SQL's\nsmallest integer type — is a better fit than a plain integer ; nothing\nabout the app changes if the column happens to be smaller than it needs\nto be, but it's worth picking the type that actually matches the data.\nThe last line is doing real work: a unique composite index across\ntwo columns together, so the pair (book_id, user_id) can't repeat —\none row per user for a given book is fine, and one row per book for a\ngiven user is fine, but the same user reviewing the same book twice would\nviolate the index and fail at the database level, not just get caught by\na validation rule that a bug could bypass.      Apply the new schema Once all four files are saved, run the migration command below once . --step records each migration separately, making it easier to reverse one later if needed. Laravel creates the new tables in timestamp order. If you see an error about a missing referenced table, check the timestamp order of your migration filenames and correct it before continuing.       php artisan migrate --step\nphp artisan migrate:status Terminal · workspace container     php artisan migrate --step\nphp artisan migrate:status Understand the migration status In migrate:status , look for the four new migrations marked Ran . Your timestamps and batch numbers may be different from those in the example below. Running php artisan migrate again will only apply migrations that are still pending.      Why the --step option matters Without --step , migrations run together in the same batch. With it, each migration gets its own batch, so you can undo the most recent change without necessarily undoing the others. Terminal      $ php artisan migrate:status\n\n  Migration name .............................................. Batch / Status\n  0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table ............................... [1] Ran\n  0001_01_01_000001_create_cache_table ............................... [1] Ran\n  0001_01_01_000002_create_jobs_table ................................ [1] Ran\n  2026_09_12_065917_create_books_table ................................ [2] Ran\n  2026_09_12_065936_create_authors_table ............................... [3] Ran\n  2026_09_12_065948_create_author_book_table ........................... [4] Ran\n  2026_09_12_070000_create_reviews_table ................................ [5] Ran Example · migration status     $ php artisan migrate:status\n\n  Migration name .............................................. Batch / Status\n  0001_01_01_000000_create_users_table ............................... [1] Ran\n  0001_01_01_000001_create_cache_table ............................... [1] Ran\n  0001_01_01_000002_create_jobs_table ................................ [1] Ran\n  2026_09_12_065917_create_books_table ................................ [2] Ran\n  2026_09_12_065936_create_authors_table ............................... [3] Ran\n  2026_09_12_065948_create_author_book_table ........................... [4] Ran\n  2026_09_12_070000_create_reviews_table ................................ [5] Ran If you need to reverse a migration Each migration contains a down() method. php artisan migrate:rollback uses it to undo the last batch; --step=1 limits the rollback to the most recently applied migration. You do not need to roll anything back for this activity. The examples below are for reference only.       php artisan migrate:rollback --step=1\n# If you deliberately rolled one migration back, reapply it:\nphp artisan migrate --step Optional reference · do not run during setup     php artisan migrate:rollback --step=1\n# If you deliberately rolled one migration back, reapply it:\nphp artisan migrate --step Working with migrations later For now, check the schema and continue to the model files. Once a migration has been shared and used by others, changes to the database structure should normally be added in a new migration rather than editing one that has already been applied.       In phpMyAdmin, you can see books , authors , author_book and reviews . In php artisan migrate:status , each is marked Ran . The reviews migration includes a unique constraint for book_id and user_id .       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      Create the database tables that will hold books, authors and reviews.    It stores the many-to-many relationship between books and authors.  ","label":"M02-S02"},{"title":"Build models and relationships","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m02-s03","excerpt":"Create PHP model classes and connect books, authors, users and reviews.","body":"What you will build Your task: Create three model files, add their fillable fields and relationship methods, then add one relationship to the existing User model. Finally, use Tinker to confirm that the book–author relationship works.      Further reading Read the related tutorial topic: project setup/eloquent models and relationships .      Models and Eloquent Your migrations created the tables, but you still need PHP classes to work with their records. Laravel's Eloquent ORM connects models to database tables. For example, Book::find(3) looks for the book with ID 3, and Book::create([...]) can insert a new book. Eloquent uses naming conventions: Book maps to books , Author maps to authors and Review maps to reviews . You do not need a separate model for author_book because Eloquent manages that pivot table through the relationship methods.      Generate the model files Open the workspace-container terminal and run the commands below, from the Laravel project directory. The make:model command creates a PHP class in app/Models/ . The project already has app/Models/User.php , so do not create another User model . After running the commands, check that your editor shows src/app/Models/Book.php , Author.php and Review.php . The src/ prefix is visible on your computer; inside the container Laravel starts at that folder.       php artisan make:model Book\nphp artisan make:model Author\nphp artisan make:model Review Terminal · workspace container     php artisan make:model Book\nphp artisan make:model Author\nphp artisan make:model Review Create the Book model Open src/app/Models/Book.php and update it to the code below. protected $fillable lists the attributes Eloquent is allowed to set through Book::create() . This prevents unexpected fields being mass-assigned when the application later accepts form data. The authors() method uses belongsToMany() to link books and authors through author_book . The reviews() method uses hasMany() because a book can have multiple reviews.       belongsToMany(Author::class);\n    }\n\n    public function reviews(): HasMany\n    {\n        return $this->hasMany(Review::class);\n    }\n} src/app/Models/Book.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Models;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\HasFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\BelongsToMany;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\HasMany;\n\nclass Book extends Model\n{\n    use HasFactory;\n\n    protected $fillable = [\n        'title',\n        'description',\n        'year',\n        'image',\n        'isbn',\n        'publisher',\n        'edition_number',\n        'price',\n    ];\n\n    public function authors(): BelongsToMany\n    {\n        return $this->belongsToMany(Author::class);\n    }\n\n    public function reviews(): HasMany\n    {\n        return $this->hasMany(Review::class);\n    }\n} Create the Author model Open src/app/Models/Author.php and update it as shown. The books() method is the other side of the many-to-many book–author relationship.       belongsToMany(Book::class);\n    }\n} src/app/Models/Author.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Models;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\HasFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\BelongsToMany;\n\nclass Author extends Model\n{\n    use HasFactory;\n\n    protected $fillable = [\n        'name',\n        'image',\n        'bio',\n    ];\n\n    public function books(): BelongsToMany\n    {\n        return $this->belongsToMany(Book::class);\n    }\n} Create the Review model Open src/app/Models/Review.php . A review belongs to one book and one user, so it uses two belongsTo() relationships. Its fillable fields include the IDs that link it to those records.       belongsTo(Book::class);\n    }\n\n    public function user(): BelongsTo\n    {\n        return $this->belongsTo(User::class);\n    }\n} src/app/Models/Review.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Models;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\HasFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\BelongsTo;\n\nclass Review extends Model\n{\n    use HasFactory;\n\n    protected $fillable = [\n        'book_id',\n        'user_id',\n        'rating',\n        'comment',\n    ];\n\n    public function book(): BelongsTo\n    {\n        return $this->belongsTo(Book::class);\n    }\n\n    public function user(): BelongsTo\n    {\n        return $this->belongsTo(User::class);\n    }\n} Add the reviews relationship to User Laravel already provided src/app/Models/User.php . Open it, and keep its existing authentication code . Add the HasMany import with the other use statements near the top of the file, then add the reviews() method inside the User class, before its final closing brace.       use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\HasMany; src/app/Models/User.php · add among the imports     use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Relations\\HasMany;  public function reviews(): HasMany\n{\n    return $this->hasMany(Review::class);\n} src/app/Models/User.php · add inside User class     public function reviews(): HasMany\n{\n    return $this->hasMany(Review::class);\n} Understand the different relationships A Book can have many authors and an Author can have many books: both use belongsToMany() . A Book or a User can have many reviews: they use hasMany() . Each individual Review refers back to one Book and one User with belongsTo() . When you call $book->authors() with parentheses, you get the relationship object, which can be used to add links. When you access $book->authors as a property, Eloquent loads the related author records.       $book->authors()->attach($author); // add a row to author_book\n$book->authors;                    // load this book's authors Eloquent relationship example     $book->authors()->attach($author); // add a row to author_book\n$book->authors;                    // load this book's authors Try it in Tinker Laravel Tinker lets you run PHP against your application without building a web page. Start it with the command below. When you see the interactive prompt, enter the PHP statements in the next code panel one at a time. Do not type a $ shell prompt or a > Tinker prompt. These records are written to your local development database, so they will still be there after you leave Tinker. You can inspect them in phpMyAdmin.       php artisan tinker Terminal · workspace container     php artisan tinker  $book = \\App\\Models\\Book::create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n$author = \\App\\Models\\Author::create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']);\n$book->authors()->attach($author);\n$book->authors->pluck('name'); Tinker · PHP statements     $book = \\App\\Models\\Book::create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n$author = \\App\\Models\\Author::create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']);\n$book->authors()->attach($author);\n$book->authors->pluck('name'); Check what happened The final Tinker statement should return a collection containing Frank Herbert . Type exit when you are finished. In the next step, you will verify the records directly in the database. The model classes here are starter implementations. Later modules extend them with factories and search functionality.      Verify the records in phpMyAdmin Open phpMyAdmin and select your book_reviews database. Choose Browse for books and authors to find the rows you created in Tinker. Then open author_book : it should contain a record linking those two IDs. Check that the IDs match the corresponding book and author rows. This is how you can confirm that attach() wrote the relationship to the database.       You can see your new Book and Author rows and their link in the author_book table in phpMyAdmin. You have also created the three models and added the reviews() relationship to User. Next, you will test them.       If the make:model command cannot find Artisan, enter the workspace container first. If you see a MassAssignmentException, check the $fillable array. If a relationship method is undefined, check the spelling, imports and class braces in the relevant model. If you get a SQL table-not-found error, check that the migrations in 02-02 ran.      Create PHP model classes and connect books, authors, users and reviews.    belongsToMany: the author_book table supports multiple authors on multiple books.  ","label":"M02-S03"},{"title":"Write your first model tests","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m02-s04","excerpt":"Create test files and check that your models and their relationships work.","body":"Your goal Create three PHP test files, one for each model. The tests will save temporary example records and check that the database and model relationships behave as expected. You will write the files yourselves, rather than copying the finished project’s later factory tests.      Where test files belong Inside the Laravel project, find src/tests/Unit (or tests/Unit from inside the workspace container). Tests in this folder are PHP classes. Laravel uses PHPUnit to run methods whose names start with test_ . Each test performs an action and checks its result with an assertion .      Generate three test classes Open the Docker workspace terminal you used in earlier activities. Run these commands from inside the Laravel directory . They create BookTest.php , AuthorTest.php and ReviewTest.php under tests/Unit . If a file already exists, inspect it rather than overwriting your work.       php artisan make:test BookTest --unit\nphp artisan make:test AuthorTest --unit\nphp artisan make:test ReviewTest --unit Terminal · workspace     php artisan make:test BookTest --unit\nphp artisan make:test AuthorTest --unit\nphp artisan make:test ReviewTest --unit What the generated files contain Open tests/Unit/BookTest.php . The generator creates a small test class. For these database tests, we need it to extend Tests\\TestCase (not the plain PHPUnit TestCase class), because Laravel’s testing helpers and database setup are required. The complete examples below include that change, all the use imports and the test methods.      Keep database tests safe These tests use RefreshDatabase . It uses transactions when possible and can also run migrations to prepare the database. It is not a guarantee that existing local data will be preserved : the initial migration setup can rebuild tables. The supplied project does not define a separate test database in phpunit.xml , so only run the examples against a disposable local development database (or a separately configured test database), never a database containing work you need to keep.      Test the Book model Replace the generated contents of tests/Unit/BookTest.php with the following complete class. It checks that a book can be saved, authors can be attached, and a book can have reviews. Check the namespace and use declarations at the top before running it.       'The Pragmatic Programmer', 'year' => 1999]);\n\n        $this->assertDatabaseHas('books', ['title' => 'The Pragmatic Programmer']);\n        $this->assertSame(1999, $book->year);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_has_many_authors(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'Clean Code', 'year' => 2008]);\n        $author = Author::create(['name' => 'Robert C. Martin']);\n        $book->authors()->attach($author);\n\n        $this->assertCount(1, $book->authors);\n        $this->assertTrue($book->authors->first()->is($author));\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_has_many_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'Refactoring', 'year' => 1999]);\n        $user = User::create([\n            'name' => 'Test User',\n            'email' => 'book.test@example.com',\n            'password' => 'password',\n        ]);\n        $book->reviews()->create(['user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 5]);\n\n        $this->assertCount(1, $book->reviews);\n    }\n} src/tests/Unit/BookTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Unit;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass BookTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_a_book_can_be_created(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'The Pragmatic Programmer', 'year' => 1999]);\n\n        $this->assertDatabaseHas('books', ['title' => 'The Pragmatic Programmer']);\n        $this->assertSame(1999, $book->year);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_has_many_authors(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'Clean Code', 'year' => 2008]);\n        $author = Author::create(['name' => 'Robert C. Martin']);\n        $book->authors()->attach($author);\n\n        $this->assertCount(1, $book->authors);\n        $this->assertTrue($book->authors->first()->is($author));\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_has_many_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'Refactoring', 'year' => 1999]);\n        $user = User::create([\n            'name' => 'Test User',\n            'email' => 'book.test@example.com',\n            'password' => 'password',\n        ]);\n        $book->reviews()->create(['user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 5]);\n\n        $this->assertCount(1, $book->reviews);\n    }\n} Test the Author model Open the generated tests/Unit/AuthorTest.php and replace its contents with the class below. The second test checks the relationship in the opposite direction, from an author to their books.       'Andy Hunt']);\n\n        $this->assertDatabaseHas('authors', ['name' => 'Andy Hunt']);\n    }\n\n    public function test_an_author_has_many_books(): void\n    {\n        $author = Author::create(['name' => 'Martin Fowler']);\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'Refactoring', 'year' => 1999]);\n        $author->books()->attach($book);\n\n        $this->assertCount(1, $author->books);\n        $this->assertTrue($author->books->first()->is($book));\n    }\n} src/tests/Unit/AuthorTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Unit;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass AuthorTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_an_author_can_be_created(): void\n    {\n        Author::create(['name' => 'Andy Hunt']);\n\n        $this->assertDatabaseHas('authors', ['name' => 'Andy Hunt']);\n    }\n\n    public function test_an_author_has_many_books(): void\n    {\n        $author = Author::create(['name' => 'Martin Fowler']);\n        $book = Book::create(['title' => 'Refactoring', 'year' => 1999]);\n        $author->books()->attach($book);\n\n        $this->assertCount(1, $author->books);\n        $this->assertTrue($author->books->first()->is($book));\n    }\n} Test reviews and the unique constraint Complete tests/Unit/ReviewTest.php using the code below. The two small makeBook() and makeUser() methods belong inside this class ; they simply avoid repeating the setup in every test. The last test deliberately tries to create two reviews for the same book and user, checking that the database rejects the duplicate.       makeBook();\n        $user = $this->makeUser();\n        $review = Review::create([\n            'book_id' => $book->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'rating' => 4,\n            'comment' => 'Excellent read.',\n        ]);\n\n        $this->assertDatabaseHas('reviews', ['rating' => 4]);\n        $this->assertTrue($review->book->is($book));\n        $this->assertTrue($review->user->is($user));\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_user_can_have_many_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = $this->makeUser();\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $this->makeBook()->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 3]);\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $this->makeBook()->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 4]);\n\n        $this->assertCount(2, $user->reviews);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_user_cannot_review_the_same_book_twice(): void\n    {\n        $book = $this->makeBook();\n        $user = $this->makeUser();\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 5]);\n\n        $this->expectException(UniqueConstraintViolationException::class);\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 1]);\n    }\n\n    private function makeBook(): Book\n    {\n        return Book::create(['title' => 'Test Book', 'year' => 2000]);\n    }\n\n    private function makeUser(): User\n    {\n        return User::create([\n            'name' => 'Test User',\n            'email' => 'review.test@example.com',\n            'password' => 'password',\n        ]);\n    }\n} src/tests/Unit/ReviewTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Unit;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\UniqueConstraintViolationException;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass ReviewTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_a_review_belongs_to_a_book_and_a_user(): void\n    {\n        $book = $this->makeBook();\n        $user = $this->makeUser();\n        $review = Review::create([\n            'book_id' => $book->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'rating' => 4,\n            'comment' => 'Excellent read.',\n        ]);\n\n        $this->assertDatabaseHas('reviews', ['rating' => 4]);\n        $this->assertTrue($review->book->is($book));\n        $this->assertTrue($review->user->is($user));\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_user_can_have_many_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = $this->makeUser();\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $this->makeBook()->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 3]);\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $this->makeBook()->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 4]);\n\n        $this->assertCount(2, $user->reviews);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_user_cannot_review_the_same_book_twice(): void\n    {\n        $book = $this->makeBook();\n        $user = $this->makeUser();\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 5]);\n\n        $this->expectException(UniqueConstraintViolationException::class);\n        Review::create(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 1]);\n    }\n\n    private function makeBook(): Book\n    {\n        return Book::create(['title' => 'Test Book', 'year' => 2000]);\n    }\n\n    private function makeUser(): User\n    {\n        return User::create([\n            'name' => 'Test User',\n            'email' => 'review.test@example.com',\n            'password' => 'password',\n        ]);\n    }\n} Run one class, then all three Save all three files. Start by running the book tests; this makes errors easier to trace. Then run all three tests. If the suite also runs other tests generated by Laravel, that is normal. Use the terminal output to find the test file and line number when something fails.       php artisan test --filter=BookTest\nphp artisan test --filter=AuthorTest\nphp artisan test --filter=ReviewTest Terminal · workspace     php artisan test --filter=BookTest\nphp artisan test --filter=AuthorTest\nphp artisan test --filter=ReviewTest Read a passing assertion assertDatabaseHas('books', [...]) asks whether a matching row exists in the database. assertCount(1, $book->authors) checks the number of related models. expectException(...) means the test succeeds only if that specific error is thrown. These are checks of the models you created in 02-03, without introducing factories ahead of the next module.       All three test classes pass on your disposable local test data. You can explain why a failed assertion points to a particular model, relationship or database rule.       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      Create test files and check that your models and their relationships work.    It gives tests a predictable schema and isolates database state.  ","label":"M02-S04"},{"title":"Install Breeze and test sign-in","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m03-s01","excerpt":"Add registration and sign-in pages, then create an account and log in to your app.","body":"What you will build Your task: Install Breeze, then open the new registration page, create a test account, sign out and sign in again. You should finish this activity logged in to your local Laravel app. Run the installation commands in the Docker workspace terminal. Use your browser for the account checks.      Further reading Read the related tutorial topic: project setup/installing laravel and breeze .      Add registration and login You have already used Composer to install PHP dependencies and npm to build frontend assets during setup. This activity uses those familiar tools to add Laravel Breeze, which provides registration and login screens, authentication routes and related code. First, look briefly at what each package manager does; then install Breeze and verify the new pages.      How Composer manages PHP packages Laravel itself, and everything it depends on, arrived in src/ as\nComposer packages — you saw vendor/ get populated by composer install during first-time setup. composer.json is the list of packages a\nproject depends on, by name and version constraint:       \"require\": {\n    \"php\": \"^8.2\",\n    \"laravel/framework\": \"^12.0\",\n    \"laravel/tinker\": \"^2.10.1\"\n} composer.json     \"require\": {\n    \"php\": \"^8.2\",\n    \"laravel/framework\": \"^12.0\",\n    \"laravel/tinker\": \"^2.10.1\"\n}  composer install reads a second file, composer.lock , and installs the exact versions it records — that's what makes first-time setup\nreproducible across machines. composer update is different: it re-reads composer.json 's version constraints, works out the newest versions that\nstill satisfy them, and rewrites composer.lock to match. Adding a\nbrand-new package — which is what installing Breeze means — always goes\nthrough update , since there's nothing in the lock file for a package\nthat isn't in composer.json yet.      How npm builds the frontend assets package.json lists the JavaScript and frontend packages. npm installs them. Tailwind provides CSS utility classes used in the Blade templates, and Vite builds the CSS/JavaScript files into public/build/ . Without that generated CSS, Tailwind class names in HTML have no effect. A three-column book grid can appear as one column with huge book covers even though the Blade markup is correct. npm run build generates the static assets the Laravel app can serve directly. Use this as your default for our Docker walkthrough , including after installing Breeze or whenever styles aren't appearing. npm run dev starts a separate development asset server for live CSS/JavaScript updates. It only works as intended while that server is running and accessible; it isn't necessary for the walkthrough. Blade's @vite(...) directive loads the assets. With no development server active, Laravel should load the built files from public/build/ .      The starter kit this project didn't install laravel new (the command that created src/ before this lesson) asks\nwhich starter kit to scaffold in, alongside the questions covered in\nthe last topic. A starter kit is a ready-made authentication setup —\nregistration, login, and a starting frontend stack — installed in the\nsame step as the framework itself. This project answered None ,\ndeliberately: every starter kit laravel new currently offers builds on\nInertia (React, Vue, or Livewire), wiring up an SPA-shaped frontend this\ncourse isn't using. Skipping the prompt doesn't mean skipping authentication — it means\ninstalling it separately, afterward, from a stack that fits this course: Laravel Breeze .      Installing Breeze Breeze isn't part of the starter-kit prompt above; it's an ordinary\nComposer package, added like any other:       composer require laravel/breeze --dev Terminal     composer require laravel/breeze --dev  --dev marks it a development-only dependency — Breeze generates auth\ncode into your app once, it isn't something the running app loads at\nrequest time, so it has no business shipping to a production install\nthe way laravel/framework does. Breeze's own installer then does the\nactual scaffolding, and takes one argument: which frontend stack to\ngenerate:       php artisan breeze:install blade Terminal     php artisan breeze:install blade  blade is the stack this app uses throughout — plain Blade views and\ncontrollers, no Inertia, matching the \"None\" choice above. (Breeze also\noffers react , vue , and api stacks; not used here.) This command generates the authentication routes, views and controllers. Depending on the installer and environment, frontend dependencies and assets may also be built, but you should explicitly run the build command in the next step to ensure the generated styling is up to date.      What actually landed routes/auth.php — registration, login, logout, and password-reset\nroutes, require d from the bottom of routes/web.php . resources/views/auth/ — a Blade view per route above; dashboard.blade.php and resources/views/profile/ for a logged-in user's own account page. app/Http/Controllers/Auth/ and ProfileController.php — the\ncontrollers those routes call. tests/Feature/Auth/ and ProfileTest.php — Breeze ships its own\nfeature tests for every route it just generated. Nothing about the database changed — Breeze's Blade stack reuses the users migration laravel new already created, so there's nothing new\nto migrate here. One smaller, purely cosmetic change is worth naming so it\ndoesn't look like a mistake later: Breeze's installer replaced the bare\ninstall's Tailwind v4 setup (a plugin in vite.config.js , configured via\nan @theme block in app.css itself) with the older tailwind.config.js postcss.config.js pair and Tailwind v3 — Breeze's Blade stack still\ntargets that setup. Both are Tailwind; only the configuration shape\ndiffers. None of this touches authoriz ation — who's allowed to do what. Breeze\nonly answers who's logged in , the same distinction raised at the start\nof this lesson. Check your routes: Breeze may replace the earlier routes/web.php file when it installs its dashboard and authentication pages. If the temporary /books route from Module 01 has disappeared, that is expected. You will add the book routes again in Module 04.      Build and check the Breeze styles Before opening the registration page, run the frontend build from the Laravel project directory ( /var/www/html in the Docker workspace, corresponding to src/ on your computer). This ensures that the Tailwind CSS generated for Breeze is available to the browser, even if the installer already attempted a build.       npm run build Terminal · workspace container / Laravel project     npm run build  Visual check: Open http://localhost:8080/register . The form should have spacing, aligned fields and styled buttons. If it looks like plain HTML, the build may not have loaded. Check the build output for errors, confirm public/build/ was created, and refresh. Later, if book covers are huge or the index becomes one column on desktop, use this same check. A working layout should not depend on leaving npm run dev running.       If you tried npm run dev : Stop it with Ctrl+C before switching to npm run build . Laravel may use a public/hot marker to request assets from the development server. If that marker remains after the server stops, Laravel might keep pointing at the wrong asset source. Check for it and remove it only if the dev server is no longer running. Do not change Blade grid classes to compensate for unstyled pages.      Register and sign in to your app Once Breeze has finished installing, open http://localhost:8080/register in your browser. You should see a registration form. Enter a name, an email address for local testing (for example, student@example.test ) and a password you choose for this practice account. Do not reuse a real password. Select Register . Laravel should create your user record and take you to /dashboard . This confirms that registration worked and that you are signed in. Use the account menu to Log Out . Open http://localhost:8080/login . Sign in using the same email and password. You should return to the dashboard. Stay signed in for the next activities, when you will be browsing book and author pages. If you are redirected to /login later, sign in using this account. This is an account in your local Laravel app. It is separate from the learning website, which saves your lesson progress in your browser.       You have created a test account at /register , seen the dashboard, logged out and successfully logged back in at /login . You should now be signed in. You can also open phpMyAdmin and find the new entry in the users table.       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      Add registration and sign-in pages, then create an account and log in to your app.    It supplies account routes, session handling and layouts used by the finished application.  ","label":"M03-S01"},{"title":"Create factories for reliable sample records","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m03-s02","excerpt":"Create sample books, authors and reviews automatically instead of entering each one by hand.","body":"What you will build Your task: Create BookFactory, AuthorFactory and ReviewFactory. Use Faker for sensible values and practise creating one model from tinker.      Further reading Read the related tutorial topic: seeding and browsing/writing factories .      Writing factories Every row you've created so far has been typed out by hand. The model\ntests from the last topic built a Book like this:       $book = Book::create([\n    'title' => 'The Pragmatic Programmer',\n    'year' => 1999,\n]); tests/Unit/BookTest.php     $book = Book::create([\n    'title' => 'The Pragmatic Programmer',\n    'year' => 1999,\n]);  That's fine for proving a schema works - one or two rows, with values you\nchose because they made the test's point. It falls apart the moment you\nwant twenty books that don't all look suspiciously identical, or just\nwant something in the database to look at while building a page,\nwithout caring what its title actually is. A factory is a class that\nknows how to produce one plausible-looking row for a model, as many\ntimes as you ask for it.      Creating a factory Generate a factory for each of the three models. Run these commands from your Laravel project directory. Laravel creates the corresponding PHP files in database/factories/ .       php artisan make:factory AuthorFactory --model=Author\nphp artisan make:factory BookFactory --model=Book\nphp artisan make:factory ReviewFactory --model=Review Terminal     php artisan make:factory AuthorFactory --model=Author\nphp artisan make:factory BookFactory --model=Book\nphp artisan make:factory ReviewFactory --model=Review  */\nclass AuthorFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * @return array */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            //\n        ];\n    }\n} Code example     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Factories;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\Factory;\n\n/**\n * @extends Factory<Author>\n */\nclass AuthorFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * @return array<string, mixed>\n     */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            //\n        ];\n    }\n}  Each generated factory begins with an empty definition() method. This method returns the attributes Laravel uses to build sample records. Add the HasFactory trait to each model, as shown below, so you can call Author::factory() , Book::factory() and Review::factory() . The User model already has the trait.       use Database\\Factories\\AuthorFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\HasFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\n\nclass Author extends Model\n{\n    /** @use HasFactory */\n    use HasFactory;\n\n    protected $fillable = [\n        // ...\n    ];\n} Code example     use Database\\Factories\\AuthorFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\HasFactory;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model;\n\nclass Author extends Model\n{\n    /** @use HasFactory<AuthorFactory> */\n    use HasFactory;\n\n    protected $fillable = [\n        // ...\n    ];\n}  Add HasFactory to Book and Review in the same way. The factory classes and the model classes have different namespaces: factories live in Database\\Factories , while Eloquent models live in App\\Models . This matters when one factory uses another model.      Filling in the definition definition() returns an array of column-name => value, exactly like the\narrays you've been passing to Model::create() - the difference is every\nvalue here comes from Faker , a library that generates realistic-looking\nrandom data, via the fake() helper. AuthorFactory is the simplest one:       */\nclass AuthorFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * @return array */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'name' => fake()->name(),\n            'image' => null,\n            'bio' => fake()->paragraph(),\n        ];\n    }\n} database/factories/AuthorFactory.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Factories;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\Factory;\n\n/**\n * @extends Factory<Author>\n */\nclass AuthorFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * @return array<string, mixed>\n     */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'name' => fake()->name(),\n            'image' => null,\n            'bio' => fake()->paragraph(),\n        ];\n    }\n}  fake()->name() returns a plausible full name (a different one every\ncall), fake()->paragraph() a plausible paragraph of prose. image is\nleft null on purpose - nothing generates a real image file, and the\ncolumn is nullable. BookFactory leans on a few more of Faker's generators:       */\nclass BookFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * @return array */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'title' => rtrim(fake()->sentence(random_int(2, 5)), '.'),\n            'description' => fake()->paragraphs(3, true),\n            'year' => fake()->numberBetween(1950, (int) date('Y')),\n            'image' => null,\n            'isbn' => fake()->isbn13(),\n            'publisher' => fake()->company(),\n            'edition_number' => (string) fake()->numberBetween(1, 5),\n            'price' => fake()->randomFloat(2, 9.99, 79.99),\n        ];\n    }\n} database/factories/BookFactory.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Factories;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\Factory;\n\n/**\n * @extends Factory<Book>\n */\nclass BookFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * @return array<string, mixed>\n     */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'title' => rtrim(fake()->sentence(random_int(2, 5)), '.'),\n            'description' => fake()->paragraphs(3, true),\n            'year' => fake()->numberBetween(1950, (int) date('Y')),\n            'image' => null,\n            'isbn' => fake()->isbn13(),\n            'publisher' => fake()->company(),\n            'edition_number' => (string) fake()->numberBetween(1, 5),\n            'price' => fake()->randomFloat(2, 9.99, 79.99),\n        ];\n    }\n}  Worth pausing on a couple of these: fake()->sentence(2, 5) produces a short sentence like \"Voluptas et\nfugiat.\" - it always ends in a full stop, which reads oddly as a book\ntitle, so rtrim(..., '.') strips it back off. year is bounded between 1950 and this actual year ( date('Y') ,\ncast to an int) rather than a fixed upper limit that would quietly go\nstale - it always generates a plausible publication year, not a\nhardcoded one that ages badly. edition_number is cast to a string with (string) because the column\nitself is a string in the migration (Part 1 keeps edition details as\nplain fields on books , rather than a separate editions table) - numberBetween() returns an int , and the factory's job is to match\nthe column's actual type.      A factory that depends on other rows: ReviewFactory A review belongs to a book and is written by a user. Open database/factories/ReviewFactory.php and use the complete class below. Notice the use App\\Models\\Book; and use App\\Models\\User; lines: without these imports, PHP looks for the classes in the factory namespace, causing a “Class Database\\Factories\\Book not found” error.       */\nclass ReviewFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * A review always needs a book and a user - by default this makes a\n     * new one of each, but the seeder overrides both so that reviews land\n     * on the books/users it already created instead.\n     *\n     * @return array */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'book_id' => Book::factory(),\n            'user_id' => User::factory(),\n            'rating' => fake()->numberBetween(1, 5),\n            'comment' => fake()->boolean(80) ? fake()->paragraph() : null,\n        ];\n    }\n} database/factories/ReviewFactory.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Factories;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Factories\\Factory;\n\n/**\n * @extends Factory<Review>\n */\nclass ReviewFactory extends Factory\n{\n    /**\n     * Define the model's default state.\n     *\n     * A review always needs a book and a user - by default this makes a\n     * new one of each, but the seeder overrides both so that reviews land\n     * on the books/users it already created instead.\n     *\n     * @return array<string, mixed>\n     */\n    public function definition(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'book_id' => Book::factory(),\n            'user_id' => User::factory(),\n            'rating' => fake()->numberBetween(1, 5),\n            'comment' => fake()->boolean(80) ? fake()->paragraph() : null,\n        ];\n    }\n}  book_id and user_id aren't Faker values - they're other factories ,\nnested inside this one. Ask for Review::factory()->create() with nothing\noverridden, and Eloquent doesn't just leave those columns empty: it runs Book::factory() and User::factory() first, creates a brand new book\nand a brand new user to go with them, and only then creates the review\npointing at both. That's a sensible default for a factory used on its\nown - it always produces a complete, valid row, never a review with a book_id pointing nowhere - even though it's rarely what you actually\nwant once specific books and users already exist to attach reviews to\ninstead. comment shows a different kind of variation: fake()->boolean(80) returns true about 80% of the time, so most reviews get a written\ncomment and some are left with just a star rating - null , since the\ncolumn is nullable.      Check your factory imports Before trying these factories in Tinker, check that the ReviewFactory header imports App\\Models\\Book and App\\Models\\User . The same applies to the model imports in the other factory files. Save your changes before running the commands below.      Using a factory Every factory gives you the same handful of methods, regardless of which\nmodel it belongs to. create() builds a model instance and saves it to the database\nstraight away:       Author::factory()->create(); Code example     Author::factory()->create();  count() (or the shorthand of passing a number straight to factory() ) makes several at once:       Book::factory()->count(3)->create();\nBook::factory(3)->create();   // same thing Code example     Book::factory()->count(3)->create();\nBook::factory(3)->create();   // same thing  Overriding attributes - pass an array to create() , same as Model::create() , and only those keys are pinned; everything else still\ncomes from definition() :       Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']); Code example     Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);  Handy when a test cares about one specific value (a known title to search\nfor, say) but doesn't care what the rest of the row looks like. make() is create() 's quieter sibling - it builds the model object\nwith the same fake attributes but never saves it, so nothing touches the\ndatabase:       $book = Book::factory()->make();   // in memory only, not in `books` Code example     $book = Book::factory()->make();   // in memory only, not in `books`  Rarely what you want for a book or author, since there's nothing to look\nat until it's actually saved - but worth knowing it exists, and that create() is really just make() plus a save() call, the same\nrelationship your own Active-Record-style classes had between building an\nobject and persisting it.       $ php artisan tinker\n> \\App\\Models\\Author::factory()->create();\n= App\\Models\\Author {#7842\n    name: \"Dr. Providenci Wolf\",\n    image: null,\n    bio: \"Quia rerum et dolores nam. Vitae nihil quod...\",\n    id: 1,\n    updated_at: \"2026-09-15 10:03:41\",\n    created_at: \"2026-09-15 10:03:41\",\n  }\n> \\App\\Models\\Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n= App\\Models\\Book {#7869\n    title: \"Dune\",\n    description: \"Sunt magni ex nihil...\",\n    year: 1998,\n    image: null,\n    isbn: \"978-1-4028-9462-6\",\n    publisher: \"Kunde-Bahringer\",\n    edition_number: \"3\",\n    price: \"42.17\",\n    id: 1,\n    updated_at: \"2026-09-15 10:04:02\",\n    created_at: \"2026-09-15 10:04:02\",\n  }\n> \\App\\Models\\Review::factory()->create(); Terminal     $ php artisan tinker\n> \\App\\Models\\Author::factory()->create();\n= App\\Models\\Author {#7842\n    name: \"Dr. Providenci Wolf\",\n    image: null,\n    bio: \"Quia rerum et dolores nam. Vitae nihil quod...\",\n    id: 1,\n    updated_at: \"2026-09-15 10:03:41\",\n    created_at: \"2026-09-15 10:03:41\",\n  }\n> \\App\\Models\\Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n= App\\Models\\Book {#7869\n    title: \"Dune\",\n    description: \"Sunt magni ex nihil...\",\n    year: 1998,\n    image: null,\n    isbn: \"978-1-4028-9462-6\",\n    publisher: \"Kunde-Bahringer\",\n    edition_number: \"3\",\n    price: \"42.17\",\n    id: 1,\n    updated_at: \"2026-09-15 10:04:02\",\n    created_at: \"2026-09-15 10:04:02\",\n  }\n> \\App\\Models\\Review::factory()->create(); Trying it out with tinker Every value except title above is Faker's doing, and will be different\nevery time you run it yourself - that last Review::factory()->create() is worth trying and then checking books and users in phpMyAdmin\nafterwards: a brand new book and a brand new user will have appeared,\nneither of which you asked for by name, exactly as the nested-factory\nbehaviour above describes.      What's still missing Every row created so far - in tinker , in last lesson's tests, and\nabove - has existed one at a time, created because someone typed a\ncommand. Nothing yet produces a whole catalogue in one go: a fixed number\nof users and authors, books attached to the right authors, reviews\nattached to the right books and users, in the kind of quantity and shape\na real (if fictional) book review site would actually have. That's the\nnext topic's job.       A BookFactory can make a valid Book independently of manually inserting a row.       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      Create sample books, authors and reviews automatically instead of entering each one by hand.    It generates repeatable test or demo records with realistic defaults.  ","label":"M03-S02"},{"title":"Write and run the seeders","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m03-s03","excerpt":"Add sample records to your database so there is something to browse in your app.","body":"What you will build Your task: Create the four seeder classes, add the complete PHP code to each file, and register them in DatabaseSeeder . You will then populate the database with five users, five authors, twenty books and sixty reviews.      Further reading Read the related tutorial topic: seeding and browsing/seeders and demo data .      Seeders and demo data Everything the last topic built - AuthorFactory , BookFactory , ReviewFactory - still only runs when someone types a command in tinker . Nothing produces the whole catalogue at once: a fixed number of\nusers and authors, books attached to the right authors, reviews attached\nto the right books and users, all in one go. That's what a seeder does - a class that scripts a batch of factory calls (and whatever else\nis needed) into one repeatable command.      Create the seeder files From the Laravel project directory, generate one seeder for each set of records. These commands create PHP files in database/seeders ; they do not insert records yet.       php artisan make:seeder UserSeeder\nphp artisan make:seeder AuthorSeeder\nphp artisan make:seeder BookSeeder\nphp artisan make:seeder ReviewSeeder Terminal     php artisan make:seeder UserSeeder\nphp artisan make:seeder AuthorSeeder\nphp artisan make:seeder BookSeeder\nphp artisan make:seeder ReviewSeeder Add the UserSeeder code Open database/seeders/UserSeeder.php and replace its contents with the complete example below. The line use App\\Models\\User; is important: without it, PHP looks for a class called Database\\Seeders\\User , which does not exist.       'Emma Watson', 'email' => 'emma.watson84@example.com'],\n            ['name' => \"Liam O'Connor\", 'email' => 'liam.oconnor22@example.com'],\n            ['name' => 'Sophia Chen', 'email' => 'sophia.chen91@example.com'],\n            ['name' => 'Carlos Silva', 'email' => 'carlos.silva33@example.com'],\n            ['name' => 'Aisha Patel', 'email' => 'aisha.patel55@example.com'],\n        ];\n\n        foreach ($users as $user) {\n            User::factory()->create($user);\n        }\n    }\n} database/seeders/UserSeeder.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Seeders;\n\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Seeder;\n\nclass UserSeeder extends Seeder\n{\n    /**\n     * Seed the users table.\n     *\n     * Five fixed, named accounts rather than random ones - easier to\n     * recognise while browsing the seeded catalogue, and to log in as\n     * while testing.\n     */\n    public function run(): void\n    {\n        $users = [\n            ['name' => 'Emma Watson', 'email' => 'emma.watson84@example.com'],\n            ['name' => \"Liam O'Connor\", 'email' => 'liam.oconnor22@example.com'],\n            ['name' => 'Sophia Chen', 'email' => 'sophia.chen91@example.com'],\n            ['name' => 'Carlos Silva', 'email' => 'carlos.silva33@example.com'],\n            ['name' => 'Aisha Patel', 'email' => 'aisha.patel55@example.com'],\n        ];\n\n        foreach ($users as $user) {\n            User::factory()->create($user);\n        }\n    }\n} Add the AuthorSeeder code Next, open database/seeders/AuthorSeeder.php . This seeder uses AuthorFactory to create five authors. Notice that it imports App\\Models\\Author .       create();\n    }\n} database/seeders/AuthorSeeder.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Seeders;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Seeder;\n\nclass AuthorSeeder extends Seeder\n{\n    /**\n     * Seed the authors table.\n     */\n    public function run(): void\n    {\n        Author::factory(5)->create();\n    }\n} Add the BookSeeder code In database/seeders/BookSeeder.php , create twenty books and connect them to the authors already in the database. Every book receives one author, and two randomly selected books receive a second author. Both model imports are required.       create();\n\n        // Most books have a single author.\n        $books->each(function (Book $book) use ($authors) {\n            $book->authors()->attach($authors->random());\n        });\n\n        // A couple of books are co-written - attach a second, different\n        // author to two randomly chosen books.\n        $books->random(2)->each(function (Book $book) use ($authors) {\n            $firstAuthorId = $book->authors->first()->id;\n\n            $secondAuthor = $authors\n                ->reject(fn (Author $author) => $author->id === $firstAuthorId)\n                ->random();\n\n            $book->authors()->attach($secondAuthor);\n        });\n    }\n} database/seeders/BookSeeder.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Seeders;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Seeder;\n\nclass BookSeeder extends Seeder\n{\n    /**\n     * Seed the books table.\n     *\n     * Books are a weak entity here - a book is meaningless without at\n     * least one author to attach to the author_book pivot - so this must\n     * run after AuthorSeeder.\n     */\n    public function run(): void\n    {\n        $authors = Author::all();\n\n        $books = Book::factory(20)->create();\n\n        // Most books have a single author.\n        $books->each(function (Book $book) use ($authors) {\n            $book->authors()->attach($authors->random());\n        });\n\n        // A couple of books are co-written - attach a second, different\n        // author to two randomly chosen books.\n        $books->random(2)->each(function (Book $book) use ($authors) {\n            $firstAuthorId = $book->authors->first()->id;\n\n            $secondAuthor = $authors\n                ->reject(fn (Author $author) => $author->id === $firstAuthorId)\n                ->random();\n\n            $book->authors()->attach($secondAuthor);\n        });\n    }\n} Add the ReviewSeeder code Open database/seeders/ReviewSeeder.php . It uses existing books and users, rather than creating new ones for each review. The full class below includes imports for Book , Review and User .       each(function (Book $book, int $index) use ($users, $reviewCounts) {\n            $reviewerCount = $reviewCounts[$index];\n\n            if ($reviewerCount === 0) {\n                return;\n            }\n\n            $users->random($reviewerCount)->each(function (User $user) use ($book) {\n                Review::factory()->create([\n                    'book_id' => $book->id,\n                    'user_id' => $user->id,\n                ]);\n            });\n        });\n    }\n} database/seeders/ReviewSeeder.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Seeders;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Seeder;\n\nclass ReviewSeeder extends Seeder\n{\n    /**\n     * Seed the reviews table.\n     *\n     * Reviews are the weakest entity of all - each one belongs to both a\n     * book and a user - so this must run after BookSeeder and\n     * UserSeeder.\n     *\n     * Every book gets between 0 and 5 reviews, one per user at most\n     * (the reviews table has a unique index on book_id + user_id, so a\n     * user can't review the same book twice), and at least two books end\n     * up with no reviews at all. The counts below always add up to 60;\n     * shuffling them means it isn't always the same books left without\n     * reviews.\n     */\n    public function run(): void\n    {\n        $users = User::all();\n        $books = Book::all();\n\n        $reviewCounts = [\n            0, 0,\n            5, 5, 5, 5,\n            4, 4, 4, 4,\n            3, 3, 3, 3,\n            2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2,\n        ];\n        shuffle($reviewCounts);\n\n        $books->each(function (Book $book, int $index) use ($users, $reviewCounts) {\n            $reviewerCount = $reviewCounts[$index];\n\n            if ($reviewerCount === 0) {\n                return;\n            }\n\n            $users->random($reviewerCount)->each(function (User $user) use ($book) {\n                Review::factory()->create([\n                    'book_id' => $book->id,\n                    'user_id' => $user->id,\n                ]);\n            });\n        });\n    }\n} Register the seeders Finally, open the existing database/seeders/DatabaseSeeder.php and replace its contents with the example below. Laravel runs these seeders in the order listed, so users and authors exist before books and reviews need them. This file does not need to import the User model: it calls the UserSeeder class, which performs that work.       call([\n            UserSeeder::class,\n            AuthorSeeder::class,\n            BookSeeder::class,\n            ReviewSeeder::class,\n        ]);\n    }\n} database/seeders/DatabaseSeeder.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Database\\Seeders;\n\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Console\\Seeds\\WithoutModelEvents;\nuse Illuminate\\Database\\Seeder;\n\nclass DatabaseSeeder extends Seeder\n{\n    use WithoutModelEvents;\n\n    /**\n     * Seed the application's database.\n     *\n     * Order matters: the strong entities (users, authors) are seeded\n     * first, since the weak entities that follow (books, then reviews)\n     * depend on rows already existing to attach to - a book needs an\n     * author, and a review needs both a book and a user.\n     */\n    public function run(): void\n    {\n        $this->call([\n            UserSeeder::class,\n            AuthorSeeder::class,\n            BookSeeder::class,\n            ReviewSeeder::class,\n        ]);\n    }\n} Check your files before running You should now have five seeder files: UserSeeder.php , AuthorSeeder.php , BookSeeder.php , ReviewSeeder.php and DatabaseSeeder.php . Each begins with namespace Database\\Seeders; ; each model referenced by its short name has a corresponding use App\\Models\\…; import. The short class names for the seeders inside DatabaseSeeder work because those classes are in the same Database\\Seeders namespace.      Why that order isn't arbitrary Swap two of those lines - say, run BookSeeder before AuthorSeeder -\nand seeding fails immediately:       Database\\Seeders\\BookSeeder ........................................ RUNNING\n\nInvalidArgumentException\n\nYou requested 1 items, but there are only 0 items available.\n\nat database/seeders/BookSeeder.php:26 Example output     Database\\Seeders\\BookSeeder ........................................ RUNNING\n\nInvalidArgumentException\n\nYou requested 1 items, but there are only 0 items available.\n\nat database/seeders/BookSeeder.php:26  BookSeeder asks for Author::all() and then attaches a random one to\nevery book. Run it before AuthorSeeder , and Author::all() comes back\nempty - there's nothing yet to pick a random one from . The rule this\nfollows is the same one from designing the schema back in Lesson 1: strong entities before the weak entities that depend on them. Users\nand authors don't need anything else to exist first; books do (an\nauthor, to attach to); reviews depend on the most, needing both a book\nand a user already in place. Seed in that order - users, authors, books,\nreviews - and every later seeder finds exactly what it needs already\nsitting in the database.      BookSeeder : attaching authors that already exist $authors = Author::all() only makes sense given the ordering rule\nabove - it's the five rows AuthorSeeder already created. Twenty books\nget created first, unattached to anyone; then every book gets exactly\none random existing author attached via $book->authors()->attach() ,\nthe same many-to-many method from the Eloquent relationships topic.\nTwo books, chosen at random, get a second author attached afterwards - reject() filters that second pick down to authors who aren't already\nattached to this particular book, so a book can never end up \"co-written\"\nwith itself.      ReviewSeeder : working around the factory's own default Last topic's ReviewFactory gives book_id and user_id a default\nworth revisiting here:       // ReviewFactory.php\n'book_id' => Book::factory(),\n'user_id' => User::factory(), Code example     // ReviewFactory.php\n'book_id' => Book::factory(),\n'user_id' => User::factory(),  That default is exactly what makes Review::factory()->create() useful\non its own in tinker - it always produces a complete row, inventing a\nbook and a user to go with it if none are supplied. Inside a seeder,\nthat's the opposite of helpful. Twenty books and five users already\nexist, created on purpose, in a specific quantity - a review seeder that\nlet every review invent its own extra book and user would blow both\nthose numbers open. It's easy to see happen:       > App\\Models\\Book::count(); App\\Models\\User::count();\n= 20\n= 5\n> App\\Models\\Review::factory()->create();\n> App\\Models\\Book::count(); App\\Models\\User::count();\n= 21\n= 6 Code example     > App\\Models\\Book::count(); App\\Models\\User::count();\n= 20\n= 5\n> App\\Models\\Review::factory()->create();\n> App\\Models\\Book::count(); App\\Models\\User::count();\n= 21\n= 6  One unguarded Review::factory()->create() call, and there's a 21st book\nand a 6th user that nobody asked for. ReviewSeeder avoids this by never\ncalling Review::factory()->create() without overriding both foreign\nkeys:       Passing 'book_id' => $book->id and 'user_id' => $user->id overrides\nthe factory's defaults completely - Book::factory() and User::factory() never run, because the values they'd have filled in\nare already supplied. Only rating and comment still come from ReviewFactory 's own Faker calls. The rest of the method controls how many reviews land on each book. $reviewCounts is twenty numbers, one per book, that always add up to\nsixty and stay within 0-5; shuffle() means it isn't always the same two\nbooks that end up with none. For whichever count a given book gets, $users->random($reviewerCount) picks that many different users out of\nthe five - never the same user twice for the same book, which matters\nbecause reviews has a unique index on book_id + user_id (from the\nmigrations topic back in Lesson 1) that would reject a second review by\nthe same user on the same book outright.       php artisan migrate:fresh --seed Terminal     php artisan migrate:fresh --seed Running it For a disposable local development database , migrate:fresh --seed drops all tables, recreates them and runs DatabaseSeeder . It deletes existing data , so do not use it on any database you need to preserve. Alternatively, php artisan db:seed runs only the seeders, without recreating tables. Because these seeders use fixed user email addresses, running them twice against the same data will produce a duplicate-email error.       Illuminate\\Database\\UniqueConstraintViolationException\n\nSQLSTATE[23000]: Integrity constraint violation: 1062 Duplicate entry\n'emma.watson84@example.com' for key 'users.users_email_unique' Example output     Illuminate\\Database\\UniqueConstraintViolationException\n\nSQLSTATE[23000]: Integrity constraint violation: 1062 Duplicate entry\n'emma.watson84@example.com' for key 'users.users_email_unique'  UserSeeder tries to insert emma.watson84@example.com again, and the\nsame unique constraint that stops two people registering with the same\nemail stops the seeder too. While working on just one seeder, db:seed --class= targets a single one instead of the whole batch - handy for,\nsay, tweaking BookSeeder without waiting on UserSeeder and AuthorSeeder to run again first:       php artisan db:seed --class=BookSeeder Terminal     php artisan db:seed --class=BookSeeder  $ php artisan tinker\n> App\\Models\\User::count();\n= 5\n> App\\Models\\Author::count();\n= 5\n> App\\Models\\Book::count();\n= 20\n> App\\Models\\Review::count();\n= 60\n> App\\Models\\Book::has('authors', '>=', 2)->count();\n= 2\n> App\\Models\\Book::doesntHave('reviews')->count();\n= 2 Terminal     $ php artisan tinker\n> App\\Models\\User::count();\n= 5\n> App\\Models\\Author::count();\n= 5\n> App\\Models\\Book::count();\n= 20\n> App\\Models\\Review::count();\n= 60\n> App\\Models\\Book::has('authors', '>=', 2)->count();\n= 2\n> App\\Models\\Book::doesntHave('reviews')->count();\n= 2 Checking the result Five users, five authors, twenty books, sixty reviews, two co-written\nbooks, two books with no reviews yet - exactly the shape this topic set\nout to build, and all of it reproducible from nothing with one command.      Confirm the records in phpMyAdmin Open phpMyAdmin and select the database configured for your Laravel project. Check that the users and authors tables contain five rows each, books contains twenty and reviews contains sixty. Open author_book to see the links between books and authors. If you have run Tinker experiments earlier, you may see extra rows until you reset your disposable database.      What's still missing The catalogue is real now, but there's still no way to actually browse\nit - no page lists the books, no page shows one book's authors and\nreviews. That's this lesson's next topic.       The catalogue has books linked to authors and reviews connected to users and books.       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      Add sample records to your database so there is something to browse in your app.    The later records need valid foreign keys to rows that already exist.  ","label":"M03-S03"},{"title":"Inspect relationships and seeded data","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m03-s04","excerpt":"Use phpMyAdmin and Laravel to check that your sample records are linked correctly.","body":"Check the populated database In the previous two activities you wrote factories and seeders. Now inspect the records they produced. Start with the data you already seeded: there is no need to run migrate:fresh --seed again simply to inspect it, because that command deletes and recreates your tables.      Browse the rows in phpMyAdmin Open phpMyAdmin and select book_reviews . Open books , authors , author_book , users and reviews . Identify a book ID in books , find that ID in author_book , and follow its author_id back to an author. In reviews , follow a review’s book_id and user_id to the related records.      Inspect the same relationships using Tinker Use Tinker to ask Eloquent for a book and its authors. The code loads both sides of the link for you. Read the IDs and names in the output, then compare them with what you saw in phpMyAdmin.       php artisan tinker Terminal · workspace     php artisan tinker  $book = \\App\\Models\\Book::with('authors', 'reviews.user')->first();\n$book?->title;\n$book?->authors->pluck('name');\n$book?->reviews->pluck('rating'); Tinker · PHP statements     $book = \\App\\Models\\Book::with('authors', 'reviews.user')->first();\n$book?->title;\n$book?->authors->pluck('name');\n$book?->reviews->pluck('rating'); Check the counts Confirm that the seeders produced records in each main table. Your counts depend on the number of times you have run seeders or used Tinker, so do not expect the numbers to match perfectly if you have already added rows by hand.       \\App\\Models\\Book::count();\n\\App\\Models\\Author::count();\n\\App\\Models\\User::count();\n\\App\\Models\\Review::count(); Tinker · PHP statements     \\App\\Models\\Book::count();\n\\App\\Models\\Author::count();\n\\App\\Models\\User::count();\n\\App\\Models\\Review::count(); Questions to answer Choose one book with reviews. Which user wrote one of those reviews? Can you find that user’s name through $review->user ? Does $book->authors match the rows in author_book ? These checks demonstrate the difference between the underlying foreign keys and the relationships Eloquent makes available in PHP.       You can trace at least one book to its author(s), and one review to its book and user, both in phpMyAdmin and Tinker.       If something fails, read the error message and check the file or command used in this step. For route problems try php artisan route:list; for failing tests try php artisan test. Check spelling, namespaces, method names and database relationships before moving on.      Use phpMyAdmin and Laravel to check that your sample records are linked correctly.    It eager-loads the authors relationship along with the book.  ","label":"M03-S04"},{"title":"Create the catalogue routes and controllers","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m04-s01","excerpt":"Add the book and author web addresses, then connect them to the controllers that will handle each page.","body":"What you will do In Module 01 you created a temporary GET /books route. During Module 03 you installed Laravel Breeze with Blade. That installation can replace or rewrite routes/web.php , so your earlier route may already be gone . In this activity you will create BookController and AuthorController , then add their routes alongside Breeze's existing routes. The Blade pages will be created in 04-02 and 04-03.      1. Generate the controllers From your terminal inside the workspace container , in the Laravel application folder (the same place you ran earlier Artisan commands), run:       php artisan make:controller BookController\nphp artisan make:controller AuthorController Terminal · workspace container     php artisan make:controller BookController\nphp artisan make:controller AuthorController  Laravel creates app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php and app/Http/Controllers/AuthorController.php . In your project folder on your computer these are under src/app/Http/Controllers/ . Open each file in your editor.      2. Implement BookController Replace the contents of src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php with the complete class below. The use App\\Models\\Book; import is required for the Book model.       withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Book $book)\n    {\n        $book->load(['authors', 'reviews.user']);\n\n        return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n    }\n} src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\n\nclass BookController extends Controller\n{\n    public function index()\n    {\n        $books = Book::with('authors')\n            ->withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Book $book)\n    {\n        $book->load(['authors', 'reviews.user']);\n\n        return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n    }\n}  index() retrieves books in title order, with their authors, number of reviews and average rating. paginate(12) returns twelve books per page. show(Book $book) receives a book through route model binding and loads its authors and reviews (including each reviewer). The view(...) calls refer to books/index.blade.php and books/show.blade.php . We will prepare these templates in 04-02. Your original beginner books/index.blade.php might still exist, but is not yet the catalogue view this controller expects.      3. Implement AuthorController Open src/app/Http/Controllers/AuthorController.php and replace its contents with this complete class:       orderBy('name')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('authors.index', compact('authors'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Author $author)\n    {\n        $author->load(['books' => function ($query) {\n            $query->with('authors')\n                ->withCount('reviews')\n                ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating');\n        }]);\n\n        return view('authors.show', compact('author'));\n    }\n} src/app/Http/Controllers/AuthorController.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\n\nclass AuthorController extends Controller\n{\n    public function index()\n    {\n        $authors = Author::withCount('books')\n            ->orderBy('name')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('authors.index', compact('authors'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Author $author)\n    {\n        $author->load(['books' => function ($query) {\n            $query->with('authors')\n                ->withCount('reviews')\n                ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating');\n        }]);\n\n        return view('authors.show', compact('author'));\n    }\n}  index() fetches authors and their book counts. show(Author $author) loads the selected author’s books, including the details that the book-card template will need. Laravel will use the {author} URL segment to find the correct model.      4. Add catalogue routes to the Breeze routes file Open src/routes/web.php in your editor. Find the routes Breeze created, including the dashboard, profile routes and the require __DIR__.'/auth.php'; statement near the bottom. First check whether the temporary Route::get('/books', function () { ... }); from Module 01 is still present. If you find it, remove just that route so it will not conflict with the new books.index route. If it has disappeared after installing Breeze, there is nothing to remove. Add these two imports near the top, alongside ProfileController :       use App\\Http\\Controllers\\AuthorController;\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController; src/routes/web.php · imports     use App\\Http\\Controllers\\AuthorController;\nuse App\\Http\\Controllers\\BookController;  Next, find the existing Route::middleware('auth')->group(function () { block created when you installed Breeze. Keep its profile routes and add the two new resource lines inside that same block , before its closing }); . This example shows the whole block so you can see exactly where the new lines go:       Route::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'edit'])->name('profile.edit');\n    Route::patch('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'update'])->name('profile.update');\n    Route::delete('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'destroy'])->name('profile.destroy');\n\n    // New: read-only catalogue routes.\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show']);\n    Route::resource('authors', AuthorController::class)->only(['index', 'show']);\n}); src/routes/web.php · authenticated routes     Route::middleware('auth')->group(function () {\n    Route::get('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'edit'])->name('profile.edit');\n    Route::patch('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'update'])->name('profile.update');\n    Route::delete('/profile', [ProfileController::class, 'destroy'])->name('profile.destroy');\n\n    // New: read-only catalogue routes.\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show']);\n    Route::resource('authors', AuthorController::class)->only(['index', 'show']);\n});  Important: that is a single auth group, not a second group to paste below the existing one. You are adding two resource routes to the existing group. Leave require __DIR__.'/auth.php'; at the bottom of web.php . Route::resource() can register seven standard routes, but only(['index', 'show']) registers just the two actions you have written for each controller. Create/edit/delete routes come later, when their methods exist.      5. Confirm Laravel recognises the routes In your Laravel workspace terminal, run the commands below. They check the route definitions before any Blade templates have been created. If the routes appear here, Laravel knows where to send the requests.       php artisan route:list --path=books\nphp artisan route:list --path=authors Terminal · workspace container     php artisan route:list --path=books\nphp artisan route:list --path=authors  You should see GET|HEAD books named books.index , GET|HEAD books/{book} named books.show , and the equivalent authors.index and authors.show entries. All four should point to their respective controller methods. There should be no duplicate temporary GET /books closure route .      6. Understand the URL parameters A request to /books/4 matches books/{book} . Laravel sees show(Book $book) , finds the Book with that ID, and passes it into the method. If no record exists, the framework returns 404. This is route model binding . Author routes work the same way with {author} and Author $author . The routes are protected by auth middleware, so guests are redirected to login. Do not judge the result by visiting /books just yet: the new controller expects catalogue Blade views that you will create in 04-02 and 04-03. You may see a missing-view or undefined-variable error until then. For now the check is route:list .       The two controllers exist, and route:list shows books.index, books.show, authors.index and authors.show once each. If a temporary /books route remained after Breeze, you have removed only that duplicate. Browser pages will follow in 04-02 and 04-03.       If route:list reports “Target class ... does not exist”, check the two controller filenames and imports. If it shows a duplicate GET /books route, inspect web.php for the old closure route. If a browser request reports a missing Blade view, that is expected until 04-02 and 04-03.      Add the book and author web addresses, then connect them to the controllers that will handle each page.    The old closure route must be replaced with the controller resource routes, not duplicated. route:list verifies they have been registered.  ","label":"M04-S01"},{"title":"Build the book index and detail pages","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m04-s02","excerpt":"Create the templates for a list of books and an individual book, then open both in your browser.","body":"What you will create Goal: create four Blade files: a book card, the book listing, a short review row and the book detail page. You will write the files in that order, then open /books and a particular book in your browser. In your editor, paths start with src/ . Inside the Laravel workspace container, the equivalent path starts with resources/ .      Before you start At this point you should have the books.index and books.show routes from 04-01, some books in the database from 03-03, and a user account for signing in. Keep Docker running. If you need to check routes again, use the command below.       php artisan route:list --path=books Terminal · workspace container     php artisan route:list --path=books  The two route names should be books.index and books.show . Opening them before the views exist may display an error; that's what this activity fixes. If you have no book records, check the books table in phpMyAdmin and revisit 03-03 before continuing.      1. Add the placeholder image The catalogue uses a default cover whenever a book has no image. Download the book placeholder image supplied with this learning site. In your Laravel project, create the folder src/public/images/ if it doesn't exist, and save the file as src/public/images/book-placeholder.png . In your browser, open http://localhost:8080/images/book-placeholder.png . You should see the placeholder image on its own. A 404 here means the file is not in the right public/images folder.      2. Create the book card In src/resources/views/ , create the folder books/partials/ . Then create a new file named card.blade.php inside it. This is a partial: a reusable piece of markup that can display one book wherever it is included. Use the complete file below; it expects a variable named $book . BookController@index has already loaded the authors, review count and average rating used here.       {{ $book->title }} {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n            · {{ $book->year }} @if ($book->reviews_count > 0)\n                {{ number_format($book->reviews_avg_rating, 1) }} / 5\n                ({{ $book->reviews_count }} {{ Str::plural('review', $book->reviews_count) }})\n            @else\n                No reviews yet\n            @endif src/resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php     <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg flex flex-col\">\n    <img src=\"{{ $book->image ? Storage::url($book->image) : asset('images/book-placeholder.png') }}\"\n         alt=\"Cover of {{ $book->title }}\"\n         class=\"w-full h-48 object-contain\">\n\n    <div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col grow\">\n        <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-lg text-gray-900\">\n            <a href=\"{{ route('books.show', $book) }}\" class=\"hover:underline\">\n                {{ $book->title }}\n            </a>\n        </h3>\n\n        <p class=\"text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1\">\n            {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n            &middot; {{ $book->year }}\n        </p>\n\n        <p class=\"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-auto pt-2\">\n            @if ($book->reviews_count > 0)\n                {{ number_format($book->reviews_avg_rating, 1) }} / 5\n                ({{ $book->reviews_count }} {{ Str::plural('review', $book->reviews_count) }})\n            @else\n                No reviews yet\n            @endif\n        </p>\n    </div>\n</div> 3. Create the book listing Now create (or open) src/resources/views/books/index.blade.php . If an earlier version exists from Module 01, replace its contents with this listing. The filename corresponds to the controller's view('books.index', ...) call. @forelse repeats the card for each book, while @empty handles an empty database. $books->links() adds pagination. The shared <x-app-layout> component comes from the Breeze installation.       {{ __('Books') }} @forelse ($books as $book)\n                    @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                @empty No books in the catalogue yet. @endforelse {{ $books->links() }} src/resources/views/books/index.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('Books') }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6\">\n                @forelse ($books as $book)\n                    @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                @empty\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 col-span-full\">No books in the catalogue yet.</p>\n                @endforelse\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"mt-6\">\n                {{ $books->links() }}\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> Check the listing in your browser Browser checkpoint 1: Sign in at http://localhost:8080/login , then visit http://localhost:8080/books . You should see a Books heading and cards showing titles, years and review counts. The placeholder cover should appear on each card. You can click a title now to see whether the URL changes to /books/1 (the number depends on the record). The detail page may still report View [books.show] not found until you complete the next two files.       At http://localhost:8080/books, signed-in users see the book cards. The title links point to /books/{id}. If no cards appear, check your seeded books in phpMyAdmin.      4. Create the review row Next create the folder src/resources/views/reviews/partials/ and the file review.blade.php inside it. The book detail page will include one of these rows for every review. For now it only displays the review; edit and delete links are added when you implement those routes later.       {{ $review->user->name }} {{ $review->rating }} / 5 @if ($review->comment) {{ $review->comment }} @endif {{ $review->created_at->format('d M Y') }} src/resources/views/reviews/partials/review.blade.php     <div class=\"border-b border-gray-200 py-4 last:border-b-0\">\n    <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n        <span class=\"font-medium text-gray-900\">{{ $review->user->name }}</span>\n        <span class=\"text-sm text-gray-500\">{{ $review->rating }} / 5</span>\n    </div>\n\n    @if ($review->comment)\n        <p class=\"text-gray-700 mt-1\">{{ $review->comment }}</p>\n    @endif\n\n    <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1\">\n        {{ $review->created_at->format('d M Y') }}\n    </p>\n</div> 5. Create the book detail page Create src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php . This is the page returned by BookController@show when a signed-in user opens a URL such as /books/1 . It displays $book , its authors relationship and its reviews relationship. The controller already loads these. For now we show author names as text; in 04-03 you'll make them clickable once the author pages exist.       {{ $book->title }} {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n                        · {{ $book->year }} @if ($book->description) {{ $book->description }} @endif @if ($book->isbn) ISBN {{ $book->isbn }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->publisher) Publisher {{ $book->publisher }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->edition_number) Edition {{ $book->edition_number }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->price) Price €{{ number_format($book->price, 2) }} @endif Reviews @forelse ($book->reviews as $review)\n                        @include('reviews.partials.review', ['review' => $review])\n                    @empty No reviews yet. @endforelse src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ $book->title }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-5xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-6\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6\">\n                <img src=\"{{ $book->image ? Storage::url($book->image) : asset('images/book-placeholder.png') }}\"\n                     alt=\"Cover of {{ $book->title }}\"\n                     class=\"w-full sm:w-48 h-48 object-contain rounded-md\">\n\n                <div class=\"flex-1\">\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-700\">\n                        {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n                        &middot; {{ $book->year }}\n                    </p>\n\n                    @if ($book->description)\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-800 mt-4\">{{ $book->description }}</p>\n                    @endif\n\n                    <dl class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-sm text-gray-600 mt-4\">\n                        @if ($book->isbn)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">ISBN</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->isbn }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->publisher)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Publisher</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->publisher }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->edition_number)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Edition</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->edition_number }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->price)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Price</dt>\n                            <dd>&euro;{{ number_format($book->price, 2) }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                    </dl>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-lg text-gray-900\">Reviews</h3>\n                <div class=\"mt-2\">\n                    @forelse ($book->reviews as $review)\n                        @include('reviews.partials.review', ['review' => $review])\n                    @empty\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-600\">No reviews yet.</p>\n                    @endforelse\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> Test the detail page in your browser Browser checkpoint 2: Return to /books , click any title and inspect the detail page. You should see that book's title, author names, year, description (if present) and its reviews. If no reviews exist for that book, the page should say No reviews yet . The detail page is intentionally read-only at this stage. Do not add buttons for books.create , books.edit , books.destroy or books.reviews.create yet: those routes will be built in later modules.       Both /books and a book detail URL render successfully when signed in. Book cards show titles; a detail page shows book information and existing reviews. No link on these two pages depends on a not-yet-created create/edit/review route.       If “View [books.index] not found” or “View [books.show] not found” appears, confirm the exact .blade.php name under src/resources/views/books/. If “View [books.partials.card] not found” appears, create src/resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php. If reviews are listed but fail to render, verify src/resources/views/reviews/partials/review.blade.php. For any remaining stale view issue, run php artisan view:clear in the container.      Create the templates for a list of books and an individual book, then open both in your browser.    It avoids duplicating the same book presentation in multiple views.  ","label":"M04-S02"},{"title":"Browse authors and handle missing covers","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m04-s03","excerpt":"Create the author pages and make it possible to move between books and their authors.","body":"What you will create Goal: create three more Blade files: an author card, an author listing and an author detail page. You'll then test both author routes and connect author names on the book detail page. You already have AuthorController and the two author routes from 04-01. The book card created in 04-02 is reused here.      1. Add the author placeholder Download the author placeholder image and save it as src/public/images/author-placeholder.png , alongside the book placeholder from 04-02. Check it by opening http://localhost:8080/images/author-placeholder.png . This should show an image, not a 404. Both images are used because seeded authors and books have no uploaded photos yet.      2. Create the author card In src/resources/views/ , create authors/partials/ . Inside it create card.blade.php . It expects a single $author and displays their name and the book count loaded by AuthorController@index .       {{ $author->name }} {{ $author->books_count }} {{ Str::plural('book', $author->books_count) }} src/resources/views/authors/partials/card.blade.php     <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-4 flex gap-4\">\n    <img src=\"{{ $author->image ?? asset('images/author-placeholder.png') }}\"\n         alt=\"{{ $author->name }}\"\n         class=\"w-16 h-16 rounded-full object-contain shrink-0\">\n\n    <div>\n        <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-lg text-gray-900\">\n            <a href=\"{{ route('authors.show', $author) }}\" class=\"hover:underline\">\n                {{ $author->name }}\n            </a>\n        </h3>\n\n        <p class=\"text-sm text-gray-500\">\n            {{ $author->books_count }} {{ Str::plural('book', $author->books_count) }}\n        </p>\n    </div>\n</div> 3. Create the author listing Create src/resources/views/authors/index.blade.php . This is the view named by view('authors.index', compact('authors')) in your controller. Notice the @include('authors.partials.card', ...) , which uses the file you just made.       {{ __('Authors') }} @forelse ($authors as $author)\n                    @include('authors.partials.card', ['author' => $author])\n                @empty No authors in the catalogue yet. @endforelse {{ $authors->links() }} src/resources/views/authors/index.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('Authors') }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6\">\n                @forelse ($authors as $author)\n                    @include('authors.partials.card', ['author' => $author])\n                @empty\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 col-span-full\">No authors in the catalogue yet.</p>\n                @endforelse\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"mt-6\">\n                {{ $authors->links() }}\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> Check the author listing Browser checkpoint 1: While signed in, open http://localhost:8080/authors . You should see the heading Authors , author names, book counts and the author placeholder. Clicking a name should take you to a URL like /authors/2 . That detail URL will work after you add the next file.       The /authors page renders, lists the seeded authors and links to /authors/{id}.      4. Create the author detail page Create src/resources/views/authors/show.blade.php . The controller sends it one $author with an already-loaded collection of books. This view uses the book card partial from 04-02 , so you do not need to create another book-card component.       {{ $author->name }} @if ($author->bio) {{ $author->bio }} @endif {{ __('Books') }} @forelse ($author->books as $book)\n                        @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                    @empty No books by this author yet. @endforelse src/resources/views/authors/show.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ $author->name }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-5xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-6\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6\">\n                <img src=\"{{ $author->image ?? asset('images/author-placeholder.png') }}\"\n                     alt=\"{{ $author->name }}\"\n                     class=\"w-32 h-32 rounded-full object-contain shrink-0\">\n\n                @if ($author->bio)\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-800\">{{ $author->bio }}</p>\n                @endif\n            </div>\n\n            <div>\n                <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-4\">{{ __('Books') }}</h3>\n\n                <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6\">\n                    @forelse ($author->books as $book)\n                        @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                    @empty\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-600 col-span-full\">No books by this author yet.</p>\n                    @endforelse\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> Check an author detail page Browser checkpoint 2: Go back to /authors and click an author's name. You should see their name, image, biography (if populated) and the books associated with that author. Click a book title. You should return to the book detail page created in 04-02. Some authors may have no books; if so, the page will display No books by this author yet instead.      5. Connect books and authors Now that the author pages exist, return to src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php . Find the paragraph that prints $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') and the publication year. Replace just that paragraph with the code below. Each author's name now links to their author page.       @forelse ($book->authors as $author) {{ $author->name }} {{ !$loop->last ? ', ' : '' }}\n    @empty\n        Unknown author\n    @endforelse\n    · {{ $book->year }} src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php · replacement paragraph     <p class=\"text-gray-700\">\n    @forelse ($book->authors as $author)\n        <a href=\"{{ route('authors.show', $author) }}\" class=\"text-blue-600 hover:underline\">\n            {{ $author->name }}\n        </a>{{ !$loop->last ? ', ' : '' }}\n    @empty\n        Unknown author\n    @endforelse\n    &middot; {{ $book->year }}\n</p> Follow the complete browsing path Final browser check: Open /books , select a book with an author, click that author, then click the same book on the author's page. The links should take you /books → /books/{id} → /authors/{id} → /books/{id} . There is no need to edit the navigation layout or add create/edit buttons yet. The next activity will automate some of the checks you have just made by hand.      6. Add Books and Authors to the navigation You can open /books and /authors by typing their addresses, but the site still needs menu links so visitors can find them. Breeze starts with a Dashboard link. Now you will add Books and Authors beside it. Open src/resources/views/layouts/navigation.blade.php . This layout has two sets of navigation links: one for wider screens and another for smaller screens. You need to update both. Keep the existing Dashboard link and all other layout code; you are only inserting the new links.      Add the desktop links Find the <!-- Navigation Links --> comment near the top of the file. Immediately below it is a <div class=\"hidden space-x-8 ...\"> with the Dashboard <x-nav-link> . Directly after the closing </x-nav-link> for Dashboard , paste the two links below. Do not add a second navigation <div> .       {{ __('Books') }} {{ __('Authors') }} src/resources/views/layouts/navigation.blade.php · add after desktop Dashboard link     <x-nav-link :href=\"route('books.index')\" :active=\"request()->routeIs('books.*')\">\n    {{ __('Books') }}\n</x-nav-link>\n<x-nav-link :href=\"route('authors.index')\" :active=\"request()->routeIs('authors.*')\">\n    {{ __('Authors') }}\n</x-nav-link> Add the mobile links Further down, find <!-- Responsive Navigation Menu --> . Inside the <div class=\"pt-2 pb-3 space-y-1\"> you will see a Dashboard <x-responsive-nav-link> . Directly after that Dashboard link , paste the following. The mobile component has a different name, so use this version rather than copying the desktop code.       {{ __('Books') }} {{ __('Authors') }} src/resources/views/layouts/navigation.blade.php · add after mobile Dashboard link     <x-responsive-nav-link :href=\"route('books.index')\" :active=\"request()->routeIs('books.*')\">\n    {{ __('Books') }}\n</x-responsive-nav-link>\n<x-responsive-nav-link :href=\"route('authors.index')\" :active=\"request()->routeIs('authors.*')\">\n    {{ __('Authors') }}\n</x-responsive-nav-link> Check the menu in your browser Save navigation.blade.php and reload http://localhost:8080/books while signed in. On a wide browser window, Dashboard, Books and Authors should appear in the top menu. Click Authors, then Books. The correct page should open each time, and its menu link should appear selected. Next, narrow the browser window until the navigation switches to a menu button (or use your browser’s mobile-device view). Open the menu and check that Books and Authors appear there too. If the links are visible in only one layout, check that you updated both sections of the file. In Module 06, you will return to this same file to add My Reviews alongside these links.       You have seven view files: books/index.blade.php, books/show.blade.php, books/partials/card.blade.php, reviews/partials/review.blade.php, authors/index.blade.php, authors/show.blade.php and authors/partials/card.blade.php. Signed-in users can follow links between books and authors without missing-view or missing-route errors. The top navigation also has working Books and Authors links on both desktop and mobile layouts.       If a page fails, note its URL and the exact error. Check the filename and folder under resources/views, the Blade @include name and the route name. Use php artisan route:list to check routes and php artisan view:clear after correcting a view. If you are sent to /login, sign in before repeating the check. If Books or Authors is missing from the top navigation, check both the and groups in layouts/navigation.blade.php.      Create the author pages and make it possible to move between books and their authors.    Book has a plural belongsToMany authors relationship, not a singular author relationship.  ","label":"M04-S03"},{"title":"Test the browsing experience","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m04-s04","excerpt":"Write tests that check whether your book and author pages work as expected.","body":"What you will test You've already checked the book and author pages in the browser. Now you'll save some of those checks as automated tests, so Laravel can repeat them after future changes. You will create tests/Feature/BookTest.php , tests/Feature/AuthorTest.php and tests/Unit/DatabaseSeederTest.php . Before running database tests: use a separately configured test database or a disposable development database. RefreshDatabase may run migrations against the database configured for tests; do not run this against data you need to keep. Review the testing setup from 02-04.      1. Repeat the browser checks First make sure the browser milestones work. Sign in and check /books , a book detail page, /authors , and an author detail page. Then sign out and revisit /books : you should be redirected to the login page because these routes use the auth middleware.       Signed-in visitors can browse /books and /authors and follow detail links. Signed-out visitors are redirected to /login.      2. Generate the feature test files In the Laravel workspace terminal, generate two feature test files. Do not add --unit here: these tests will make HTTP requests. Laravel creates them inside tests/Feature/ .       php artisan make:test BookTest\nphp artisan make:test AuthorTest Terminal · workspace container     php artisan make:test BookTest\nphp artisan make:test AuthorTest  Note that tests/Unit/BookTest.php and tests/Unit/AuthorTest.php already exist from Module 02. These are different files in tests/Feature/ . Open the two new files and replace the generated contents with the complete versions below.       get(route('books.index'));\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('login', absolute: false));\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_lists_books(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index'));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_show_page_displays_a_books_authors_and_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        $book->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']));\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $book->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'comment' => 'A genuine classic.',\n        ]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.show', $book));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Frank Herbert');\n        $response->assertSee('A genuine classic.');\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_nonexistent_book_returns_a_404(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get('/books/999');\n        $response->assertNotFound();\n    }\n} src/tests/Feature/BookTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Feature;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass BookTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_guests_are_redirected_to_login(): void\n    {\n        $response = $this->get(route('books.index'));\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('login', absolute: false));\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_lists_books(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index'));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_show_page_displays_a_books_authors_and_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        $book->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']));\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $book->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'comment' => 'A genuine classic.',\n        ]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.show', $book));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Frank Herbert');\n        $response->assertSee('A genuine classic.');\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_nonexistent_book_returns_a_404(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get('/books/999');\n        $response->assertNotFound();\n    }\n}  $this->get() sends a test HTTP request. actingAs($user) makes that request as a signed-in user. assertSee() checks what the Blade template actually rendered, rather than only testing the database.       get(route('authors.index'));\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('login', absolute: false));\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_lists_authors(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.index'));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Frank Herbert');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_show_page_displays_an_authors_books(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $author = Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']);\n        $author->books()->attach(Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']));\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.show', $author));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_nonexistent_author_returns_a_404(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get('/authors/999');\n        $response->assertNotFound();\n    }\n} src/tests/Feature/AuthorTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Feature;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass AuthorTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_guests_are_redirected_to_login(): void\n    {\n        $response = $this->get(route('authors.index'));\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('login', absolute: false));\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_lists_authors(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.index'));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Frank Herbert');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_show_page_displays_an_authors_books(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $author = Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']);\n        $author->books()->attach(Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']));\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.show', $author));\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_nonexistent_author_returns_a_404(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get('/authors/999');\n        $response->assertNotFound();\n    }\n}  These tests check that guests are redirected, that listing and detail pages display real data, and that a nonexistent book or author returns a 404. They do not depend on the seeded catalogue in your browser; the factories create temporary test records instead.      3. Test the sample data seeder Now generate one unit test for the seeder. The --unit flag selects the tests/Unit/ folder. Replace the generated PHP file with the complete class below.       php artisan make:test DatabaseSeederTest --unit Terminal · workspace container     php artisan make:test DatabaseSeederTest --unit  seed();\n\n        $this->assertSame(5, User::count());\n        $this->assertSame(5, Author::count());\n        $this->assertSame(20, Book::count());\n        $this->assertSame(60, Review::count());\n    }\n\n    public function test_seeding_produces_two_co_written_books(): void\n    {\n        $this->seed();\n\n        $this->assertSame(2, Book::has('authors', '>=', 2)->count());\n    }\n\n    public function test_seeding_leaves_two_books_without_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $this->seed();\n\n        $this->assertSame(2, Book::doesntHave('reviews')->count());\n    }\n} src/tests/Unit/DatabaseSeederTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Unit;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass DatabaseSeederTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_seeding_produces_the_expected_catalogue_shape(): void\n    {\n        $this->seed();\n\n        $this->assertSame(5, User::count());\n        $this->assertSame(5, Author::count());\n        $this->assertSame(20, Book::count());\n        $this->assertSame(60, Review::count());\n    }\n\n    public function test_seeding_produces_two_co_written_books(): void\n    {\n        $this->seed();\n\n        $this->assertSame(2, Book::has('authors', '>=', 2)->count());\n    }\n\n    public function test_seeding_leaves_two_books_without_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $this->seed();\n\n        $this->assertSame(2, Book::doesntHave('reviews')->count());\n    }\n}  $this->seed() runs your seeder inside the test. The assertions verify the sample catalogue's size and a couple of its intended relationships. They are based on the seeded data built in Module 03; if you intentionally changed those counts, adjust the test to reflect your own agreed data.      4. Run the tests Run each new file individually so failures are easier to locate. Then run the full suite. From the workspace container, where artisan is located:       php artisan test tests/Feature/BookTest.php\nphp artisan test tests/Feature/AuthorTest.php\nphp artisan test tests/Unit/DatabaseSeederTest.php\nphp artisan test Terminal · workspace container     php artisan test tests/Feature/BookTest.php\nphp artisan test tests/Feature/AuthorTest.php\nphp artisan test tests/Unit/DatabaseSeederTest.php\nphp artisan test What passing tests mean If a test reports View [books.partials.card] not found , return to 04-02 and check that exact file. If a test reports Route [authors.show] not defined , check 04-01. If the tests pass but the actual browser doesn't, verify Docker is running, the same project's src/ is being served and you are signed in. What you have completed: students can view books and authors, and you have feature tests covering both listing and detail routes. Creating/editing books and reviews are still future activities.       The BookTest and AuthorTest feature files run successfully, and you can still open /books, /books/{id}, /authors and /authors/{id} in the browser. The DatabaseSeederTest confirms the expected sample-data structure.       If a page fails, note its URL and the exact error. Check the filename and folder under resources/views, the Blade @include name and the route name. Use php artisan route:list to check routes and php artisan view:clear after correcting a view. If you are sent to /login, sign in before repeating the check.      Write tests that check whether your book and author pages work as expected.    They exercise the HTTP response generated by the whole route-to-view path.  ","label":"M04-S04"},{"title":"Add your first book through a form","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m05-s01","excerpt":"Create an Add Book page, connect it to the database and check that the new book appears in the catalogue.","body":"Before you begin You should have finished Module 04: /books displays the catalogue, clicking a book opens its detail page, and you can log in with your test account. Keep your Docker containers running. Work in your Laravel project, not the learning-site files. All file paths below start from your project folder on your computer ( src/… ). Run Artisan commands in the workspace terminal, as before.      1. Add two routes to your existing list Open src/routes/web.php . Inside the existing Route::middleware('auth')->group(...) , find the books Route::resource line you created in 04-01. Replace that one line with the version below. Keep the author routes and everything else in the file. create gives you a new form page at GET /books/create ; store handles the form when it is submitted to POST /books .       Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show', 'create', 'store']); src/routes/web.php · replace the existing books resource line     Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show', 'create', 'store']); Check the new routes Run this in the Laravel workspace terminal. Look for the route names books.create and books.store , as well as the existing books.index and books.show .       php artisan route:list --path=books Terminal · workspace container     php artisan route:list --path=books 2. Add the create and store actions Open src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php and replace its contents with the complete class below. Your existing index() and show() methods are retained; two new methods are added. create() shows the form. store() checks the title and year, saves a new Book and redirects to that book’s detail page. We will expand the form and validation in 05-02.       withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Book $book)\n    {\n        $book->load(['authors', 'reviews.user']);\n\n        return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n    }\n    public function create()\n    {\n        return view('books.create');\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate([\n            'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n            'year' => 'required|integer|digits:4',\n        ]);\n\n        $book = Book::create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n} src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · complete file     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\n\nclass BookController extends Controller\n{\n    public function index()\n    {\n        $books = Book::with('authors')\n            ->withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Book $book)\n    {\n        $book->load(['authors', 'reviews.user']);\n\n        return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n    }\n    public function create()\n    {\n        return view('books.create');\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate([\n            'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n            'year' => 'required|integer|digits:4',\n        ]);\n\n        $book = Book::create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n} 3. Create the Blade template for the form In your editor, create src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php . The books folder already exists from Module 04. Copy in the complete file below. This first version has only two fields, title and year. @csrf is required for the form’s POST request; Breeze’s x-input-… components provide the same styling as its login and registration pages.       Add a Book @csrf Add Book src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php · new file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">Add a Book</h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"title\" value=\"Title\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"title\" name=\"title\" type=\"text\"\n                                      class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" required autofocus />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"year\" value=\"Year\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"year\" name=\"year\" type=\"number\"\n                                      class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" required />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <x-primary-button>Add Book</x-primary-button>\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 4. Add an Add Book link to the catalogue Open src/resources/views/books/index.blade.php , which you made in 04-02. Replace its contents with the updated file below. The book grid is the same; only the heading gains an Add Book link.       {{ __('Books') }} {{ __('Add Book') }} @forelse ($books as $book)\n                    @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                @empty No books in the catalogue yet. @endforelse {{ $books->links() }} src/resources/views/books/index.blade.php · complete updated file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n            <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n                {{ __('Books') }}\n            </h2>\n            <a href=\"{{ route('books.create') }}\"\n               class=\"inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 bg-gray-800 rounded-md text-xs font-semibold uppercase text-white hover:bg-gray-700\">\n                {{ __('Add Book') }}\n            </a>\n        </div>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6\">\n                @forelse ($books as $book)\n                    @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                @empty\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 col-span-full\">No books in the catalogue yet.</p>\n                @endforelse\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"mt-6\">\n                {{ $books->links() }}\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 5. Check the form in your browser Make sure you are signed in. Go to http://localhost:8080/books and click Add Book , or go directly to http://localhost:8080/books/create . You should see a page headed Add a Book , with Title, Year and Add Book. Enter AAA Practice Book for the title and 2024 for the year; submit. You should be taken to the new book’s detail page. Return to /books and find AAA Practice Book . Optional database check: in phpMyAdmin, open the books table and find the new record. The book will have no linked author yet because this form doesn’t assign authors. That is expected.       The Add Book link opens /books/create . Submitting a title and year creates a record and redirects to the new book’s detail page.       Getting a 404? Check php artisan route:list --path=books . Getting “View [books.create] not found”? Check the exact filename src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php . Getting “Class … Request not found”? Check the use Illuminate\\Http\\Request; import at the top of the controller. If redirected to login, sign in with the account created in Module 03.      Create an Add Book page, connect it to the database and check that the new book appears in the catalogue.    Laravel validates the CSRF token to protect state-changing browser requests.  ","label":"M05-S01"},{"title":"Check form entries and show useful errors","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m05-s02","excerpt":"Add the remaining book fields and show helpful messages without clearing what the user has typed.","body":"Start with the working form from 05-01 At the end of the previous activity, you could add a book using its title and year. We will now add the optional details (description, ISBN, publisher, edition and price) and improve what happens when a value is invalid. In this activity you change two existing files . There are no new routes, controllers or Blade files.      1. Update the store() method Open src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php and find the store(Request $request) method from 05-01. Replace only that method with the code below. Leave index() , show() and create() as they are. The new rules allow optional details to be blank ( nullable ), require a four-digit year and reject a negative price. $validated contains only fields that pass these rules; it is then used to create the book.       public function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $validated = $request->validate([\n        'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n        'description' => 'nullable|string',\n        'year' => 'required|integer|digits:4',\n        'isbn' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n        'publisher' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n        'edition_number' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n        'price' => 'nullable|numeric|min:0',\n    ]);\n\n    $book = Book::create($validated);\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n} src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · replace store() method     public function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $validated = $request->validate([\n        'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n        'description' => 'nullable|string',\n        'year' => 'required|integer|digits:4',\n        'isbn' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n        'publisher' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n        'edition_number' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n        'price' => 'nullable|numeric|min:0',\n    ]);\n\n    $book = Book::create($validated);\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n} What happens when validation fails? Laravel automatically sends the browser back to the form with an error message and the values already entered. You do not need to write a separate redirect. The next step displays these messages and values on the form.      2. Replace the Add Book template Open src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php and replace the entire file with this version. It adds the remaining fields and, below each field, an x-input-error component. It also uses old('field') so previously submitted values remain in the form when validation fails.       Add a Book @csrf {{ old('description') }} Add Book src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php · complete updated file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">Add a Book</h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"title\" value=\"Title\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"title\" name=\"title\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\"\n                                      value=\"{{ old('title') }}\" required autofocus />\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('title')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"description\" value=\"Description\" />\n                        <textarea id=\"description\" name=\"description\" rows=\"4\"\n                                  class=\"mt-1 block w-full border-gray-300 rounded-md shadow-sm\">{{ old('description') }}</textarea>\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('description')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"year\" value=\"Year\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"year\" name=\"year\" type=\"number\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\"\n                                      value=\"{{ old('year') }}\" required />\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('year')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"isbn\" value=\"ISBN\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"isbn\" name=\"isbn\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\"\n                                      value=\"{{ old('isbn') }}\" />\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('isbn')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"publisher\" value=\"Publisher\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"publisher\" name=\"publisher\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\"\n                                      value=\"{{ old('publisher') }}\" />\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('publisher')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"edition_number\" value=\"Edition\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"edition_number\" name=\"edition_number\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\"\n                                      value=\"{{ old('edition_number') }}\" />\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('edition_number')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"price\" value=\"Price\" />\n                        <x-text-input id=\"price\" name=\"price\" type=\"number\" step=\"0.01\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\"\n                                      value=\"{{ old('price') }}\" />\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('price')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <x-primary-button>Add Book</x-primary-button>\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 3. Check invalid and valid submissions Open http://localhost:8080/books/create while signed in. Enter Another Practice Book as Title, 123 as Year and 19.99 as Price. Submit. The four-digit-year rule should reject 123 . You should remain on the Add Book form, see an error beside Year, and still see the title and price you typed. Change Year to 2024 and submit again. You should see the new book detail page, including any optional details you entered. Why test the year rather than an empty title? The browser’s built-in required check may stop an empty title before Laravel receives the form. Entering a three-digit year lets you see Laravel’s own validation message.      A quick note on the rules required means a field cannot be empty. nullable means it may be left blank. digits:4 requires exactly four digits, and min:0 prevents a negative price. old() and x-input-error make errors easier to correct. We will reuse these rules when adding the Edit Book page.       An invalid three-digit year produces a clear error and preserves the other form values. Correcting the year lets the book save.       If the form submits but you see no validation message, verify that store() uses $request->validate() and that the Year field contains <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('year')\" … /> . If an SQL error appears, check that you are using $validated , not $request->all() , in Book::create() .      Add the remaining book fields and show helpful messages without clearing what the user has typed.    The previously submitted input flashed to the session after validation failure.  ","label":"M05-S02"},{"title":"Edit books and upload cover images","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m05-s03","excerpt":"Create an Edit Book page, reuse the form and let users upload a cover image.","body":"What you will make You already have a working Add Book form. Now you will create the Edit Book page, use the same form fields for adding and editing, and allow cover image uploads. We will do this by creating two new Blade files , updating the existing Add Book page and BookController, then adding an Edit link to the book detail page. Continue from your completed 05-02 work. The file paths below are in your Laravel project’s src/ folder.      1. Add the edit and update routes Open src/routes/web.php . Find the existing books Route::resource line and replace only that line with this one. Keep the surrounding auth group and the author routes. GET /books/{book}/edit will display the form. Submitting changes uses PUT /books/{book} .       Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show', 'create', 'store', 'edit', 'update']); src/routes/web.php · replace books resource line     Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show', 'create', 'store', 'edit', 'update']); 2. Create a shared form partial In src/resources/views/books/partials/ (the folder already contains card.blade.php ), create form.blade.php . This is a reusable form body: both the Add Book and Edit Book pages will include it. Copy the complete partial below. Its fields use old('title', $book->title ?? '') : on the Edit page that shows the existing book value, while on Add Book the field starts blank. Following a validation error, the value just typed takes priority.       {{-- Shared between create.blade.php and edit.blade.php. $book is only set\n     when editing - old('field', $book->field ?? '') falls back to the\n     book's current value there, or to an empty string on create, and to\n     whatever was just typed in on either page if validation just failed. --}} {{ old('description', $book->description ?? '') }} @isset($book)\n        @if ($book->image) @endif\n    @endisset {{ isset($book) ? __('Save Changes') : __('Add Book') }} src/resources/views/books/partials/form.blade.php · new file     {{-- Shared between create.blade.php and edit.blade.php. $book is only set\n     when editing - old('field', $book->field ?? '') falls back to the\n     book's current value there, or to an empty string on create, and to\n     whatever was just typed in on either page if validation just failed. --}}\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"title\" :value=\"__('Title')\" />\n    <x-text-input id=\"title\" name=\"title\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" value=\"{{ old('title', $book->title ?? '') }}\" required autofocus />\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('title')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"description\" :value=\"__('Description')\" />\n    <textarea id=\"description\" name=\"description\" rows=\"4\"\n              class=\"mt-1 block w-full border-gray-300 focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 rounded-md shadow-sm\">{{ old('description', $book->description ?? '') }}</textarea>\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('description')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"year\" :value=\"__('Year')\" />\n    <x-text-input id=\"year\" name=\"year\" type=\"number\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" value=\"{{ old('year', $book->year ?? '') }}\" required />\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('year')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"isbn\" :value=\"__('ISBN')\" />\n    <x-text-input id=\"isbn\" name=\"isbn\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" value=\"{{ old('isbn', $book->isbn ?? '') }}\" />\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('isbn')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"publisher\" :value=\"__('Publisher')\" />\n    <x-text-input id=\"publisher\" name=\"publisher\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" value=\"{{ old('publisher', $book->publisher ?? '') }}\" />\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('publisher')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"edition_number\" :value=\"__('Edition')\" />\n    <x-text-input id=\"edition_number\" name=\"edition_number\" type=\"text\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" value=\"{{ old('edition_number', $book->edition_number ?? '') }}\" />\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('edition_number')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"price\" :value=\"__('Price')\" />\n    <x-text-input id=\"price\" name=\"price\" type=\"number\" step=\"0.01\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full\" value=\"{{ old('price', $book->price ?? '') }}\" />\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('price')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"image\" :value=\"__('Cover image')\" />\n\n    @isset($book)\n        @if ($book->image)\n            <img src=\"{{ Storage::url($book->image) }}\" alt=\"Current cover of {{ $book->title }}\" class=\"mt-2 h-32 object-contain\">\n        @endif\n    @endisset\n\n    <input id=\"image\" name=\"image\" type=\"file\" accept=\"image/*\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full text-sm text-gray-700\">\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('image')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"flex items-center gap-4\">\n    <x-primary-button>{{ isset($book) ? __('Save Changes') : __('Add Book') }}</x-primary-button>\n</div> 3. Update the existing Add Book page Open src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php and replace the whole file with the code below. The fields you wrote in 05-02 now live in books.partials.form , so the page itself is much shorter. enctype=\"multipart/form-data\" is essential when a form includes a file upload.       {{ __('Add a Book') }} @csrf\n\n                    @include('books.partials.form') src/resources/views/books/create.blade.php · complete updated file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('Add a Book') }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\" enctype=\"multipart/form-data\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n\n                    @include('books.partials.form')\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 4. Create the Edit Book page In the same books folder, create src/resources/views/books/edit.blade.php . Copy the file below. It uses the same form partial but sends its data to books.update and includes @method('PUT') so Laravel knows the action is an update rather than a new record.       {{ __('Edit') }} {{ $book->title }} @csrf\n                    @method('PUT')\n\n                    @include('books.partials.form', ['book' => $book]) src/resources/views/books/edit.blade.php · new file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('Edit') }} {{ $book->title }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.update', $book) }}\" enctype=\"multipart/form-data\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n                    @method('PUT')\n\n                    @include('books.partials.form', ['book' => $book])\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 5. Update BookController Open src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php and replace the entire file with this version. The index() and show() methods from Module 04 are still here. The controller now includes edit() , update() , image upload handling in store() and update() , and one validationRules() method shared by both actions. The two new imports are Illuminate\\Http\\Request and Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Storage . Each upload is placed in the books/ folder on Laravel’s public disk, and only its saved path goes into the books.image database column.       withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Book $book)\n    {\n        $book->load(['authors', 'reviews.user']);\n\n        return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n    }\n\n    public function create()\n    {\n        return view('books.create');\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate($this->validationRules());\n\n        if ($request->hasFile('image')) {\n            $validated['image'] = Storage::disk('public')->putFile('books', $request->file('image'));\n        }\n\n        $book = Book::create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    public function edit(Book $book)\n    {\n        return view('books.edit', compact('book'));\n    }\n\n    public function update(Request $request, Book $book)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate($this->validationRules());\n\n        if ($request->hasFile('image')) {\n            $validated['image'] = Storage::disk('public')->putFile('books', $request->file('image'));\n        }\n\n        $book->update($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    private function validationRules(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n            'description' => 'nullable|string',\n            'year' => 'required|integer|digits:4',\n            'isbn' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n            'publisher' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n            'edition_number' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n            'price' => 'nullable|numeric|min:0',\n            'image' => 'nullable|image|max:2048',\n        ];\n    }\n} src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · complete updated file     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Storage;\n\nclass BookController extends Controller\n{\n    public function index()\n    {\n        $books = Book::with('authors')\n            ->withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12);\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    }\n\n    public function show(Book $book)\n    {\n        $book->load(['authors', 'reviews.user']);\n\n        return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n    }\n\n    public function create()\n    {\n        return view('books.create');\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate($this->validationRules());\n\n        if ($request->hasFile('image')) {\n            $validated['image'] = Storage::disk('public')->putFile('books', $request->file('image'));\n        }\n\n        $book = Book::create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    public function edit(Book $book)\n    {\n        return view('books.edit', compact('book'));\n    }\n\n    public function update(Request $request, Book $book)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate($this->validationRules());\n\n        if ($request->hasFile('image')) {\n            $validated['image'] = Storage::disk('public')->putFile('books', $request->file('image'));\n        }\n\n        $book->update($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    private function validationRules(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'title' => 'required|string|max:255',\n            'description' => 'nullable|string',\n            'year' => 'required|integer|digits:4',\n            'isbn' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n            'publisher' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n            'edition_number' => 'nullable|string|max:255',\n            'price' => 'nullable|numeric|min:0',\n            'image' => 'nullable|image|max:2048',\n        ];\n    }\n} 6. Make uploaded covers accessible Run the following command in the workspace container (from the Laravel application directory). It creates the public storage link if it does not already exist. You only need to do this once for the project.       php artisan storage:link Terminal · workspace container     php artisan storage:link 7. Add an Edit link to the book detail page Open src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php (created in 04-02). Replace its contents with the updated version below. It retains your existing authors, book details and reviews, but adds an Edit button beside the book title. The cover already uses Storage::url($book->image) in Module 04, so it can now display real uploaded images as well as the placeholder.       {{ $book->title }} Edit {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n                        · {{ $book->year }} @if ($book->description) {{ $book->description }} @endif @if ($book->isbn) ISBN {{ $book->isbn }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->publisher) Publisher {{ $book->publisher }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->edition_number) Edition {{ $book->edition_number }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->price) Price €{{ number_format($book->price, 2) }} @endif Reviews @forelse ($book->reviews as $review)\n                        @include('reviews.partials.review', ['review' => $review])\n                    @empty No reviews yet. @endforelse src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php · complete updated file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-3\">\n            <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n                {{ $book->title }}\n            </h2>\n            <a href=\"{{ route('books.edit', $book) }}\"\n               class=\"inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 bg-gray-800 rounded-md text-xs font-semibold uppercase text-white hover:bg-gray-700\">\n                Edit\n            </a>\n        </div>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-5xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-6\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6\">\n                <img src=\"{{ $book->image ? Storage::url($book->image) : asset('images/book-placeholder.png') }}\"\n                     alt=\"Cover of {{ $book->title }}\"\n                     class=\"w-full sm:w-48 h-48 object-contain rounded-md\">\n\n                <div class=\"flex-1\">\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-700\">\n                        {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n                        &middot; {{ $book->year }}\n                    </p>\n\n                    @if ($book->description)\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-800 mt-4\">{{ $book->description }}</p>\n                    @endif\n\n                    <dl class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-sm text-gray-600 mt-4\">\n                        @if ($book->isbn)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">ISBN</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->isbn }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->publisher)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Publisher</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->publisher }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->edition_number)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Edition</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->edition_number }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->price)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Price</dt>\n                            <dd>&euro;{{ number_format($book->price, 2) }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                    </dl>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-lg text-gray-900\">Reviews</h3>\n                <div class=\"mt-2\">\n                    @forelse ($book->reviews as $review)\n                        @include('reviews.partials.review', ['review' => $review])\n                    @empty\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-600\">No reviews yet.</p>\n                    @endforelse\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 8. Check the pages and upload in your browser Sign in and go to http://localhost:8080/books . Open an existing book, then select Edit . Its title, year and any other saved details should already be filled in. Change its title or price and click Save Changes . You should return to the updated detail page. Choose Edit again, select a small JPG or PNG (under 2 MB) as the cover image and save. You should now see the uploaded cover on the detail page, and it should also appear when you return to the catalogue. Open http://localhost:8080/books/create . Create a new book with a cover to confirm Add Book still works. You can also verify the saved image path in the books.image column in phpMyAdmin. It will look something like books/…jpg , not like a full URL.       The Edit button opens /books/{id}/edit with the current details filled in. Saving updates the book and uploaded cover images display correctly.       A “View [books.partials.form] not found” error means the shared file is missing or incorrectly named. A 404 on Edit usually means books.edit was not added to routes/web.php . If the cover uploads but displays as broken, run php artisan storage:link , confirm enctype=\"multipart/form-data\" is on the form and check the books.image value in phpMyAdmin.      Create an Edit Book page, reuse the form and let users upload a cover image.    In books.image, using the path returned by the public storage disk.  ","label":"M05-S03"},{"title":"Delete books and test your changes","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m05-s04","excerpt":"Add a Delete button, then test adding, editing and deleting books.","body":"Your final catalogue change You can now add books, edit their details and upload covers. This activity adds a Delete button and checks the complete flow using both the browser and Laravel feature tests. Take care: deleting a book removes its database record. Use one of the practice books you created, not an important record.      1. Register the delete route Open src/routes/web.php . Find the books resource line from 05-03 and replace it with the version below, which adds destroy . Leave all other routes unchanged. Unlike opening or editing a book, deleting a book sends a DELETE request to /books/{book} .       Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show', 'create', 'store', 'edit', 'update', 'destroy']); src/routes/web.php · replace books resource line     Route::resource('books', BookController::class)->only(['index', 'show', 'create', 'store', 'edit', 'update', 'destroy']); 2. Add deletion to BookController Open src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php . Find the private validationRules() method near the bottom. Insert this new public destroy() method immediately before validationRules() . Do not remove or replace the other methods. First it deletes the uploaded cover file, if there is one; then it deletes the database record. Finally it sends the user back to the book listing.       public function destroy(Book $book)\n{\n    if ($book->image) {\n        Storage::disk('public')->delete($book->image);\n    }\n\n    $book->delete();\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.index');\n} src/app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · insert method inside class     public function destroy(Book $book)\n{\n    if ($book->image) {\n        Storage::disk('public')->delete($book->image);\n    }\n\n    $book->delete();\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.index');\n} 3. Add a Delete button to the book page Open src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php and replace the entire file with the updated version below. The Delete button sits next to Edit. The form uses @csrf for security and @method('DELETE') because ordinary HTML forms cannot send a DELETE method directly. The browser confirmation asks before the request is sent.       {{ $book->title }} Edit @csrf\n                    @method('DELETE') Delete {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n                        · {{ $book->year }} @if ($book->description) {{ $book->description }} @endif @if ($book->isbn) ISBN {{ $book->isbn }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->publisher) Publisher {{ $book->publisher }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->edition_number) Edition {{ $book->edition_number }} @endif\n                        @if ($book->price) Price €{{ number_format($book->price, 2) }} @endif Reviews @forelse ($book->reviews as $review)\n                        @include('reviews.partials.review', ['review' => $review])\n                    @empty No reviews yet. @endforelse src/resources/views/books/show.blade.php · complete updated file     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-3\">\n            <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n                {{ $book->title }}\n            </h2>\n            <div class=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n                <a href=\"{{ route('books.edit', $book) }}\"\n                   class=\"inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 bg-gray-800 rounded-md text-xs font-semibold uppercase text-white hover:bg-gray-700\">\n                    Edit\n                </a>\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.destroy', $book) }}\"\n                      onsubmit=\"return confirm('Delete this book? This cannot be undone.');\">\n                    @csrf\n                    @method('DELETE')\n                    <x-danger-button>Delete</x-danger-button>\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-5xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-6\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6\">\n                <img src=\"{{ $book->image ? Storage::url($book->image) : asset('images/book-placeholder.png') }}\"\n                     alt=\"Cover of {{ $book->title }}\"\n                     class=\"w-full sm:w-48 h-48 object-contain rounded-md\">\n\n                <div class=\"flex-1\">\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-700\">\n                        {{ $book->authors->pluck('name')->join(', ') ?: 'Unknown author' }}\n                        &middot; {{ $book->year }}\n                    </p>\n\n                    @if ($book->description)\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-800 mt-4\">{{ $book->description }}</p>\n                    @endif\n\n                    <dl class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-sm text-gray-600 mt-4\">\n                        @if ($book->isbn)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">ISBN</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->isbn }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->publisher)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Publisher</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->publisher }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->edition_number)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Edition</dt>\n                            <dd>{{ $book->edition_number }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                        @if ($book->price)\n                            <dt class=\"font-medium\">Price</dt>\n                            <dd>&euro;{{ number_format($book->price, 2) }}</dd>\n                        @endif\n                    </dl>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <h3 class=\"font-semibold text-lg text-gray-900\">Reviews</h3>\n                <div class=\"mt-2\">\n                    @forelse ($book->reviews as $review)\n                        @include('reviews.partials.review', ['review' => $review])\n                    @empty\n                        <p class=\"text-gray-600\">No reviews yet.</p>\n                    @endforelse\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 4. Test deleting a practice book in your browser Sign in, visit http://localhost:8080/books and open a book you created for testing. Check that the detail page now displays both Edit and Delete . Click Delete, then Cancel in the confirmation: the book should remain. Click Delete again and confirm . You should return to the catalogue, and the book should no longer appear. Optional: look in the books table in phpMyAdmin and confirm the record is gone.      5. Extend the feature tests you created in 04-04 Open the existing src/tests/Feature/BookTest.php . Do not create another file and do not delete the read-only browsing tests you already wrote. At the top of the file, alongside the other use statements, add these two imports if they are not already present. They let us make fake uploaded images without touching real files.       use Illuminate\\Http\\UploadedFile;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Storage; src/tests/Feature/BookTest.php · add imports near top     use Illuminate\\Http\\UploadedFile;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Storage; 6. Add the new test methods Still in src/tests/Feature/BookTest.php , scroll down to the last closing brace of the BookTest class . Paste the methods below inside the class, immediately before that final brace. Keep the existing test methods intact. These tests check successful creation, invalid data, editing, deleting, deleting the stored cover and the full add–edit–delete journey. They also check that a successful create redirects to the new book’s page.       public function test_a_book_can_be_created(): void\n    {\n        Storage::fake('public');\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.store'), [\n            'title' => 'Project Hail Mary',\n            'year' => 2021,\n            'image' => UploadedFile::fake()->image('cover.jpg'),\n        ]);\n\n        $book = Book::firstWhere('title', 'Project Hail Mary');\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertNotNull($book);\n        Storage::disk('public')->assertExists($book->image);\n    }\n\n    public function test_creating_a_book_without_a_title_fails_validation_and_repopulates_the_form(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)\n            ->from(route('books.create'))\n            ->post(route('books.store'), [\n                'title' => '',\n                'year' => 2021,\n            ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.create'));\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('title');\n        $response->assertSessionHasInput('year', 2021);\n        $this->assertDatabaseMissing('books', ['year' => 2021]);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_can_be_updated(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Old Title']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => 'New Title',\n            'year' => $book->year,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertSame('New Title', $book->fresh()->title);\n    }\n\n    public function test_updating_a_book_without_a_title_fails_validation_and_leaves_it_unchanged(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => '',\n            'year' => $book->year,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('title');\n        $this->assertSame('Dune', $book->fresh()->title);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_can_be_deleted(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book));\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.index'));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($book);\n    }\n\n    public function test_deleting_a_book_removes_its_cover_image_from_storage(): void\n    {\n        Storage::fake('public');\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $path = UploadedFile::fake()->image('cover.jpg')->store('books', 'public');\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['image' => $path]);\n\n        $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book));\n\n        Storage::disk('public')->assertMissing($path);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_full_create_edit_delete_flow(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $createResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.store'), [\n            'title' => 'The Fellowship of the Ring',\n            'year' => 1954,\n        ]);\n        $book = Book::firstWhere('title', 'The Fellowship of the Ring');\n        $createResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n\n        $editResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => 'The Fellowship of the Ring (Revised)',\n            'year' => 1954,\n        ]);\n        $editResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertSame('The Fellowship of the Ring (Revised)', $book->fresh()->title);\n\n        $deleteResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book));\n        $deleteResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.index'));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($book);\n    } src/tests/Feature/BookTest.php · insert before the class’s final }         public function test_a_book_can_be_created(): void\n    {\n        Storage::fake('public');\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.store'), [\n            'title' => 'Project Hail Mary',\n            'year' => 2021,\n            'image' => UploadedFile::fake()->image('cover.jpg'),\n        ]);\n\n        $book = Book::firstWhere('title', 'Project Hail Mary');\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertNotNull($book);\n        Storage::disk('public')->assertExists($book->image);\n    }\n\n    public function test_creating_a_book_without_a_title_fails_validation_and_repopulates_the_form(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)\n            ->from(route('books.create'))\n            ->post(route('books.store'), [\n                'title' => '',\n                'year' => 2021,\n            ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.create'));\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('title');\n        $response->assertSessionHasInput('year', 2021);\n        $this->assertDatabaseMissing('books', ['year' => 2021]);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_can_be_updated(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Old Title']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => 'New Title',\n            'year' => $book->year,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertSame('New Title', $book->fresh()->title);\n    }\n\n    public function test_updating_a_book_without_a_title_fails_validation_and_leaves_it_unchanged(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => '',\n            'year' => $book->year,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('title');\n        $this->assertSame('Dune', $book->fresh()->title);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_book_can_be_deleted(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book));\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.index'));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($book);\n    }\n\n    public function test_deleting_a_book_removes_its_cover_image_from_storage(): void\n    {\n        Storage::fake('public');\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $path = UploadedFile::fake()->image('cover.jpg')->store('books', 'public');\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['image' => $path]);\n\n        $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book));\n\n        Storage::disk('public')->assertMissing($path);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_full_create_edit_delete_flow(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $createResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.store'), [\n            'title' => 'The Fellowship of the Ring',\n            'year' => 1954,\n        ]);\n        $book = Book::firstWhere('title', 'The Fellowship of the Ring');\n        $createResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n\n        $editResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => 'The Fellowship of the Ring (Revised)',\n            'year' => 1954,\n        ]);\n        $editResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertSame('The Fellowship of the Ring (Revised)', $book->fresh()->title);\n\n        $deleteResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book));\n        $deleteResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.index'));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($book);\n    } 7. Run the tests From the Laravel workspace terminal, run the Book feature tests first. If they pass, run the full suite. The test code uses RefreshDatabase , so use your existing disposable testing database configuration from Module 04; do not point tests at a database containing work you need to keep.       php artisan test tests/Feature/BookTest.php\nphp artisan test Terminal · workspace container     php artisan test tests/Feature/BookTest.php\nphp artisan test What you should see The tests should report PASS rather than a failed assertion. The exact number of passing tests depends on which earlier tests you completed. In the browser you should now be able to add a book, edit it (including a cover image), and delete it. If a test fails, read the first error carefully: a missing route points to web.php ; a missing view points to a Blade filename; an unknown Storage or UploadedFile class usually means an import is missing.       You have added the delete route and button, deleted a practice book in the browser and run the Book feature tests successfully.       If the Delete button is missing, verify the updated show.blade.php . If the route is missing, run php artisan route:list --path=books and look for books.destroy . If PHP reports “unexpected token public”, you may have inserted destroy() outside the controller class or inside another method. Review where you placed the code. If the tests fail with missing classes, check the imports at the top of BookTest.php .      Add a Delete button, then test adding, editing and deleting books.    Deleting the database row does not automatically remove the file from storage.  ","label":"M05-S04"},{"title":"Write a review for a book","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m06-s01","excerpt":"Create a review form, save a rating and comment, and check the result on the book page.","body":"Before you begin You already have books, accounts and seeded reviews. Now you will add a page where a signed-in user can write a real review. Complete each step in order.      1. Generate the controller From your Laravel project folder, generate the controller. Do not create a model or migration here; the Review model and reviews table were created in Module 02.       php artisan make:controller ReviewController Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan make:controller ReviewController 2. Register the two review routes Open routes/web.php . At the top add the ReviewController import. Inside the existing Route::middleware('auth')->group(...) , add the nested resource route shown below. Keep your book, author and profile routes.       use App\\Http\\Controllers\\ReviewController; routes/web.php · import     use App\\Http\\Controllers\\ReviewController;  Route::resource('books.reviews', ReviewController::class)->only(['create', 'store']); routes/web.php · inside the existing auth group     Route::resource('books.reviews', ReviewController::class)->only(['create', 'store']); Why the route is nested This creates GET /books/{book}/reviews/create and POST /books/{book}/reviews . The book ID in the URL tells Laravel which book is being reviewed.      3. Add the controller methods Open app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php and replace the generated class with this complete version , including all imports. The controller validates the rating, refuses a second review by the same user for the same book, and gets the user ID from the login session.       validate([\n            'rating' => 'required|integer|between:1,5',\n            'comment' => 'nullable|string',\n        ]);\n\n        if ($book->reviews()->where('user_id', auth()->id())->exists()) {\n            return back()->withErrors([\n                'rating' => 'You have already reviewed this book.',\n            ])->withInput();\n        }\n\n        $validated['user_id'] = auth()->id();\n        $book->reviews()->create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n} app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\n\nclass ReviewController extends Controller\n{\n    public function create(Book $book)\n    {\n        return view('reviews.create', compact('book'));\n    }\n\n    public function store(Request $request, Book $book)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate([\n            'rating' => 'required|integer|between:1,5',\n            'comment' => 'nullable|string',\n        ]);\n\n        if ($book->reviews()->where('user_id', auth()->id())->exists()) {\n            return back()->withErrors([\n                'rating' => 'You have already reviewed this book.',\n            ])->withInput();\n        }\n\n        $validated['user_id'] = auth()->id();\n        $book->reviews()->create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n} 4. Create the review form Create the folder resources/views/reviews if it does not exist, then create create.blade.php inside it. Paste the entire template. This is a temporary standalone form; in 06-02 you will move its fields into a reusable partial.       Review: {{ $book->title }} @csrf Select a rating @for ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++) {{ $i }} / 5 @endfor {{ old('comment') }} Submit Review resources/views/reviews/create.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            Review: {{ $book->title }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.reviews.store', $book) }}\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"rating\" :value=\"__('Rating')\" />\n                        <select id=\"rating\" name=\"rating\" required class=\"mt-1 block w-full border-gray-300 rounded-md\">\n                            <option value=\"\">Select a rating</option>\n                            @for ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++)\n                                <option value=\"{{ $i }}\" @selected(old('rating') == $i)>{{ $i }} / 5</option>\n                            @endfor\n                        </select>\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('rating')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <div>\n                        <x-input-label for=\"comment\" :value=\"__('Comment (optional)')\" />\n                        <textarea id=\"comment\" name=\"comment\" rows=\"4\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full border-gray-300 rounded-md\">{{ old('comment') }}</textarea>\n                        <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('comment')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n                    </div>\n\n                    <x-primary-button>Submit Review</x-primary-button>\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 5. Add a link on each book page Open resources/views/books/show.blade.php . Find the section containing the Reviews heading. Add this link beside the heading, or just underneath it. Do not replace the rest of the book-detail page.       Write a Review resources/views/books/show.blade.php · inside the Reviews section     <a href=\"{{ route('books.reviews.create', $book) }}\" class=\"text-blue-600 hover:underline\">Write a Review</a>  php artisan route:list --path=books Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan route:list --path=books 7. Check in your browser While signed in, open http://localhost:8080/books , select a book, and click Write a Review . Submit a rating and optional comment. You should return to the book page and see your review. Open the form again for the same book and try to submit another: you should get the message You have already reviewed this book. You can inspect the new row in phpMyAdmin’s reviews table too.       A new review can be submitted and appears on its book page; a duplicate review for the same signed-in account is rejected with a form error.       If Request or Book is reported as an unknown class, check the imports at the top of ReviewController.php. If the route is missing, verify it was added inside the auth group and run php artisan route:list --path=books. If a view is missing, check resources/views/reviews/create.blade.php.      Create a review form, save a rating and comment, and check the result on the book page.    The controller uses the authenticated session rather than trusting a user_id in the submitted form.  ","label":"M06-S01"},{"title":"Edit and delete your own reviews","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m06-s02","excerpt":"Let users change their reviews, while preventing changes to reviews written by someone else.","body":"What changes in this activity You can now write a review, but you cannot correct or delete it. You will add the edit and delete actions, and make sure that a different user cannot change somebody else’s review.      1. Register routes for existing reviews Open routes/web.php and add the second resource route inside the existing auth group , after books.reviews . Keep the existing nested route from 06-01.       Route::resource('reviews', ReviewController::class)->only(['edit', 'update', 'destroy']); routes/web.php · inside the auth group     Route::resource('reviews', ReviewController::class)->only(['edit', 'update', 'destroy']); Why there are two resource routes An existing review has its own ID, so its edit and delete URLs can be /reviews/{review}/edit and /reviews/{review} . The create and store routes remain nested under a book.      2. Update the controller Open ReviewController.php and replace its contents with the class below. This keeps your 06-01 create/store logic and adds edit() , update() and destroy() . The authorizeOwner() method returns a 403 response when another user tries to change the review, even by typing the URL directly. It also reuses the same rating validation on create and update.       validate($this->validationRules());\n\n        // The migration's unique index on [book_id, user_id] already stops\n        // a second row for this exact pair existing - this check exists so\n        // trying anyway gets the same friendly redirect-back-with-errors\n        // treatment as any other validation failure, rather than a raw\n        // \"Duplicate entry\" database exception reaching the browser.\n        if ($book->reviews()->where('user_id', auth()->id())->exists()) {\n            return back()->withErrors([\n                'rating' => 'You have already reviewed this book.',\n            ])->withInput();\n        }\n\n        $validated['user_id'] = auth()->id();\n        $book->reviews()->create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Show the form for editing the given review.\n     */\n    public function edit(Review $review)\n    {\n        $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n        return view('reviews.edit', compact('review'));\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Update the given review.\n     */\n    public function update(Request $request, Review $review)\n    {\n        $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n        $review->update($request->validate($this->validationRules()));\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $review->book);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Delete the given review.\n     */\n    public function destroy(Review $review)\n    {\n        $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n        // $review->book has to be read before delete() - afterwards the\n        // row (and the foreign key that made this relationship work) is\n        // gone, and there'd be nothing left to redirect back to.\n        $book = $review->book;\n        $review->delete();\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Validation rules shared by store() and update() - a review still has\n     * to make sense the second time it's saved, not just the first.\n     */\n    private function validationRules(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'rating' => 'required|integer|between:1,5',\n            'comment' => 'nullable|string',\n        ];\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Guard edit/update/destroy against anyone but the review's own author.\n     * A stand-in for the Policy class Part 2 introduces - Laravel's actual\n     * mechanism for exactly this kind of check - kept this plain for now\n     * so that lesson isn't taught early by accident.\n     */\n    private function authorizeOwner(Review $review): void\n    {\n        if ($review->user_id !== auth()->id()) {\n            abort(403);\n        }\n    }\n} app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Controllers;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\n\nclass ReviewController extends Controller\n{\n    /**\n     * Show the form for leaving a review on the given book.\n     */\n    public function create(Book $book)\n    {\n        return view('reviews.create', compact('book'));\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Store a newly created review for the given book.\n     */\n    public function store(Request $request, Book $book)\n    {\n        $validated = $request->validate($this->validationRules());\n\n        // The migration's unique index on [book_id, user_id] already stops\n        // a second row for this exact pair existing - this check exists so\n        // trying anyway gets the same friendly redirect-back-with-errors\n        // treatment as any other validation failure, rather than a raw\n        // \"Duplicate entry\" database exception reaching the browser.\n        if ($book->reviews()->where('user_id', auth()->id())->exists()) {\n            return back()->withErrors([\n                'rating' => 'You have already reviewed this book.',\n            ])->withInput();\n        }\n\n        $validated['user_id'] = auth()->id();\n        $book->reviews()->create($validated);\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Show the form for editing the given review.\n     */\n    public function edit(Review $review)\n    {\n        $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n        return view('reviews.edit', compact('review'));\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Update the given review.\n     */\n    public function update(Request $request, Review $review)\n    {\n        $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n        $review->update($request->validate($this->validationRules()));\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $review->book);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Delete the given review.\n     */\n    public function destroy(Review $review)\n    {\n        $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n        // $review->book has to be read before delete() - afterwards the\n        // row (and the foreign key that made this relationship work) is\n        // gone, and there'd be nothing left to redirect back to.\n        $book = $review->book;\n        $review->delete();\n\n        return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Validation rules shared by store() and update() - a review still has\n     * to make sense the second time it's saved, not just the first.\n     */\n    private function validationRules(): array\n    {\n        return [\n            'rating' => 'required|integer|between:1,5',\n            'comment' => 'nullable|string',\n        ];\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Guard edit/update/destroy against anyone but the review's own author.\n     * A stand-in for the Policy class Part 2 introduces - Laravel's actual\n     * mechanism for exactly this kind of check - kept this plain for now\n     * so that lesson isn't taught early by accident.\n     */\n    private function authorizeOwner(Review $review): void\n    {\n        if ($review->user_id !== auth()->id()) {\n            abort(403);\n        }\n    }\n} 3. Make a shared review form Create the folder resources/views/reviews/partials . Create form.blade.php inside it with the following complete code. It handles both a blank create form and an edit form that starts with saved values.       {{-- Shared between create.blade.php and edit.blade.php. $review is only set\n     when editing - old('field', $review->field ?? '') falls back to the\n     review's current value there, or to an empty string on create, and to\n     whatever was just typed in on either page if validation just failed. --}} {{ __('Select a rating') }} @for ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++) rating ?? '') == $i)>{{ $i }} / 5 @endfor {{ old('comment', $review->comment ?? '') }} {{ isset($review) ? __('Save Changes') : __('Submit Review') }} resources/views/reviews/partials/form.blade.php     {{-- Shared between create.blade.php and edit.blade.php. $review is only set\n     when editing - old('field', $review->field ?? '') falls back to the\n     review's current value there, or to an empty string on create, and to\n     whatever was just typed in on either page if validation just failed. --}}\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"rating\" :value=\"__('Rating')\" />\n    <select id=\"rating\" name=\"rating\" class=\"mt-1 block w-full border-gray-300 focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 rounded-md shadow-sm\" required>\n        <option value=\"\">{{ __('Select a rating') }}</option>\n        @for ($i = 1; $i <= 5; $i++)\n            <option value=\"{{ $i }}\" @selected(old('rating', $review->rating ?? '') == $i)>{{ $i }} / 5</option>\n        @endfor\n    </select>\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('rating')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div>\n    <x-input-label for=\"comment\" :value=\"__('Comment')\" />\n    <textarea id=\"comment\" name=\"comment\" rows=\"4\"\n              class=\"mt-1 block w-full border-gray-300 focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 rounded-md shadow-sm\">{{ old('comment', $review->comment ?? '') }}</textarea>\n    <x-input-error :messages=\"$errors->get('comment')\" class=\"mt-2\" />\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"flex items-center gap-4\">\n    <x-primary-button>{{ isset($review) ? __('Save Changes') : __('Submit Review') }}</x-primary-button>\n</div> 4. Update the create page Replace the standalone reviews/create.blade.php from 06-01 with this shorter file. The form fields now come from the shared partial.       {{ __('Review') }}: {{ $book->title }} @csrf\n\n                    @include('reviews.partials.form') resources/views/reviews/create.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('Review') }}: {{ $book->title }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.reviews.store', $book) }}\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n\n                    @include('reviews.partials.form')\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 5. Create the edit page Create a new file resources/views/reviews/edit.blade.php for the edit page. The @method('PUT') line tells Laravel that this form updates an existing record.       {{ __('Edit Review') }}: {{ $review->book->title }} @csrf\n                    @method('PUT')\n\n                    @include('reviews.partials.form', ['review' => $review]) resources/views/reviews/edit.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('Edit Review') }}: {{ $review->book->title }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-2xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('reviews.update', $review) }}\" class=\"space-y-6\">\n                    @csrf\n                    @method('PUT')\n\n                    @include('reviews.partials.form', ['review' => $review])\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 6. Show Edit and Delete beside your own reviews Finally, replace the existing resources/views/reviews/partials/review.blade.php , first created in Module 04, with this version. It shows Edit and Delete only alongside reviews belonging to the signed-in account. The controller is still responsible for enforcing ownership.       {{ $review->user->name }} {{ $review->rating }} / 5 @if ($review->comment) {{ $review->comment }} @endif {{ $review->created_at->format('d M Y') }} @if ($review->user_id === auth()->id()) {{ __('Edit') }} @csrf\n                    @method('DELETE') {{ __('Delete') }} @endif resources/views/reviews/partials/review.blade.php     <div class=\"border-b border-gray-200 py-4 last:border-b-0\">\n    <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n        <span class=\"font-medium text-gray-900\">{{ $review->user->name }}</span>\n        <span class=\"text-sm text-gray-500\">{{ $review->rating }} / 5</span>\n    </div>\n\n    @if ($review->comment)\n        <p class=\"text-gray-700 mt-1\">{{ $review->comment }}</p>\n    @endif\n\n    <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between mt-1\">\n        <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-400\">{{ $review->created_at->format('d M Y') }}</p>\n\n        @if ($review->user_id === auth()->id())\n            <div class=\"flex items-center gap-3\">\n                <a href=\"{{ route('reviews.edit', $review) }}\" class=\"text-xs text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-800 hover:underline\">\n                    {{ __('Edit') }}\n                </a>\n\n                <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('reviews.destroy', $review) }}\"\n                      onsubmit=\"return confirm('Delete this review? This cannot be undone.');\">\n                    @csrf\n                    @method('DELETE')\n                    <button type=\"submit\" class=\"text-xs text-red-600 hover:text-red-800 hover:underline\">\n                        {{ __('Delete') }}\n                    </button>\n                </form>\n            </div>\n        @endif\n    </div>\n</div>  php artisan route:list --path=reviews Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan route:list --path=reviews 8. Check in your browser Sign in as the test account you used in 06-01. Open that book, edit your rating, save, then delete the review. Check that its row is gone in the browser and in phpMyAdmin. For the ownership check, use a second registered account: you should not see Edit or Delete beside the first user’s review. If you type the first review’s actual edit URL while signed in as the second user, you should receive 403 Forbidden .       The owner can edit and delete a review. Other signed-in users do not see the controls and cannot use those routes directly.       If a review form fails, check that both create.blade.php and edit.blade.php include reviews.partials.form and that the form file exists. If you see an unknown Review or Request class, compare all imports in the complete ReviewController code. If edit routes return 404, run route:list --path=reviews.      Let users change their reviews, while preventing changes to reviews written by someone else.    The controller must check ownership because anyone can attempt the URL directly.  ","label":"M06-S02"},{"title":"Create a My Reviews page","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m06-s03","excerpt":"Add a page where each user can find and manage the reviews they have written.","body":"The goal Your reviews currently appear under individual books. Next, add one page that lists only the reviews written by the logged-in user.      1. Add the My Reviews route Inside the authentication group in routes/web.php , add the following GET route after the existing review routes. There is no reviews.index resource action here; this page is specifically called reviews.mine .       Route::get('/my-reviews', [ReviewController::class, 'mine'])->name('reviews.mine'); routes/web.php · inside auth group     Route::get('/my-reviews', [ReviewController::class, 'mine'])->name('reviews.mine'); 2. Add the controller action Open app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php and add this method inside the existing ReviewController class , before its final closing brace. Do not remove the methods from 06-02. auth()->user()->reviews() filters by the current user, and with('book') loads book titles efficiently.       public function mine()\n{\n    $reviews = auth()->user()->reviews()\n        ->with('book')\n        ->latest()\n        ->paginate(12);\n\n    return view('reviews.mine', compact('reviews'));\n} app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php · method to add     public function mine()\n{\n    $reviews = auth()->user()->reviews()\n        ->with('book')\n        ->latest()\n        ->paginate(12);\n\n    return view('reviews.mine', compact('reviews'));\n} 3. Create the row partial Create resources/views/reviews/partials/mine-row.blade.php . Each review row links back to its book and includes the existing edit/delete routes.       {{ $review->book->title }} {{ $review->rating }} / 5 @if ($review->comment) {{ $review->comment }} @endif {{ $review->created_at->format('d M Y') }} {{ __('Edit') }} @csrf\n                @method('DELETE') {{ __('Delete') }} resources/views/reviews/partials/mine-row.blade.php     <div class=\"border-b border-gray-200 py-4 last:border-b-0\">\n    <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n        <a href=\"{{ route('books.show', $review->book) }}\" class=\"font-medium text-gray-900 hover:underline\">\n            {{ $review->book->title }}\n        </a>\n        <span class=\"text-sm text-gray-500\">{{ $review->rating }} / 5</span>\n    </div>\n\n    @if ($review->comment)\n        <p class=\"text-gray-700 mt-1\">{{ $review->comment }}</p>\n    @endif\n\n    <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between mt-1\">\n        <p class=\"text-xs text-gray-400\">{{ $review->created_at->format('d M Y') }}</p>\n\n        <div class=\"flex items-center gap-3\">\n            <a href=\"{{ route('reviews.edit', $review) }}\" class=\"text-xs text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-800 hover:underline\">\n                {{ __('Edit') }}\n            </a>\n\n            <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('reviews.destroy', $review) }}\"\n                  onsubmit=\"return confirm('Delete this review? This cannot be undone.');\">\n                @csrf\n                @method('DELETE')\n                <button type=\"submit\" class=\"text-xs text-red-600 hover:text-red-800 hover:underline\">\n                    {{ __('Delete') }}\n                </button>\n            </form>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</div> 4. Create the My Reviews page Create resources/views/reviews/mine.blade.php . It uses the row partial, shows an empty message when necessary, and adds pagination.       {{ __('My Reviews') }} @forelse ($reviews as $review)\n                    @include('reviews.partials.mine-row', ['review' => $review])\n                @empty {{ __(\"You haven't reviewed any books yet.\") }} {{ __('Browse the catalogue') }} {{ __('to find one.') }} @endforelse {{ $reviews->links() }} resources/views/reviews/mine.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n            {{ __('My Reviews') }}\n        </h2>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-4xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <div class=\"bg-white overflow-hidden shadow-sm rounded-lg p-6\">\n                @forelse ($reviews as $review)\n                    @include('reviews.partials.mine-row', ['review' => $review])\n                @empty\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600\">\n                        {{ __(\"You haven't reviewed any books yet.\") }}\n                        <a href=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\" class=\"text-gray-800 underline\">{{ __('Browse the catalogue') }}</a>\n                        {{ __('to find one.') }}\n                    </p>\n                @endforelse\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"mt-6\">\n                {{ $reviews->links() }}\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> 5. Add the navigation links In 04-03 , you added Books and Authors links to both versions of the Breeze menu. Now open src/resources/views/layouts/navigation.blade.php again. Add the My Reviews link once immediately after Authors in the desktop navigation list, and once after Authors in the mobile list. Keep the existing Dashboard, Books and Authors links. Do not replace the navigation layout.       {{ __('My Reviews') }} src/resources/views/layouts/navigation.blade.php · desktop     <x-nav-link :href=\"route('reviews.mine')\" :active=\"request()->routeIs('reviews.mine')\">\n    {{ __('My Reviews') }}\n</x-nav-link>  {{ __('My Reviews') }} src/resources/views/layouts/navigation.blade.php · mobile     <x-responsive-nav-link :href=\"route('reviews.mine')\" :active=\"request()->routeIs('reviews.mine')\">\n    {{ __('My Reviews') }}\n</x-responsive-nav-link>  php artisan route:list --path=my-reviews Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan route:list --path=my-reviews 6. Check in your browser While signed in, open http://localhost:8080/my-reviews or click My Reviews in the navigation. It should show just your reviews. Log in with a different test account: that account should see only its own reviews, or the empty-state message if it has none. Try Edit on a row and return to My Reviews afterwards.       My Reviews opens from navigation, lists only the current user’s reviews, and shows a useful message when that user has none.       If the page returns 404, inspect route:list --path=my-reviews. If it says method mine does not exist, make sure it is inside ReviewController, not after the closing brace. If the navigation breaks, check that you added a link rather than replacing a Breeze layout section.      Add a page where each user can find and manage the reviews they have written.    Starting from auth()->user()->reviews() restricts the results to that account.  ","label":"M06-S03"},{"title":"Test review creation and ownership","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m06-s04","excerpt":"Write feature tests that prove reviews work and that only their owners can change them.","body":"What the tests will check You have tested reviews by hand. Now add automated checks for creating, editing and deleting, including attempts made by the wrong user. Tests must run against a disposable test database , not an important database you wish to keep.      1. Create the feature test class From the Laravel src directory, generate the feature test file. This is different from the tests/Unit/ReviewTest.php you created earlier; both files can exist.       php artisan make:test ReviewTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan make:test ReviewTest 2. Add tests for the review actions Open tests/Feature/ReviewTest.php and replace its contents with the complete class below, including namespace, model imports, RefreshDatabase and all test methods.       create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n            'rating' => 4,\n            'comment' => 'Solid read.',\n        ]);\n\n        $review = Review::firstWhere(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id]);\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertNotNull($review);\n        $this->assertSame(4, $review->rating);\n    }\n\n    public function test_creating_a_review_without_a_rating_fails_validation_and_repopulates_the_form(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)\n            ->from(route('books.reviews.create', $book))\n            ->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n                'rating' => '',\n                'comment' => 'Should not save.',\n            ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.reviews.create', $book));\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('rating');\n        $response->assertSessionHasInput('comment', 'Should not save.');\n        $this->assertDatabaseMissing('reviews', ['comment' => 'Should not save.']);\n    }\n\n    public function test_reviewing_the_same_book_twice_fails_with_a_friendly_error(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n        Review::factory()->create(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n            'rating' => 3,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('rating');\n        $this->assertCount(1, $book->reviews()->where('user_id', $user->id)->get());\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_review_can_be_updated_by_its_owner(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 2]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('reviews.update', $review), [\n            'rating' => 5,\n            'comment' => 'Changed my mind - loved it.',\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $review->book));\n        $this->assertSame(5, $review->fresh()->rating);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_review_can_be_deleted_by_its_owner(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $user->id]);\n        $book = $review->book;\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('reviews.destroy', $review));\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($review);\n    }\n\n    public function test_editing_someone_elses_review_is_forbidden(): void\n    {\n        $owner = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $owner->id]);\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($someoneElse)->get(route('reviews.edit', $review));\n\n        $response->assertForbidden();\n    }\n\n    public function test_updating_someone_elses_review_is_forbidden(): void\n    {\n        $owner = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $owner->id, 'rating' => 3]);\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($someoneElse)->put(route('reviews.update', $review), [\n            'rating' => 1,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertForbidden();\n        $this->assertSame(3, $review->fresh()->rating);\n    }\n\n    public function test_deleting_someone_elses_review_is_forbidden(): void\n    {\n        $owner = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $owner->id]);\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($someoneElse)->delete(route('reviews.destroy', $review));\n\n        $response->assertForbidden();\n        $this->assertModelExists($review);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_full_write_edit_delete_flow_as_the_reviews_own_author(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $createResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n            'rating' => 3,\n            'comment' => 'Decent.',\n        ]);\n        $review = Review::firstWhere(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id]);\n        $createResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n\n        $updateResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('reviews.update', $review), [\n            'rating' => 5,\n            'comment' => 'Actually, loved it on a re-read.',\n        ]);\n        $updateResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertSame(5, $review->fresh()->rating);\n\n        $deleteResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('reviews.destroy', $review));\n        $deleteResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($review);\n    }\n} tests/Feature/ReviewTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Feature;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass ReviewTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_a_review_can_be_created(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n            'rating' => 4,\n            'comment' => 'Solid read.',\n        ]);\n\n        $review = Review::firstWhere(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id]);\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertNotNull($review);\n        $this->assertSame(4, $review->rating);\n    }\n\n    public function test_creating_a_review_without_a_rating_fails_validation_and_repopulates_the_form(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)\n            ->from(route('books.reviews.create', $book))\n            ->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n                'rating' => '',\n                'comment' => 'Should not save.',\n            ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.reviews.create', $book));\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('rating');\n        $response->assertSessionHasInput('comment', 'Should not save.');\n        $this->assertDatabaseMissing('reviews', ['comment' => 'Should not save.']);\n    }\n\n    public function test_reviewing_the_same_book_twice_fails_with_a_friendly_error(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n        Review::factory()->create(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n            'rating' => 3,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertSessionHasErrors('rating');\n        $this->assertCount(1, $book->reviews()->where('user_id', $user->id)->get());\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_review_can_be_updated_by_its_owner(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 2]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('reviews.update', $review), [\n            'rating' => 5,\n            'comment' => 'Changed my mind - loved it.',\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $review->book));\n        $this->assertSame(5, $review->fresh()->rating);\n    }\n\n    public function test_a_review_can_be_deleted_by_its_owner(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $user->id]);\n        $book = $review->book;\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('reviews.destroy', $review));\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($review);\n    }\n\n    public function test_editing_someone_elses_review_is_forbidden(): void\n    {\n        $owner = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $owner->id]);\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($someoneElse)->get(route('reviews.edit', $review));\n\n        $response->assertForbidden();\n    }\n\n    public function test_updating_someone_elses_review_is_forbidden(): void\n    {\n        $owner = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $owner->id, 'rating' => 3]);\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($someoneElse)->put(route('reviews.update', $review), [\n            'rating' => 1,\n        ]);\n\n        $response->assertForbidden();\n        $this->assertSame(3, $review->fresh()->rating);\n    }\n\n    public function test_deleting_someone_elses_review_is_forbidden(): void\n    {\n        $owner = User::factory()->create();\n        $review = Review::factory()->create(['user_id' => $owner->id]);\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($someoneElse)->delete(route('reviews.destroy', $review));\n\n        $response->assertForbidden();\n        $this->assertModelExists($review);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_full_write_edit_delete_flow_as_the_reviews_own_author(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $createResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->post(route('books.reviews.store', $book), [\n            'rating' => 3,\n            'comment' => 'Decent.',\n        ]);\n        $review = Review::firstWhere(['book_id' => $book->id, 'user_id' => $user->id]);\n        $createResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n\n        $updateResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->put(route('reviews.update', $review), [\n            'rating' => 5,\n            'comment' => 'Actually, loved it on a re-read.',\n        ]);\n        $updateResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertSame(5, $review->fresh()->rating);\n\n        $deleteResponse = $this->actingAs($user)->delete(route('reviews.destroy', $review));\n        $deleteResponse->assertRedirect(route('books.show', $book));\n        $this->assertModelMissing($review);\n    }\n} How the ownership tests work In the tests, actingAs($user) signs in as a particular account. assertForbidden() confirms that a different user receives HTTP 403 when attempting an action they do not own. The tests check actual database changes, not just whether buttons appear in Blade.       php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest 4. Run the full suite You should see all the new review feature tests pass. If a test fails, read the named failure and compare the route, controller action and form data with that test. Then run the full suite below. Do not rely on a specific fixed total number of tests, as that depends on which earlier exercises you completed.       php artisan test Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test 5. Quick browser confirmation Lastly, return to the browser and try submitting a rating without choosing a number. Confirm the form displays a validation error and preserves any comment. Then use a second user account to check that another person’s edit URL gives 403. These manual checks complement the automated ones.       Review feature tests pass, including invalid rating, duplicate review, owner edit/delete and forbidden non-owner actions.       An unknown model class in a test usually means a missing use App\\Models\\... import. If no tests are discovered, verify the file is tests/Feature/ReviewTest.php and the class extends Tests\\TestCase. RefreshDatabase may migrate the configured connection: check phpunit.xml and .env.testing before running it.      Write feature tests that prove reviews work and that only their owners can change them.    assertForbidden() checks that Laravel returned HTTP 403.  ","label":"M06-S04"},{"title":"Give users a role","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m07-s01","excerpt":"Give each account a reader or administrator role, then promote a test account safely.","body":"What you will build Signing in proves who somebody is (authentication). A role describes the kind of access they should have (authorisation). We will use two roles: user and admin . Ordinary registration must never create an admin automatically.      1. Add a role to the users table In your terminal inside src , generate a new migration. Do not edit the old users-table migration: it may already have run on your database.       php artisan make:migration add_role_to_users_table --table=users Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan make:migration add_role_to_users_table --table=users Edit the generated migration Open the new file in database/migrations/ . Keep its generated class structure and replace only the up() and down() methods. A default of user ensures existing accounts and future registrations are ordinary users.       public function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->string('role')->default('user');\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->dropColumn('role');\n    });\n} database/migrations/*_add_role_to_users_table.php · methods     public function up(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->string('role')->default('user');\n    });\n}\n\npublic function down(): void\n{\n    Schema::table('users', function (Blueprint $table) {\n        $table->dropColumn('role');\n    });\n} 2. Run the migration and inspect your accounts Use the same MySQL environment as the rest of the course. Run migrate rather than migrate:fresh so you do not discard the work you have already saved.       php artisan migrate\nphp artisan tinker Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan migrate\nphp artisan tinker 3. Promote one test account In Tinker, find the account you registered earlier. Replace the example address with its actual email. Assign the new role directly , then save it. Run each line in order.       $admin = \\App\\Models\\User::where('email', 'your-admin@example.com')->first();\n// If the result is null, check the email address before continuing.\n$admin->role = 'admin';\n$admin->save();\n$admin->fresh()->role; Tinker · run one line at a time     $admin = \\App\\Models\\User::where('email', 'your-admin@example.com')->first();\n// If the result is null, check the email address before continuing.\n$admin->role = 'admin';\n$admin->save();\n$admin->fresh()->role; What about User.php? The column can be read as $user->role without adding it to $fillable . Do not add role to User::$fillable : a public registration form must not be able to mass-assign somebody the administrator role. The earlier $admin->update(['role' => 'admin']) approach may silently fail because the field is not fillable; direct assignment followed by save() is the correct choice here.       The database has a role column; an ordinary account reports user; your selected test admin reports admin. A newly registered account is a user.       If Tinker prints null for $admin, check the exact email used at registration. If a migration fails, check its filename and ensure Schema and Blueprint imports remain in the generated file. Never put a role selection field on the registration page.      Give each account a reader or administrator role, then promote a test account safely.    Developer-controlled promotion keeps the administrator role away from ordinary registration.  ","label":"M07-S01"},{"title":"Protect the book management routes","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m07-s02","excerpt":"Add one reusable role check and use it to protect book create, edit and delete actions.","body":"Why routes need protection Hiding an Add Book link is not enough. A visitor could still type /books/create or send a POST request. Laravel must check permissions before the controller runs . We will allow every signed-in user to browse books, but only an admin to change them.      1. Create the middleware class Run this from the Laravel src directory. Middleware checks a request as it enters the application.       php artisan make:middleware EnsureUserHasRole Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan make:middleware EnsureUserHasRole 2. Check the role Open app/Http/Middleware/EnsureUserHasRole.php and replace the generated file with the complete class below. The $role argument lets us specify a required role on a route. If the logged-in user does not have that role, Laravel returns 403 Forbidden .       user()?->role === $role, 403);\n\n        return $next($request);\n    }\n} app/Http/Middleware/EnsureUserHasRole.php · complete class     <?php\n\nnamespace App\\Http\\Middleware;\n\nuse Closure;\nuse Illuminate\\Http\\Request;\nuse Symfony\\Component\\HttpFoundation\\Response;\n\nclass EnsureUserHasRole\n{\n    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next, string $role): Response\n    {\n        abort_unless($request->user()?->role === $role, 403);\n\n        return $next($request);\n    }\n} 3. Register a short name for this middleware Open bootstrap/app.php . Near the top, add the use line below beside the other imports. This lets Laravel refer to the class by name.       use App\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureUserHasRole; bootstrap/app.php · add alongside existing imports     use App\\Http\\Middleware\\EnsureUserHasRole; Find the existing withMiddleware callback Farther down in bootstrap/app.php , you will find one ->withMiddleware(...) callback. In a fresh Laravel 12 project it looks like the first example below. Edit this existing callback; do not create a second one.       ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {\n    //\n}) bootstrap/app.php · before your change     ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {\n    //\n}) Replace that callback with this version Only the two alias() lines are new. Keep the surrounding withRouting and withExceptions calls in the file untouched. The alias means role:admin on a route runs EnsureUserHasRole , passing admin as its required role.       ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {\n    $middleware->alias([\n        'role' => EnsureUserHasRole::class,\n    ]);\n}) bootstrap/app.php · complete updated withMiddleware callback     ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {\n    $middleware->alias([\n        'role' => EnsureUserHasRole::class,\n    ]);\n}) 4. Put administrator routes before the general book routes Open routes/web.php . Inside the existing auth group , find the current Route::resource('books', ...) line from Module 05. Replace only that book resource route with the following two blocks. Keep all other routes, including authors and reviews, unchanged. Order matters: the specific /books/create route must be registered before the parameterised /books/{book} route. Otherwise Laravel may treat the word create as a book ID and return 404.       // More specific administrator actions must come first.\nRoute::middleware('role:admin')->group(function () {\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)\n        ->only(['create', 'store', 'edit', 'update', 'destroy']);\n});\n\n// General browsing routes come afterwards.\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class)\n    ->only(['index', 'show']); routes/web.php · inside the existing auth group, replace books resource route     // More specific administrator actions must come first.\nRoute::middleware('role:admin')->group(function () {\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)\n        ->only(['create', 'store', 'edit', 'update', 'destroy']);\n});\n\n// General browsing routes come afterwards.\nRoute::resource('books', BookController::class)\n    ->only(['index', 'show']); 5. Try it out with two different accounts First, sign in as your administrator. Open the Books page, choose Add Book and create a test book. Open that book, edit its title, save your changes, then delete the test book. All three actions should work. Next, sign out and sign in as an ordinary user whose role is user . You should still be able to see the list of books and open a book page. For now, the Add/Edit/Delete links may still be visible; we will hide them in 07-04. Try visiting /books/create directly. You should see 403 Forbidden , not the form. Try editing an existing book at /books/1/edit (replace 1 with a real book ID). That should also be forbidden. If you want to check delete protection, use a test request rather than deleting a real record. An ordinary user must never be able to create, change or delete a book. Why 403? It means Laravel understood the request but refused it because this account does not have permission. If /books/create returns 404 for an administrator, check the route order: the admin-only resource routes must appear before the general books/{book} route. For a further check, run the commands below. The route list helps you inspect the routes, but the two-account browser test is the important part.       php artisan route:clear\nphp artisan route:list --path=books Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan route:clear\nphp artisan route:list --path=books  The admin can open /books/create. An ordinary user receives 403 for book-management routes. Both accounts can browse books, and /books/create is not accidentally matched as /books/{book}.       If the alias is unknown, check the import and confirm it is inside the existing withMiddleware callback in bootstrap/app.php. If everyone gets 403, check the Tinker role assignment from 07-01. If /books/create returns 404, move the administrator routes above the index/show resource registration and run route:clear.      Add one reusable role check and use it to protect book create, edit and delete actions.    Laravel registers and matches route patterns in order. The fixed /books/create route needs to precede the dynamic /books/{book} route.  ","label":"M07-S02"},{"title":"Understand and verify review ownership","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m07-s03","excerpt":"Check that people can edit or delete their own reviews, but never somebody else’s.","body":"A different kind of permission Roles control broad access, such as who can manage the book catalogue. Ownership controls individual records: two people may both have the user role, but each should change only their own reviews. The Book Reviews app already implements this in Module 06. We will inspect and test it rather than write another controller.      1. Find the check that protects reviews Open app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php . Locate the authorizeOwner() method you added in Module 06. It compares the review’s stored user_id with the ID of the account currently signed in.       private function authorizeOwner(Review $review): void\n{\n    if ($review->user_id !== auth()->id()) {\n        abort(403);\n    }\n} app/Http/Controllers/ReviewController.php · existing method (do not add it again)     private function authorizeOwner(Review $review): void\n{\n    if ($review->user_id !== auth()->id()) {\n        abort(403);\n    }\n} 2. Check which actions call it In the same controller , confirm that edit() , update() and destroy() each call $this->authorizeOwner($review) before returning a form, saving changes or deleting data. The check must happen on the server; hiding an Edit button alone is not sufficient.       public function update(Request $request, Review $review)\n{\n    $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n    $review->update($request->validate($this->validationRules()));\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $review->book);\n} ReviewController.php · example from the existing update() action     public function update(Request $request, Review $review)\n{\n    $this->authorizeOwner($review);\n\n    $review->update($request->validate($this->validationRules()));\n\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $review->book);\n} 3. Make sure users only see their own controls Open resources/views/reviews/partials/review.blade.php . Module 06 already wraps the Edit and Delete controls in this condition. It improves the user experience, but the controller check above is still the security rule.       @if ($review->user_id === auth()->id())\n    {{-- Existing Edit link and Delete form --}}\n@endif resources/views/reviews/partials/review.blade.php · existing condition (do not duplicate)     @if ($review->user_id === auth()->id())\n    {{-- Existing Edit link and Delete form --}}\n@endif 4. Test with two users Sign in as User A and write a review. Sign out, then sign in as User B and open that same book. User B should see the review but not its Edit/Delete controls. Try the review’s edit URL directly as User B; Laravel should return 403 . Sign back in as User A and confirm editing and deleting your own review still work. The checks above already exist. You do not need to add new review routes or rewrite the ReviewController.       php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest  A user may edit and delete their own reviews. Another user cannot edit or delete that review through the interface or a direct request (403). Review feature tests still pass.       A 404 usually means the review ID does not exist; a 403 means the record exists but belongs to somebody else. If other users can update a review, confirm the ownership check runs in update() and destroy(), not just edit().      Check that people can edit or delete their own reviews, but never somebody else’s.    The interface hides controls for usability; the controller must enforce ownership when requests arrive.  ","label":"M07-S03"},{"title":"Show the right controls and test book roles","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m07-s04","excerpt":"Hide book-management controls from readers and verify that the server blocks unauthorised requests.","body":"Why update the interface? The middleware in 07-02 protects the routes. Now we will make the interface match those rules: an ordinary user should not see buttons they cannot use. The server-side checks are still essential.      1. Show Add Book only to administrators Open resources/views/books/index.blade.php . Locate the existing Add Book link. Wrap it inside this condition, without removing the existing link markup.       @if (auth()->user()?->role === 'admin')\n    {{-- Your existing Add Book link goes here. --}}\n@endif resources/views/books/index.blade.php · wrap the existing Add Book link     @if (auth()->user()?->role === 'admin')\n    {{-- Your existing Add Book link goes here. --}}\n@endif 2. Show Edit and Delete Book only to administrators Open resources/views/books/show.blade.php . Wrap the existing edit link and delete form in the same condition. This should not hide book details or reviews from ordinary users.       @if (auth()->user()?->role === 'admin')\n    {{-- Your existing Edit Book link and Delete Book form go here. --}}\n@endif resources/views/books/show.blade.php · wrap existing management controls     @if (auth()->user()?->role === 'admin')\n    {{-- Your existing Edit Book link and Delete Book form go here. --}}\n@endif 3. Keep the earlier book tests meaningful Some of your Module 05 feature tests create an ordinary user before making an authorised store, edit, update or delete request. Those requests now require an administrator. Change the user factory in the management tests only ; keep tests for ordinary book browsing as ordinary users.       $user = User::factory()->create(['role' => 'admin']); tests/Feature/BookTest.php · in authorised book management tests     $user = User::factory()->create(['role' => 'admin']); 4. Write a role-access feature test Generate a fresh feature test, then replace its contents with the complete example. The tests check actual HTTP responses , including a reader trying the protected URLs directly. They also confirm administrators can open the management forms.       php artisan make:test RoleAccessTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan make:test RoleAccessTest  create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.index'))->assertOk();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.show', $book))->assertOk();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.create'))->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.edit', $book))->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->post(route('books.store'), [\n            'title' => 'Not allowed', 'year' => 2020,\n        ])->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => 'Changed', 'year' => 2020,\n        ])->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book))\n            ->assertForbidden();\n    }\n\n    public function test_administrators_can_open_book_management_forms(): void\n    {\n        $admin = User::factory()->create(['role' => 'admin']);\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $this->actingAs($admin)->get(route('books.create'))->assertOk();\n        $this->actingAs($admin)->get(route('books.edit', $book))->assertOk();\n    }\n} tests/Feature/RoleAccessTest.php · complete file     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Feature;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass RoleAccessTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_readers_can_browse_but_cannot_manage_books(): void\n    {\n        $reader = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.index'))->assertOk();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.show', $book))->assertOk();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.create'))->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->get(route('books.edit', $book))->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->post(route('books.store'), [\n            'title' => 'Not allowed', 'year' => 2020,\n        ])->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->put(route('books.update', $book), [\n            'title' => 'Changed', 'year' => 2020,\n        ])->assertForbidden();\n        $this->actingAs($reader)->delete(route('books.destroy', $book))\n            ->assertForbidden();\n    }\n\n    public function test_administrators_can_open_book_management_forms(): void\n    {\n        $admin = User::factory()->create(['role' => 'admin']);\n        $book = Book::factory()->create();\n\n        $this->actingAs($admin)->get(route('books.create'))->assertOk();\n        $this->actingAs($admin)->get(route('books.edit', $book))->assertOk();\n    }\n} 5. Run the tests and compare two accounts Run the role tests, review tests and then the whole suite. In the browser, confirm an ordinary user can browse and review books but cannot see Add/Edit/Delete Book controls. Confirm an administrator can use those controls. Your review ownership checks should still work for both roles.       php artisan test --filter=RoleAccessTest\nphp artisan test --filter=BookTest\nphp artisan test --filter=ReviewTest\nphp artisan test Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=RoleAccessTest\nphp artisan test --filter=BookTest\nphp artisan test --filter=ReviewTest\nphp artisan test Apply the same idea to authors later The catalogue currently provides author index and show pages. A similar pattern could restrict any future author management routes to administrators, using the same role middleware and Blade conditions. We are not adding Author CRUD in this lesson. You can develop that independently if your project needs it.       Readers can browse and write their own reviews but cannot manage books. Administrators can manage books. Neither role can edit or delete someone else’s review. All three test classes pass.       If BookTest starts returning 403, confirm the test user has the admin role for modification cases. If RoleAccessTest expects 403 but sees 404 for /books/create, confirm the order of resource routes in 07-02. If the interface still shows Add Book for ordinary users, check that you wrapped the existing link in the Blade condition.      Hide book-management controls from readers and verify that the server blocks unauthorised requests.    Different rules protect different resources: role middleware for book management and per-review ownership checks for editing and deleting reviews.  ","label":"M07-S04"},{"title":"Search books by title","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m08-s01","excerpt":"Add a search box that finds books by their titles and remembers what the user typed.","body":"What you will add Your catalogue now contains books and reviews. In this activity you will add a search box that finds books by title. Author and year search come in the next activity.      1. Add title search to the Book model Open app/Models/Book.php . Add the import below with the other imports near the top. Then add the scopeSearch() method inside the Book class , before its final closing brace. Keep all existing fields and relationships.       use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Builder; app/Models/Book.php · add to imports     use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Builder;  public function scopeSearch(Builder $query, ?string $term): Builder\n{\n    if (! $term) {\n        return $query;\n    }\n\n    return $query->where('title', 'like', '%'.$term.'%');\n} app/Models/Book.php · add method inside class     public function scopeSearch(Builder $query, ?string $term): Builder\n{\n    if (! $term) {\n        return $query;\n    }\n\n    return $query->where('title', 'like', '%'.$term.'%');\n} What the scope does A Laravel local scope is a reusable piece of a database query. A method named scopeSearch can be called as Book::search($term) . If the search box is empty, it returns the unfiltered query.      2. Update the catalogue controller Open app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php . This controller already imports Illuminate\\Http\\Request from Module 05. If that import is missing in your file, add it. Replace only the existing index() method with this method. Leave create, show, store, edit, update and destroy unchanged.       use Illuminate\\Http\\Request; app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · import if needed     use Illuminate\\Http\\Request;  public function index(Request $request)\n    {\n        // Eager-load authors, and aggregate the review count/average rating\n        // in the same query (withCount/withAvg), rather than loading every\n        // review row just to print a number - avoids the N+1 problem without\n        // pulling data the listing never displays.\n        $books = Book::with('authors')\n            ->withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->search($request->query('search'))\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12)\n            ->withQueryString();\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    } app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · replace index() only     public function index(Request $request)\n    {\n        // Eager-load authors, and aggregate the review count/average rating\n        // in the same query (withCount/withAvg), rather than loading every\n        // review row just to print a number - avoids the N+1 problem without\n        // pulling data the listing never displays.\n        $books = Book::with('authors')\n            ->withCount('reviews')\n            ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n            ->search($request->query('search'))\n            ->orderBy('title')\n            ->paginate(12)\n            ->withQueryString();\n\n        return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n    } How the request reaches the query The controller reads ?search=... from the URL, applies your scope and keeps any query-string value when building pagination links. withCount() and withAvg() supply the review statistics already shown on your book cards.      3. Add the search box to the listing page Open resources/views/books/index.blade.php . Replace the whole file with the version below. It retains the Add Book link and book cards from earlier lessons, and puts the search form above the list.       {{ __('Books') }} {{ __('Add Book') }} {{ __('Search') }} @if (request('search')) {{ __('Clear') }} @endif @forelse ($books as $book)\n                    @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                @empty @if (request('search'))\n                            {{ __('No books match \":search\".', ['search' => request('search')]) }}\n                        @else\n                            {{ __('No books in the catalogue yet.') }}\n                        @endif @endforelse {{ $books->links() }} resources/views/books/index.blade.php     <x-app-layout>\n    <x-slot name=\"header\">\n        <div class=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n            <h2 class=\"font-semibold text-xl text-gray-800 leading-tight\">\n                {{ __('Books') }}\n            </h2>\n\n            <a href=\"{{ route('books.create') }}\" class=\"inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 bg-gray-800 border border-transparent rounded-md font-semibold text-xs text-white uppercase tracking-widest hover:bg-gray-700\">\n                {{ __('Add Book') }}\n            </a>\n        </div>\n    </x-slot>\n\n    <div class=\"py-12\">\n        <div class=\"max-w-7xl mx-auto sm:px-6 lg:px-8\">\n            <form method=\"GET\" action=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\" class=\"mb-6 flex gap-3\">\n                <input type=\"search\" name=\"search\" value=\"{{ request('search') }}\" placeholder=\"Search by title...\"\n                       class=\"flex-1 rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-gray-500 focus:ring-gray-500\">\n\n                <button type=\"submit\" class=\"inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 bg-gray-800 border border-transparent rounded-md font-semibold text-xs text-white uppercase tracking-widest hover:bg-gray-700\">\n                    {{ __('Search') }}\n                </button>\n\n                @if (request('search'))\n                    <a href=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\" class=\"inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 text-xs text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-800 hover:underline\">\n                        {{ __('Clear') }}\n                    </a>\n                @endif\n            </form>\n\n            <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6\">\n                @forelse ($books as $book)\n                    @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n                @empty\n                    <p class=\"text-gray-600 col-span-full\">\n                        @if (request('search'))\n                            {{ __('No books match \":search\".', ['search' => request('search')]) }}\n                        @else\n                            {{ __('No books in the catalogue yet.') }}\n                        @endif\n                    </p>\n                @endforelse\n            </div>\n\n            <div class=\"mt-6\">\n                {{ $books->links() }}\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</x-app-layout> Why this form uses GET The search form uses GET because it only retrieves records. That gives you a normal URL like /books?search=Dune , which you can bookmark or share. No CSRF token is needed for this read-only GET form.      4. Try the new search in your browser Open http://localhost:8080/books while logged in. Search for part of a title you can see in your catalogue. To make a predictable example, create a book titled Dune using Add Book or through Tinker. Search for Dune , then use Clear to return to all books. The search field should keep what you typed.       php artisan tinker\n# Once Tinker opens, run:\n\\App\\Models\\Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']); Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan tinker\n# Once Tinker opens, run:\n\\App\\Models\\Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);  Searching at /books?search=Dune shows the matching book, the text remains in the search field, and Clear returns to the full catalogue.       If search() is unknown, check that the Book model method is named scopeSearch and that the Builder import is present. If Request is unknown, add use Illuminate\\Http\\Request; to BookController. If no books appear, confirm you have test records and are signed in.      Add a search box that finds books by their titles and remembers what the user typed.    A local scope is a reusable query filter.  ","label":"M08-S01"},{"title":"Search by author and year","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m08-s02","excerpt":"Extend the search box so it also finds books by author name or publication year.","body":"Building on 07-01 The title search is working. Now let the same search box match an author’s name or an exact publication year. You will change the Book model, not create another form.      1. Extend the scope Open app/Models/Book.php . Find scopeSearch() from 07-01 and replace that method only with the following version. Keep the existing Book relationships, use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Builder; and other imports.       public function scopeSearch(Builder $query, ?string $term): Builder\n    {\n        if (! $term) {\n            return $query;\n        }\n\n        return $query->where(function (Builder $query) use ($term) {\n            $query->where('title', 'like', '%'.$term.'%')\n                ->orWhereHas('authors', fn (Builder $query) => $query->where('name', 'like', '%'.$term.'%'));\n\n            if (is_numeric($term)) {\n                $query->orWhere('year', $term);\n            }\n        });\n    } app/Models/Book.php · replace scopeSearch() only     public function scopeSearch(Builder $query, ?string $term): Builder\n    {\n        if (! $term) {\n            return $query;\n        }\n\n        return $query->where(function (Builder $query) use ($term) {\n            $query->where('title', 'like', '%'.$term.'%')\n                ->orWhereHas('authors', fn (Builder $query) => $query->where('name', 'like', '%'.$term.'%'));\n\n            if (is_numeric($term)) {\n                $query->orWhere('year', $term);\n            }\n        });\n    } How the three searches work whereHas('authors') searches author records related to a book through the existing many-to-many relationship. The conditions are wrapped in one where(function ...) so the title, author and year alternatives stay grouped together. A numeric search term is also checked against the year.      2. Update the hint in the search box Open resources/views/books/index.blade.php . Change the placeholder on the search input from Search by title... to Search by title, author, or year... . No route or controller changes are needed.       placeholder=\"Search by title, author, or year...\" resources/views/books/index.blade.php · input attribute     placeholder=\"Search by title, author, or year...\" 3. Try each search type In the browser, open /books and search using (a) a known title, (b) an author name from /authors , and (c) a four-digit year for a book you can see. Each should return the relevant book. A phrase that matches nothing should show the empty-results message.      If the author search returns no matches If your named test book is not associated with an author, use an existing seeded book for the author search or attach an author through Tinker. The relationship is what makes the author search work.       The same search field finds a book by title, a related author’s name, or its publication year.       Check the Book::authors() relationship and author_book pivot rows if author search fails. Ensure the replacement scope has all its closing parentheses and braces. A search for year uses an exact numeric comparison, not a partial match.      Extend the search box so it also finds books by author name or publication year.    whereHas() applies a condition to related author records.  ","label":"M08-S02"},{"title":"Test the catalogue search","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m08-s03","excerpt":"Add tests that check the search works both in Eloquent and on the actual catalogue page.","body":"What you will test You have checked searching in the browser. Now add tests so that future changes to the model and catalogue controller do not silently break title, author or year searches.      1. Prepare the model tests Open your existing tests/Unit/BookTest.php from Module 02. It should already contain a BookTest class. Confirm these imports are present; add any that are missing. Do not create a second BookTest class.       use App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase; tests/Unit/BookTest.php · imports to check     use App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase; 2. Add the search-scope tests Paste the following three methods inside the existing BookTest class , immediately before its final closing brace. Do not paste another <?php , namespace or class declaration. The tests check a match, no match and an empty search.       public function test_the_search_scope_matches_a_books_title_an_authors_name_or_its_year(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n        $book->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']));\n\n        $this->assertTrue(Book::search('Dune')->exists());\n        $this->assertTrue(Book::search('Herbert')->exists());\n        $this->assertTrue(Book::search('1965')->exists());\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_search_scope_finds_nothing_for_a_non_matching_term(): void\n    {\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n\n        $this->assertFalse(Book::search('zzz')->exists());\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_search_scope_with_a_blank_term_returns_every_book(): void\n    {\n        Book::factory()->count(3)->create();\n\n        $this->assertSame(3, Book::search(null)->count());\n        $this->assertSame(3, Book::search('')->count());\n    } tests/Unit/BookTest.php · new methods     public function test_the_search_scope_matches_a_books_title_an_authors_name_or_its_year(): void\n    {\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n        $book->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']));\n\n        $this->assertTrue(Book::search('Dune')->exists());\n        $this->assertTrue(Book::search('Herbert')->exists());\n        $this->assertTrue(Book::search('1965')->exists());\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_search_scope_finds_nothing_for_a_non_matching_term(): void\n    {\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n\n        $this->assertFalse(Book::search('zzz')->exists());\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_search_scope_with_a_blank_term_returns_every_book(): void\n    {\n        Book::factory()->count(3)->create();\n\n        $this->assertSame(3, Book::search(null)->count());\n        $this->assertSame(3, Book::search('')->count());\n    } 3. Add browser-response tests Open your existing tests/Feature/BookTest.php from Module 04/05. Ensure it imports Author , Book and User . Then add these three test methods inside its existing class. They make HTTP requests and check that the correct book appears on the page.       use App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\User; tests/Feature/BookTest.php · imports to check     use App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\User;  public function test_the_index_page_search_matches_a_books_title(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Some Other Title']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Dune']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Some Other Title');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_search_matches_an_authors_name(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        $book->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']));\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Some Other Title']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Herbert']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Some Other Title');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_search_matches_a_year(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Some Other Title', 'year' => 1999]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => '1965']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Some Other Title');\n    } tests/Feature/BookTest.php · new methods     public function test_the_index_page_search_matches_a_books_title(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Some Other Title']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Dune']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Some Other Title');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_search_matches_an_authors_name(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        $book->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->create(['name' => 'Frank Herbert']));\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Some Other Title']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Herbert']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Some Other Title');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_index_page_search_matches_a_year(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune', 'year' => 1965]);\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Some Other Title', 'year' => 1999]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => '1965']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Some Other Title');\n    } Avoid duplicate test methods If the named methods already exist in your file, compare them rather than duplicating them. Adding the same method twice causes a PHP error.       php artisan test --filter=BookTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=BookTest 5. Interpret failures The newly added tests should pass. If an author test fails, confirm the author is attached through $book->authors()->attach(...) . If an HTTP test fails, confirm BookController@index() uses ->search($request->query('search')) .       The search scope and the catalogue page both pass tests for title, author and year searches.       If you see a duplicate-method error, remove the extra method in the existing class. If a model is not found, check use App\\Models\\... imports. Run tests with a disposable testing connection; RefreshDatabase may apply migrations.      Add tests that check the search works both in Eloquent and on the actual catalogue page.    The two layers catch different problems.  ","label":"M08-S03"},{"title":"Check empty results and pagination","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m08-s04","excerpt":"Make sure searches with no matches are clear, and page links keep the search term.","body":"The final search checks A search should remain useful when no results are found or when there are many results. This activity checks both cases and finishes the search tests.      1. Check the empty-results message Open resources/views/books/index.blade.php and find the book list. Your file from 07-01 should already have @forelse / @empty and {{ $books->links() }} . If so, leave it in place. The important part of the empty-state branch is shown below for reference.       @empty @if (request('search'))\n            {{ __('No books match \":search\".', ['search' => request('search')]) }}\n        @else\n            {{ __('No books in the catalogue yet.') }}\n        @endif @endforelse resources/views/books/index.blade.php · inside the @empty branch     @empty\n    <p class=\"text-gray-600 col-span-full\">\n        @if (request('search'))\n            {{ __('No books match \":search\".', ['search' => request('search')]) }}\n        @else\n            {{ __('No books in the catalogue yet.') }}\n        @endif\n    </p>\n@endforelse 2. Check this in the browser If no book matches, the page should say No books match \"...\" rather than suggesting the whole catalogue is empty. The search box should still show the submitted term.      3. Add the last search tests Open tests/Feature/BookTest.php . Add the following two methods inside the existing class , before the final closing brace. Keep the other book tests from 07-03.       public function test_the_index_page_shows_a_message_for_a_non_matching_search(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'zzz']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertDontSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertSee('No books match \"zzz\".');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_search_box_repopulates_with_the_current_search_term(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Dune']));\n\n        $response->assertSee('value=\"Dune\"', false);\n    } tests/Feature/BookTest.php · two new methods     public function test_the_index_page_shows_a_message_for_a_non_matching_search(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'zzz']));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertDontSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertSee('No books match \"zzz\".');\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_search_box_repopulates_with_the_current_search_term(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Dune']));\n\n        $response->assertSee('value=\"Dune\"', false);\n    }  php artisan test --filter=BookTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=BookTest 5. Test pagination in your browser To check pagination manually, you need more than 12 matching books. In Tinker, create 15 with a common word, then search for that word. Go to page 2 and check that search= is still present in the URL. The controller’s withQueryString() keeps that search filter active across pages.       php artisan tinker\n# Inside Tinker:\n\\App\\Models\\Book::factory()->count(15)->create(['title' => 'Workshop sample']); Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan tinker\n# Inside Tinker:\n\\App\\Models\\Book::factory()->count(15)->create(['title' => 'Workshop sample']); 6. Clean up if needed After testing, you can remove those temporary records individually or simply leave them in your disposable development database. Do not run migrate:fresh just to clean up if you want to keep other work.       Unmatched searches show a helpful message, the search field remembers the term, and page 2 keeps the same search in the URL.       If page 2 loses the search, make sure the BookController paginator ends in ->paginate(12)->withQueryString(). If the empty message never appears, check the @forelse/@empty section in books/index.blade.php.      Make sure searches with no matches are clear, and page links keep the search term.    withQueryString() carries query-string parameters into generated pagination links.  ","label":"M08-S04"},{"title":"Check coverage of My Reviews","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m09-s01","excerpt":"Finish the feature tests so the personal reviews page is checked as carefully as book and review actions.","body":"What we are checking Before finishing, review whether each feature has a test. Your review tests cover creating, editing and deleting, but the My Reviews page needs its own tests. You will add these to the same file you created in 06-04.      1. Check the existing tests In a terminal in the Laravel src directory, run the review feature tests first. This gives you a starting point before adding anything.       php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest 2. Test My Reviews Open tests/Feature/ReviewTest.php . The file should already import Book , Review and User and use RefreshDatabase . Add these four new methods inside the existing ReviewTest class . Do not replace the methods from 06-04.       public function test_guests_are_redirected_to_login_from_my_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $response = $this->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('login', absolute: false));\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_lists_the_users_own_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $book->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'rating' => 5,\n            'comment' => 'A genuine classic.',\n        ]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertSee('A genuine classic.');\n        $response->assertSee('5 / 5');\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_does_not_list_another_users_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'My Book'])\n            ->reviews()->create(['user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 4]);\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Someone Elses Book'])\n            ->reviews()->create(['user_id' => $someoneElse->id, 'rating' => 2]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertSee('My Book');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Someone Elses Book');\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_shows_the_most_recently_written_review_first(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $older = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Older Review']);\n        $newer = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Newer Review']);\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $older->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'created_at' => now()->subDay(),\n        ]);\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $newer->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'created_at' => now(),\n        ]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertSeeInOrder(['Newer Review', 'Older Review']);\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_shows_a_message_when_the_user_has_no_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee(\"You haven't reviewed any books yet.\");\n    } tests/Feature/ReviewTest.php · add four methods     public function test_guests_are_redirected_to_login_from_my_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $response = $this->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertRedirect(route('login', absolute: false));\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_lists_the_users_own_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $book = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $book->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'rating' => 5,\n            'comment' => 'A genuine classic.',\n        ]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee('Dune');\n        $response->assertSee('A genuine classic.');\n        $response->assertSee('5 / 5');\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_does_not_list_another_users_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $someoneElse = User::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'My Book'])\n            ->reviews()->create(['user_id' => $user->id, 'rating' => 4]);\n        Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Someone Elses Book'])\n            ->reviews()->create(['user_id' => $someoneElse->id, 'rating' => 2]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertSee('My Book');\n        $response->assertDontSee('Someone Elses Book');\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_shows_the_most_recently_written_review_first(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n        $older = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Older Review']);\n        $newer = Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Newer Review']);\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $older->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'created_at' => now()->subDay(),\n        ]);\n        Review::factory()->create([\n            'book_id' => $newer->id,\n            'user_id' => $user->id,\n            'created_at' => now(),\n        ]);\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertSeeInOrder(['Newer Review', 'Older Review']);\n    }\n\n    public function test_my_reviews_shows_a_message_when_the_user_has_no_reviews(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('reviews.mine'));\n\n        $response->assertOk();\n        $response->assertSee(\"You haven't reviewed any books yet.\");\n    } What the tests prove These five tests cover a guest redirect, the current user’s reviews, hiding another user’s reviews, newest-first ordering, and an empty-state message.      3. Run the checks Run just this test class again, then the whole suite. You should see the new tests pass as well as the earlier create, edit and delete tests. If one fails, check the mine() query and the reviews.mine Blade page introduced in 06-03.       php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest\nphp artisan test Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=ReviewTest\nphp artisan test 4. Check in the browser As a final manual check, sign in with each of two test accounts and open http://localhost:8080/my-reviews . Each account should see only its own reviews. The address should redirect to Login when you are signed out.       My Reviews has automated checks for authentication, ownership, ordering and the empty state, and the full suite still passes.       If you get a duplicate method error, do not add the same test twice. If the guest test returns 200 instead of redirecting, verify the /my-reviews route is inside Route::middleware('auth'). If records from other users appear, use auth()->user()->reviews() rather than Review::all().      Finish the feature tests so the personal reviews page is checked as carefully as book and review actions.    The ownership test confirms another account’s records stay hidden.  ","label":"M09-S01"},{"title":"Check database queries as the app grows","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m09-s02","excerpt":"Add tests to catch unnecessary database queries when showing more books and reviews.","body":"Why this matters If a catalogue loads one extra database query for every book or review displayed, it can get much slower as the data grows. This is called the N+1 query problem . Here you will add a test that checks the number of database queries stays steady as more records are displayed.      1. Review the catalogue query Eloquent with() loads related models in advance. In the completed controller code, with('authors') , withCount('reviews') and withAvg('reviews', 'rating') are used to avoid repeated database lookups in the book listing. Your BookController@index() from 07-01 already contains these calls.       Book::with('authors')\n    ->withCount('reviews')\n    ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n    ->search($request->query('search'))\n    ->orderBy('title')\n    ->paginate(12)\n    ->withQueryString(); app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php · excerpt     Book::with('authors')\n    ->withCount('reviews')\n    ->withAvg('reviews', 'rating')\n    ->search($request->query('search'))\n    ->orderBy('title')\n    ->paginate(12)\n    ->withQueryString(); 2. Generate a query-count test Create a new feature test file. The --unit flag is not used because this test makes HTTP requests.       php artisan make:test QueryCountTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan make:test QueryCountTest 3. Add the complete test class Open tests/Feature/QueryCountTest.php and replace its contents with the following complete class. It includes the required imports, five tests and two helper methods. seedBooks() builds sample data; countQueries() measures database activity during each page request.       create();\n\n        $this->seedBooks(3);\n        $withThreeBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index')));\n\n        $this->seedBooks(12); // 15 books total now\n        $withFifteenBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index')));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeBooks, $withFifteenBooks);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_books_show_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_review_count(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $fewReviews = Book::factory()->create();\n        $fewReviews->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->count(2)->create());\n        Review::factory()->count(3)->create(['book_id' => $fewReviews->id]);\n        $withThreeReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.show', $fewReviews)));\n\n        $manyReviews = Book::factory()->create();\n        $manyReviews->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->count(2)->create());\n        Review::factory()->count(15)->create(['book_id' => $manyReviews->id]);\n        $withFifteenReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.show', $manyReviews)));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeReviews, $withFifteenReviews);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_authors_index_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_author_count(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        Author::factory()->count(3)->create();\n        $withThreeAuthors = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.index')));\n\n        Author::factory()->count(12)->create(); // 15 authors total now\n        $withFifteenAuthors = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.index')));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeAuthors, $withFifteenAuthors);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_authors_show_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_book_count(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $fewBooks = Author::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->count(3)->create()->each(fn (Book $book) => $book->authors()->attach($fewBooks));\n        $withThreeBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.show', $fewBooks)));\n\n        $manyBooks = Author::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->count(15)->create()->each(fn (Book $book) => $book->authors()->attach($manyBooks));\n        $withFifteenBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.show', $manyBooks)));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeBooks, $withFifteenBooks);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_my_reviews_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_review_count(): void\n    {\n        $fewReviews = User::factory()->create();\n        Review::factory()->count(3)->create(['user_id' => $fewReviews->id]);\n        $withThreeReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($fewReviews)->get(route('reviews.mine')));\n\n        $manyReviews = User::factory()->create();\n        Review::factory()->count(15)->create(['user_id' => $manyReviews->id]);\n        $withFifteenReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($manyReviews)->get(route('reviews.mine')));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeReviews, $withFifteenReviews);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Create $count books, each with two shared authors and two reviews -\n     * enough relationships attached per book that an N+1 anywhere in the\n     * chain would show up as a query count that grows with $count.\n     */\n    private function seedBooks(int $count): void\n    {\n        $authors = Author::factory()->count(2)->create();\n\n        Book::factory()->count($count)->create()->each(function (Book $book) use ($authors) {\n            $book->authors()->attach($authors);\n            Review::factory()->count(2)->create(['book_id' => $book->id]);\n        });\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Run $fn and return exactly how many database queries it issued.\n     */\n    private function countQueries(callable $fn): int\n    {\n        DB::enableQueryLog();\n        DB::flushQueryLog();\n\n        $fn();\n\n        $count = count(DB::getQueryLog());\n        DB::disableQueryLog();\n\n        return $count;\n    }\n} tests/Feature/QueryCountTest.php     <?php\n\nnamespace Tests\\Feature;\n\nuse App\\Models\\Author;\nuse App\\Models\\Book;\nuse App\\Models\\Review;\nuse App\\Models\\User;\nuse Illuminate\\Foundation\\Testing\\RefreshDatabase;\nuse Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\DB;\nuse Tests\\TestCase;\n\nclass QueryCountTest extends TestCase\n{\n    use RefreshDatabase;\n\n    public function test_the_books_index_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_catalogue_size(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $this->seedBooks(3);\n        $withThreeBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index')));\n\n        $this->seedBooks(12); // 15 books total now\n        $withFifteenBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.index')));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeBooks, $withFifteenBooks);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_books_show_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_review_count(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $fewReviews = Book::factory()->create();\n        $fewReviews->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->count(2)->create());\n        Review::factory()->count(3)->create(['book_id' => $fewReviews->id]);\n        $withThreeReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.show', $fewReviews)));\n\n        $manyReviews = Book::factory()->create();\n        $manyReviews->authors()->attach(Author::factory()->count(2)->create());\n        Review::factory()->count(15)->create(['book_id' => $manyReviews->id]);\n        $withFifteenReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('books.show', $manyReviews)));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeReviews, $withFifteenReviews);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_authors_index_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_author_count(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        Author::factory()->count(3)->create();\n        $withThreeAuthors = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.index')));\n\n        Author::factory()->count(12)->create(); // 15 authors total now\n        $withFifteenAuthors = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.index')));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeAuthors, $withFifteenAuthors);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_authors_show_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_book_count(): void\n    {\n        $user = User::factory()->create();\n\n        $fewBooks = Author::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->count(3)->create()->each(fn (Book $book) => $book->authors()->attach($fewBooks));\n        $withThreeBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.show', $fewBooks)));\n\n        $manyBooks = Author::factory()->create();\n        Book::factory()->count(15)->create()->each(fn (Book $book) => $book->authors()->attach($manyBooks));\n        $withFifteenBooks = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($user)->get(route('authors.show', $manyBooks)));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeBooks, $withFifteenBooks);\n    }\n\n    public function test_the_my_reviews_page_runs_the_same_number_of_queries_regardless_of_review_count(): void\n    {\n        $fewReviews = User::factory()->create();\n        Review::factory()->count(3)->create(['user_id' => $fewReviews->id]);\n        $withThreeReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($fewReviews)->get(route('reviews.mine')));\n\n        $manyReviews = User::factory()->create();\n        Review::factory()->count(15)->create(['user_id' => $manyReviews->id]);\n        $withFifteenReviews = $this->countQueries(fn () => $this->actingAs($manyReviews)->get(route('reviews.mine')));\n\n        $this->assertSame($withThreeReviews, $withFifteenReviews);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Create $count books, each with two shared authors and two reviews -\n     * enough relationships attached per book that an N+1 anywhere in the\n     * chain would show up as a query count that grows with $count.\n     */\n    private function seedBooks(int $count): void\n    {\n        $authors = Author::factory()->count(2)->create();\n\n        Book::factory()->count($count)->create()->each(function (Book $book) use ($authors) {\n            $book->authors()->attach($authors);\n            Review::factory()->count(2)->create(['book_id' => $book->id]);\n        });\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Run $fn and return exactly how many database queries it issued.\n     */\n    private function countQueries(callable $fn): int\n    {\n        DB::enableQueryLog();\n        DB::flushQueryLog();\n\n        $fn();\n\n        $count = count(DB::getQueryLog());\n        DB::disableQueryLog();\n\n        return $count;\n    }\n} How to read the result The tests compare requests with a small and a larger set of records. The number of SQL queries should remain the same even when there are more books, authors or reviews. The tests check query count, not the amount of data sent or exact page load time.       php artisan test --filter=QueryCountTest Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan test --filter=QueryCountTest 5. Investigate a failure If the book listing test fails, check the with(...) and aggregate calls in BookController@index() . If the author page test fails, check AuthorController@show() and the relationships its book-card partial needs. If My Reviews fails, check ->with('book') in ReviewController@mine() .      Optional experiment Optionally, in a disposable working copy, remove with('authors') briefly from BookController@index() , run the test and observe whether it fails, then restore the line immediately . Do not leave the deliberate performance problem in your project.       The five query-count tests pass, and the controller methods load the related data needed by their Blade views.       If countQueries or seedBooks is undefined, ensure you copied the full class including its private helper methods. If a model class is unknown, check the imports at the top. Never hardcode an expected query count from somebody else’s machine; compare the small and large cases.      Add tests to catch unnecessary database queries when showing more books and reviews.    N+1 means extra queries often grow with the number of related records displayed.  ","label":"M09-S02"},{"title":"Write a short project handoff","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m09-s03","excerpt":"Describe what you built, how to check it, and what could be improved later.","body":"What you will produce Good software projects need a clear record of what is finished and what is still an idea. This activity is about documenting your work, not writing another controller or migration.      1. Create the handoff file At the root of your Git working folder, alongside README.md and compose.yaml , create a new Markdown file named HANDOFF.md . Do not replace the project’s existing README or edit the finished reference repository.       # Book Reviews: handoff notes\n\n## What works now\n- Users can register, sign in and sign out.\n- Signed-in users can browse and search books and authors.\n- Signed-in users can add, edit and delete books.\n- A user can write one review per book, then edit or delete their own review.\n- My Reviews lists only reviews written by the current user.\n\n## What to check before sharing\n- `php artisan test` passes against a safe test database.\n- Main pages work in a browser.\n- `.env` and credentials are not committed.\n\n## Possible future improvements\n- Use Laravel policies for permissions and ownership checks.\n- Add roles for different levels of access.\n- Consider a separate model for different editions of a book.\n\n## Limitations of this version\n- Book editing is available to all signed-in users; it is not restricted by role.\n- Book records do not have separate edition records. HANDOFF.md · starter text     # Book Reviews: handoff notes\n\n## What works now\n- Users can register, sign in and sign out.\n- Signed-in users can browse and search books and authors.\n- Signed-in users can add, edit and delete books.\n- A user can write one review per book, then edit or delete their own review.\n- My Reviews lists only reviews written by the current user.\n\n## What to check before sharing\n- `php artisan test` passes against a safe test database.\n- Main pages work in a browser.\n- `.env` and credentials are not committed.\n\n## Possible future improvements\n- Use Laravel policies for permissions and ownership checks.\n- Add roles for different levels of access.\n- Consider a separate model for different editions of a book.\n\n## Limitations of this version\n- Book editing is available to all signed-in users; it is not restricted by role.\n- Book records do not have separate edition records. 2. Make the document accurate Read through the example and update it to reflect what you have actually completed . If a feature has not worked during your walkthrough, mark it as incomplete rather than claiming it is finished.      3. Explain what is not implemented Your current review controller uses a direct ownership check and returns 403 when a different user tries to edit or delete a review. Laravel policies offer a more structured way of handling these permissions, but are not part of this application’s implementation yet. Likewise, the app currently lets any signed-in user manage books. Roles and more granular permissions would be future work.      4. Note a design choice The book record currently stores fields such as ISBN, publisher and edition number directly. A larger project might model multiple editions separately. That is a possible extension, not something to build now.      5. Review the handoff Open HANDOFF.md in your editor’s Markdown preview and check that the headings and lists display correctly. Confirm that it clearly separates working functionality, known limitations and future ideas.       HANDOFF.md describes the working application, known limits and possible next steps without presenting future features as already built.       If you are unsure whether something is finished, verify it in the browser or with a test first. Avoid copying code for policies or roles from the reference application: those are discussion topics here, not required changes.      Describe what you built, how to check it, and what could be improved later.    Policies are a possible extension, not a feature this version already implements.  ","label":"M09-S03"},{"title":"Verify and save your finished project","type":"Learning Lab","url":"learning-lab/index.html#m09-s04","excerpt":"Run tests, check the app in the browser and commit your completed work.","body":"Before you finish This final activity is a checklist to confirm the project still works from beginning to end. Run commands in the indicated places and check the browser with a signed-in account.      1. Run Laravel checks In the running Laravel container, from src , check your database migrations, registered routes and test suite. Make sure the test database configuration is safe before running php artisan test .       php artisan migrate:status\nphp artisan route:list\nphp artisan test Terminal · Laravel container / src     php artisan migrate:status\nphp artisan route:list\nphp artisan test 2. Check the front-end build Still inside the Laravel workspace, run the front-end build command used during setup. This checks the Blade/Vite styles and JavaScript assets used by Breeze. If your Docker setup runs npm on the host rather than inside the container, use the same method you used in Module 03.       npm run build Terminal · in src, where npm is installed     npm run build 3. Final browser checklist In your browser, work through these flows in order. Check that each one finishes without an error.       Register another account or sign in with an existing one. Open /books and /authors . Add a book, edit it, upload an image, then delete your temporary book. Write, edit and delete a review. Confirm another account cannot edit someone else’s review. Open /my-reviews , check that only your reviews appear and that an empty account sees the empty state. Search for a title, an author and a year. Check a no-match search and a paginated search. Sign out and confirm protected pages redirect to Login.      4. Review your Git changes Leave your handoff notes accurate. From the Git repository root (the folder with compose.yaml and src ), inspect your changes before committing. The review step is important: do not accidentally add credentials or generated dependencies.       git status\ngit diff --check\ngit diff --stat Terminal · Git repository root     git status\ngit diff --check\ngit diff --stat 5. Save your working version Stage the files you actually changed, including HANDOFF.md if you created it. The example below stages src and that handoff file, but review git status before committing . Keep src/.env , dependency directories and local database files out of Git.       git add src HANDOFF.md\ngit status\ngit commit -m \"Complete Book Reviews learning project\" Terminal · Git repository root     git add src HANDOFF.md\ngit status\ngit commit -m \"Complete Book Reviews learning project\" 6. Confirm you are finished If Git says a file is ignored or there is nothing to commit, do not force it. Review what has changed. A successful commit is a saved checkpoint, not a substitute for the tests and browser checks above.       Laravel tests and route checks pass, the complete browser walkthrough works, the handoff is accurate, and your reviewed project changes are committed without secrets.       Use the terminal in the correct folder: Artisan and npm from src; Git from the repository root. If tests fail, fix the first named error before proceeding. If the build fails, verify npm dependencies are installed using the setup instructions.      Run tests, check the app in the browser and commit your completed work.    Git commands can run from subfolders, but the repository root makes it easiest to review all changes.  ","label":"M09-S04"},{"title":"Plan a reading-list data model","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x10-s01","excerpt":"Give every signed-in user a private list of books and their current reading status.","body":"Your brief A signed-in user should be able to add a book to their reading list. The same book can appear on many users’ lists, but no user should see or modify someone else’s statuses.      Where to begin Start with your completed Module 09 application. Open app/Models/User.php , app/Models/Book.php and database/migrations/ . No UI work yet.      Your implementation roadmap Sketch the relationship Draw User ↔ Book. One user saves many books, and a book can be saved by many users. Each connection carries its own status. Create the table Use php artisan make:migration create_book_user_table . Add user_id , book_id , status and timestamps. Use foreign keys and a composite unique index. Add both relationships In User.php , add books() ; in Book.php , add readers() . Both should use belongsToMany() and expose pivot status . Experiment in Tinker Run migrations, create two users and a book, then attach that book with different statuses. Update one status and detach the entry.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation A user can save several books; two users can save the same book. The database rejects two rows with the same user–book pair. Changing one user’s status does not alter another user’s status.      Stretch goal Add the date a book was finished, without requiring it for books not yet finished.      Give every signed-in user a private list of books and their current reading status.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X10-S01"},{"title":"Build the reading-list interface","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x10-s02","excerpt":"Let readers save books and manage their statuses from a personal page.","body":"Your brief Add an action on the book-detail page to save or change its status. Build a “My Reading List” page grouped or filtered by status.      Where to begin Reuse the routes, forms and Breeze navigation patterns from Modules 04–06. Work in routes/web.php , a new reading-list controller, books/show.blade.php and a new reading-list view.      Your implementation roadmap Design two screens Decide how a signed-in reader chooses a status on a book page, and how “My Reading List” groups books. Include an empty-list state. Add protected routes Create routes for showing the list, saving/changing a status, and removing an entry inside the existing auth route group. Write user-scoped actions Use auth()->user()->books() , not a user ID from the request. Validate status using a fixed set such as want_to_read , reading , finished . Connect the interface Create a POST form on the book page with @csrf , and show the list in a new Blade view. Add a desktop and mobile navigation link. Try full lifecycle Add a book, change its status, remove it and refresh after each action. Sign in as another user and compare.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Guests are redirected to login before saving or viewing a private list. The selected status survives a refresh; removing a book removes only that user’s entry. The desktop and mobile navigation both reach the new page.      Stretch goal Add optional personal notes, visible only to their owner.      Let readers save books and manage their statuses from a personal page.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X10-S02"},{"title":"Protect and test reading lists","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x10-s03","excerpt":"Prove that reading-list data is private and reliable.","body":"Your brief Write feature tests that cover normal use and attempted access to another person’s reading-list entries.      Where to begin Create tests/Feature/ReadingListTest.php . Revisit the feature-test patterns in Modules 05, 06 and 08.      Your implementation roadmap Test guests Write tests for an unauthenticated visit and save request. Decide whether redirect-to-login is expected. Test the happy path With a signed-in user, save a book, change status and remove it. Assert database pivot state after each action. Test invalid input Submit an unknown status; check the validation response and confirm no invalid value was saved. Test isolation Create users A and B with the same book but different statuses. Make an authenticated request as A and verify B’s record remains untouched. Run regression tests Run your new test file and the full suite. Note one edge case you initially missed.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Tests cover guest, valid, invalid and cross-user scenarios. An attempted change to user B’s data does not affect B’s record. Existing core tests still pass.      Stretch goal Add a count of finished books on the reading-list page.      Prove that reading-list data is private and reliable.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X10-S03"},{"title":"Explore ratings data","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x11-s01","excerpt":"Build a trustworthy top-rated books query using existing reviews.","body":"Your brief Display the highest-rated books while avoiding the trap of treating a single five-star review as equally persuasive as many five-star reviews.      Where to begin Inspect app/Models/Book.php , app/Models/Review.php and BookController@index() . Module 07 already introduced rating aggregates, so build on them.      Your implementation roadmap Define ranking rules Write down the minimum number of reviews required, how unrated books behave, and a tie-breaker (for example title). Prepare known data Create books with zero, one and several reviews. Include a five-star book with one review and a four-star book with many reviews. Build a top-rated query Use aggregate counts/averages in the database query, filter using your threshold, order by average, and paginate or limit the result set. Display the result Present title, average rating and number of reviews. Explain your minimum-review rule on the page. Check ordering Compare expected order against the page using your seeded data.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Books below the minimum-review threshold are omitted from the ranked list. Ties have a predictable ordering and unrated books are handled deliberately. No per-book review queries are required to show aggregates.      Stretch goal Offer two sorts: highest-rated and most-reviewed.      Build a trustworthy top-rated books query using existing reviews.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X11-S01"},{"title":"Recommend something similar","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x11-s02","excerpt":"Add understandable “You might also like” suggestions to book pages.","body":"Your brief Show a short list of other books by one or more of the current book’s authors. Clearly explain why they were suggested.      Where to begin Use the Book–Author relationship already built in Module 02. Work near BookController@show and the existing books/show.blade.php page.      Your implementation roadmap Define simple rules Recommend at most five different books sharing an author with the current book. Exclude the current book itself. Prototype in Tinker Find author IDs on a book; use a relationship-aware query to find other books with any of those authors. Use efficient loading Load any author data required for cards in the same query, rather than querying once per displayed book. Integrate with book detail Pass recommendations to the view, add a “More by these authors” section and a useful empty message. Check difficult cases Try books with two authors, no shared authors and multiple matches. Watch out for duplicates.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation No recommendation is the current book. The list is capped and does not repeat the same book. The empty state is clear when no other book shares an author.      Stretch goal Introduce a genre relationship and combine shared-genre and shared-author signals. Clearly label this as an optional schema change.      Add understandable “You might also like” suggestions to book pages.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X11-S02"},{"title":"Measure query performance","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x11-s03","excerpt":"Test that rankings and recommendations do not grow into an N+1 problem.","body":"Your brief Prove that your ratings page and related-book suggestions work efficiently and remain correct when the database grows.      Where to begin Revisit the query-count test from Module 08 and create new feature tests, for example tests/Feature/RecommendationsTest.php .      Your implementation roadmap Make repeatable fixtures Construct known ratings and author links that produce an unambiguous expected order. Test ranking logic Assert the minimum-review rule, average ordering and tie-breakers. Include an unrated book. Test recommendations Verify same-author matches, exclusion of the current book, result limit and empty state. Compare query counts Measure queries when displaying a small versus larger book list; repeat under comparable conditions. Fix any N+1 issue Inspect where the page accesses relationships. Add eager loading or aggregates only where required.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Automated tests prove both correctness and edge cases. A written query-count comparison explains what you measured. Any performance improvement leaves the expected output unchanged.      Stretch goal Show a “Recently reviewed” section and compare its query cost to your rankings page.      Test that rankings and recommendations do not grow into an N+1 problem.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X11-S03"},{"title":"Design the search interaction","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x12-s01","excerpt":"Plan an accessible, progressive enhancement of the existing search form. Follow the guided checkpoints and use the hints only if you need them.","body":"Your brief As someone types into the existing book search, update results without reloading the whole page. The original server-rendered form must still work if JavaScript is unavailable.      Where to begin Start from the Module 07 catalogue search in resources/views/books/index.blade.php . Open developer tools and inspect the existing query string on the Books page.      Choose one supported implementation path To reduce unnecessary complexity, use server-rendered HTML fragments for this extension. Laravel already knows how to draw the book cards. The browser only needs to request replacement HTML and display it. You do not need a separate JSON API or a new JavaScript framework. The existing GET /books?search=... must continue to produce a complete HTML page for ordinary navigation.      Start with these two Blade regions Open resources/views/books/index.blade.php . Find the search form and book grid. Give the form an ID, and wrap the results grid and pagination in a container with a stable ID. Add a status element that can announce loading or errors. Choose IDs consistently, as the JavaScript will look for them.        books/index.blade.php · attributes to adapt · example to adapt     <form id=\"book-search\" method=\"GET\" action=\"{{ route('books.index') }}\">\n    <!-- Keep your existing search input and submit button -->\n</form>\n\n<p id=\"search-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<div id=\"book-results\">\n    <!-- Existing book grid and pagination go here -->\n</div> Draw the sequence before coding User types → wait approximately 300 ms → request search results → replace the grid → update the status message. If the user types again before a request finishes, the older result must not overwrite the newer one. First checkpoint: without any JavaScript, submitting the GET form still searches normally. The browser address should show ?search=... .      Your implementation roadmap Describe desired behaviour Write a short scenario for typing, deleting characters, zero matches, slow responses and a failed request. Choose a response format Compare returning a small HTML fragment with returning JSON. Choose one and explain how it will update the existing results region. Mark up the interface Identify the search input, results container and status message. Plan keyboard and screen-reader-friendly status updates. Preserve normal submission Keep the search form’s server-rendered GET behaviour so it works when JavaScript is disabled. Make a request sketch Outline event → debounce → fetch → update results. Decide how stale responses will be ignored.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation The form still works with JavaScript disabled. The plan addresses loading, errors, no matches and accessibility. Your chosen HTML/JSON response format has a clear reason.      Stretch goal Keep the current search term in the URL so users can bookmark or share their search.      Plan an accessible, progressive enhancement of the existing search form. Follow the guided checkpoints and use the hints only if you need them.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X12-S01"},{"title":"Make search update live","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x12-s02","excerpt":"Connect the browser input to the existing Laravel search behaviour. Follow the guided checkpoints and use the hints only if you need them.","body":"Your brief Use the existing search logic to fetch and display matching books as the user types, without adding a framework.      Where to begin Work in the existing books index view, a small JavaScript file loaded by your layout/build setup, and a Laravel route or controller action. Reuse Module 07’s search scope.      Keep the server response small First move the existing book results markup (including pagination) into a new partial such as resources/views/books/partials/results.blade.php . In the full index view, use @include('books.partials.results') inside the book-results container. Both the full page and the live search response can now reuse that one partial.      A controller pattern to adapt Update BookController@index(Request $request) after it prepares the filtered $books collection. If the request asks for a fragment, return only the partial. Otherwise, return the existing full-page view. Do not duplicate the search query.       if ($request->header('X-Search-Fragment') === '1') {\n    return view('books.partials.results', compact('books'));\n}\n\nreturn view('books.index', compact('books')); BookController@index · after building $books · example to adapt     if ($request->header('X-Search-Fragment') === '1') {\n    return view('books.partials.results', compact('books'));\n}\n\nreturn view('books.index', compact('books')); Begin with one fetch, not the entire script Create resources/js/live-search.js , or add code to an existing JS entry point. Ensure your build loads it (for example, import it from resources/js/app.js in a Vite-based Breeze project). First test a single request in the browser console. It should return markup containing the expected book cards.       const form = document.querySelector('#book-search');\nconst results = document.querySelector('#book-results');\nconst input = form?.querySelector('input[name=\"search\"]');\n\nif (form && results && input) {\n    const url = new URL(form.action);\n    url.searchParams.set('search', input.value);\n    // Test this request before adding the input event handler.\n    fetch(url, { headers: { 'X-Search-Fragment': '1' } })\n        .then(response => {\n            if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Search failed');\n            return response.text();\n        })\n        .then(html => { results.innerHTML = html; })\n        .catch(error => console.error(error));\n} resources/js/live-search.js · request example · example to adapt     const form = document.querySelector('#book-search');\nconst results = document.querySelector('#book-results');\nconst input = form?.querySelector('input[name=\"search\"]');\n\nif (form && results && input) {\n    const url = new URL(form.action);\n    url.searchParams.set('search', input.value);\n    // Test this request before adding the input event handler.\n    fetch(url, { headers: { 'X-Search-Fragment': '1' } })\n        .then(response => {\n            if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Search failed');\n            return response.text();\n        })\n        .then(html => { results.innerHTML = html; })\n        .catch(error => console.error(error));\n} Now add responsive behaviour Typing: attach an input event listener and read input.value . Debouncing: keep a timer variable. On every keystroke, call clearTimeout(timer) , then set a new setTimeout(..., 300) . Older requests: create an AbortController per request; abort the previous one before starting the next. Also check that the returned search term still matches the latest input before updating the grid. Status: write simple messages into search-status with textContent (for example “Searching…” or “Unable to load results”). Ignore deliberate abort errors. Pagination: initially, let pagination links navigate normally. Dynamic pagination can be a stretch goal.       let timer;\ninput.addEventListener('input', () => {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n    timer = setTimeout(() => {\n        // Call your async search function with input.value here.\n    }, 300);\n}); live-search.js · debounce pattern (adapt) · example to adapt     let timer;\ninput.addEventListener('input', () => {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n    timer = setTimeout(() => {\n        // Call your async search function with input.value here.\n    }, 300);\n}); Common errors and how to recognise them The entire layout appears inside the results area: the request did not include the fragment header, or the controller returned the full view. Nothing happens when typing: check the browser console, the CSS selectors and whether the JS file is imported by Vite. Results jump backwards: an older response is replacing a newer one; check abort and current-query logic.      Your implementation roadmap Return suitable results Implement a lightweight endpoint or conditional response using the existing search scope. Keep normal GET requests returning the full view. Listen for input Read the search field value and construct a URL with URLSearchParams . Avoid hand-building an unescaped query string. Debounce network calls Delay each request by around 250–350 ms. Reset the timer when input changes. Prevent stale updates Abort the previous request or track a request sequence number before rendering new results. Render feedback safely Show loading, no-results and errors. Prefer safe text insertion or trusted server-rendered HTML over unescaped strings. Check real interactions Type quickly, delete the term, and throttle the network. Confirm the page never reverts to outdated results.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Results update without a full navigation while JavaScript is enabled. Rapid typing never leaves a stale result list on screen. Errors and empty matches produce understandable messages.      Stretch goal Add a simple sort control and retain the search term when sorting.      Connect the browser input to the existing Laravel search behaviour. Follow the guided checkpoints and use the hints only if you need them.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X12-S02"},{"title":"Test and refine live search","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x12-s03","excerpt":"Verify accessible behaviour, graceful errors and correct results. Follow the guided checkpoints and use the hints only if you need them.","body":"Your brief Test the server response and the browser experience under realistic conditions.      Where to begin Use browser developer tools and create feature tests for your chosen asynchronous endpoint, alongside the existing Module 07 search tests.      Work through four concrete scenarios Normal form: disable JavaScript, search for an existing book, and confirm the full page still works. Fragment response: request /books?search=Dune with the X-Search-Fragment: 1 header. The response should contain book results but not the application layout. Empty results: search for a distinctive nonsense term. The existing Blade empty state should appear inside the results container. Slow network: in browser dev tools, throttle network speed. Type two different searches quickly and confirm the older response never replaces the newer one.      A small feature-test starting point Create a new feature test (or extend BookTest ). Use a signed-in account if the Books route requires authentication, as it does in the core walkthrough. Assert the fragment contains a matching title but not the surrounding page header.       public function test_search_fragment_returns_matching_books(): void\n{\n    $user = User::factory()->create();\n    Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n    $response = $this->actingAs($user)\n        ->withHeader('X-Search-Fragment', '1')\n        ->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Dune']));\n\n    $response->assertOk()->assertSee('Dune');\n    $response->assertDontSee('<html', false);\n} tests/Feature/BookSearchTest.php · test idea · example to adapt     public function test_search_fragment_returns_matching_books(): void\n{\n    $user = User::factory()->create();\n    Book::factory()->create(['title' => 'Dune']);\n\n    $response = $this->actingAs($user)\n        ->withHeader('X-Search-Fragment', '1')\n        ->get(route('books.index', ['search' => 'Dune']));\n\n    $response->assertOk()->assertSee('Dune');\n    $response->assertDontSee('<html', false);\n} Check accessibility and recovery Use only the keyboard to reach and type in the search field. The role=\"status\" element should announce changes without stealing focus. Temporarily disconnect the network and confirm the page shows an understandable message rather than silently failing. Test an empty search term to restore the whole catalogue. Final regression: run php artisan test --filter=BookSearchTest , php artisan test , and test ordinary search with JavaScript disabled.      Your implementation roadmap Protect the old behaviour Disable JavaScript and confirm GET search still produces the correct page and URL. Test server responses Write Laravel tests for a match, empty results, and a request with unexpected/missing parameters. Simulate a race Use network throttling: make query A slow and query B fast. Confirm A cannot overwrite B after B has appeared. Check accessibility Navigate using only the keyboard. Ensure loading and result-count updates are announced appropriately. Check errors and regressions Simulate offline/failed requests and verify a useful status. Run both search tests and the full suite.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Old-style search still works without JavaScript. Out-of-order responses, empty results and network errors are handled. Both automated checks and a short manual browser checklist are complete.      Stretch goal Add a small “Clear search” control that restores the original catalogue.      Verify accessible behaviour, graceful errors and correct results. Follow the guided checkpoints and use the hints only if you need them.    A clear definition of success makes an independent implementation easier to design and assess.  ","label":"X12-S03"},{"title":"Polish the responsive book grid","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x13-s01","excerpt":"Improve the existing three-column catalogue layout with stronger visual hierarchy, consistent cards and better small-screen behaviour.","body":"Your design challenge The catalogue already has a responsive 1/2/3-column grid. Your task is to refine it, not to build the same grid again. Make each book easy to scan, ensure images behave consistently, and retain the search and pagination controls.      Where to begin Starting point: Use your completed Module 09 Book Reviews app. This challenge improves its existing interface; it does not require new controllers, migrations or external front-end libraries. Open resources/views/books/index.blade.php and resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php . Compare the current list at desktop and mobile widths. Decide what should stand out: cover, title, author and rating.      Checkpoint 1 · Understand the existing grid Find the existing grid classes. Resize the browser while watching how the number of columns changes. Try changing the spacing or the breakpoint, then choose the version that reads best.       @forelse ($books as $book)\n        @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n    @empty No books found. @endforelse resources/views/books/index.blade.php · existing layout to inspect     <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6\">\n    @forelse ($books as $book)\n        @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book])\n    @empty\n        <p class=\"col-span-full\">No books found.</p>\n    @endforelse\n</div> Checkpoint 2 · Improve the card Work in the existing card partial. Try a consistent image area, a clearer title, subtle borders or a more intentional rating position. Keep the existing cover URL expression, author names and rating logic; they are already wired to your models.       {{ $book->title }} {{-- Reuse the existing author, year and review-rating markup here. --}} resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php · classes to try     <article class=\"flex h-full flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white shadow-sm transition-shadow hover:shadow-md\">\n    <div class=\"aspect-[3/4] bg-gray-50 p-4\">\n        <img src=\"{{ $book->image ? Storage::url($book->image) : asset('images/book-placeholder.png') }}\"\n             alt=\"Cover of {{ $book->title }}\"\n             class=\"h-full w-full object-contain\">\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"flex flex-1 flex-col gap-2 p-5\">\n        <h3 class=\"text-lg font-semibold leading-snug\">\n            <a href=\"{{ route('books.show', $book) }}\" class=\"rounded focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-indigo-600\">\n                {{ $book->title }}\n            </a>\n        </h3>\n        {{-- Reuse the existing author, year and review-rating markup here. --}}\n    </div>\n</article> Checkpoint 3 · Build a reusable star rating The existing card shows an average such as 4.2 / 5 (6 reviews) . Let’s keep that useful number but add five visual stars . The star display should represent the average rating, not one individual review. Create resources/views/books/partials/rating-stars.blade.php . This partial receives two variables: $averageRating (a number from 0 to 5) and $reviewCount (how many reviews exist). It rounds the average for the stars but preserves one decimal place in the text. For example, 3.6 displays ★★★★☆ and 3.6 / 5 .       @php\n    // Round the average to a whole number of filled stars (0–5).\n    $filledStars = max(0, min(5, (int) round($averageRating ?? 0)));\n@endphp\n\n@if ($reviewCount > 0) @for ($star = 1; $star <= 5; $star++)\n                {{ $star <= $filledStars ? '★' : '☆' }}\n            @endfor {{ number_format($averageRating, 1) }} / 5\n            ({{ $reviewCount }} {{ \\Illuminate\\Support\\Str::plural('review', $reviewCount) }}) @else Not rated yet @endif resources/views/books/partials/rating-stars.blade.php · create this file     @php\n    // Round the average to a whole number of filled stars (0–5).\n    $filledStars = max(0, min(5, (int) round($averageRating ?? 0)));\n@endphp\n\n@if ($reviewCount > 0)\n    <div class=\"flex items-center gap-2 text-sm\">\n        <span class=\"text-amber-500\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n            @for ($star = 1; $star <= 5; $star++)\n                {{ $star <= $filledStars ? '★' : '☆' }}\n            @endfor\n        </span>\n        <span class=\"text-gray-600\">\n            {{ number_format($averageRating, 1) }} / 5\n            ({{ $reviewCount }} {{ \\Illuminate\\Support\\Str::plural('review', $reviewCount) }})\n        </span>\n    </div>\n@else\n    <p class=\"text-sm text-gray-500\">Not rated yet</p>\n@endif How the star loop works @for repeats five times, once for each star. If the current position is at or below $filledStars , Blade prints a filled star ( ★ ); otherwise it prints an outline ( ☆ ). aria-hidden=\"true\" prevents screen readers from reading five decorative symbols; the adjacent numeric rating and review count communicate the same information. When there are no reviews, show Not rated yet , not zero stars implying a poor rating.      Checkpoint 4 · Use the stars on each book card Open resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php . Find the existing paragraph that prints reviews_avg_rating , reviews_count and “No reviews yet”. Replace that whole rating paragraph with the include below; don’t leave both versions on the card. Keep the rest of your card styling as you designed it. These fields are already provided by the book index controller using withAvg('reviews', 'rating') and withCount('reviews') .       @include('books.partials.rating-stars', [\n    'averageRating' => $book->reviews_avg_rating,\n    'reviewCount' => $book->reviews_count,\n]) resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php · replace existing rating paragraph     @include('books.partials.rating-stars', [\n    'averageRating' => $book->reviews_avg_rating,\n    'reviewCount' => $book->reviews_count,\n]) Checkpoint 5 · Verify what students actually see Open /books with books that have: no reviews , one review rated 5, and several reviews with different ratings. Check that the number of filled stars changes, the decimal average remains correct, and the review count uses “review” versus “reviews”. Try the page at a narrow viewport and check that stars do not break the card layout. If the new styles are not appearing, run npm run build from your Laravel src directory and refresh the browser.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation One column on narrow screens, two on medium screens and three on large screens, unless you justify another arrangement. Long titles and missing covers remain readable. Book links have visible keyboard focus; search, empty states and pagination still work. Each rated book displays five star positions, a numeric average and review count; books with no reviews say “Not rated yet”.      Stretch goal Offer a compact list view alongside the grid while reusing the same star-rating partial, without introducing a JavaScript framework.      Improve the existing three-column catalogue layout with stronger visual hierarchy, consistent cards and better small-screen behaviour.    The book index already provides responsive columns; the design challenge is to improve its presentation without losing working functionality.  ","label":"X13-S01"},{"title":"Redesign the book detail page","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x13-s02","excerpt":"Give the book cover, details and reviews a clear hierarchy using responsive layout and reusable Blade markup.","body":"Your design challenge A visitor should quickly understand which book they are viewing, who wrote it and what others think. Aim for a more inviting page without changing the data model or the review actions.      Where to begin Starting point: Use your completed Module 09 Book Reviews app. This challenge improves its existing interface; it does not require new controllers, migrations or external front-end libraries. Open resources/views/books/show.blade.php . Identify the existing cover, title, author links, rating and review list. If reviews are rendered using a partial, preserve that inclusion rather than copying its code.      Checkpoint 1 · Sketch the layout Sketch a mobile view with the cover above the details, and a desktop view with the cover beside the details. Decide where the review heading and “Write a Review” action belong. Place the key information before less important details.       {{-- Move your existing book-cover markup here. --}} {{ $book->title }} {{-- Move existing author, year and rating markup here. --}} resources/views/books/show.blade.php · responsive layout idea     <div class=\"grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,240px)_1fr]\">\n    <div class=\"rounded-xl bg-gray-50 p-5\">\n        {{-- Move your existing book-cover markup here. --}}\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"space-y-4\">\n        <h1 class=\"text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900\">\n            {{ $book->title }}\n        </h1>\n        {{-- Move existing author, year and rating markup here. --}}\n    </div>\n</div> Checkpoint 2 · Add the same stars to the detail page Reuse the rating-stars.blade.php partial from E4-01. Open resources/views/books/show.blade.php and put the include below beside the book title and author details, replacing any duplicate numeric rating display. Unlike the index cards, the detail page has the book’s loaded reviews collection available, so calculate the average and count from that collection. No controller or database changes are required. An unrated book still shows “Not rated yet”.       @include('books.partials.rating-stars', [\n    'averageRating' => $book->reviews->avg('rating'),\n    'reviewCount' => $book->reviews->count(),\n]) resources/views/books/show.blade.php · add beside book details     @include('books.partials.rating-stars', [\n    'averageRating' => $book->reviews->avg('rating'),\n    'reviewCount' => $book->reviews->count(),\n]) If you started with E4-02 The extensions are optional. If you have not completed E4-01, first create the books/partials/rating-stars.blade.php file using the example in that activity, then add the include above. The detail page should not assume the card redesign is already complete.      Checkpoint 3 · Make reviews easier to scan Separate the book summary from the reviews with a section heading and generous spacing. Reuse the existing review partials, keeping edit/delete controls and ownership checks intact. Try consistent spacing between reviews and a readable text width.       Reader reviews {{-- Keep your current review list, review partial and actions here. --}} resources/views/books/show.blade.php · review section idea     <section class=\"mt-10 border-t border-gray-200 pt-8\" aria-labelledby=\"reviews-heading\">\n    <h2 id=\"reviews-heading\" class=\"mb-5 text-2xl font-semibold\">\n        Reader reviews\n    </h2>\n    {{-- Keep your current review list, review partial and actions here. --}}\n</section> Checkpoint 4 · Check the experience Review the page with no reviews, many reviews, a long title and a missing cover. Verify the existing “Write a Review” link and management actions. Test mobile widths and keyboard navigation.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation The important book details are easy to scan on mobile and desktop. Existing author links, review content, and create/edit/delete review actions still work. Section headings form a sensible order and controls can be reached by keyboard. The star rating matches the book’s reviews and gracefully handles books without ratings.      Stretch goal Experiment with showing a breakdown of five-star, four-star and other reviews. Keep the main average and review count easy to understand.      Give the book cover, details and reviews a clear hierarchy using responsive layout and reusable Blade markup.    Rearrange the Blade presentation while preserving the working features and access rules.  ","label":"X13-S02"},{"title":"Make forms and feedback clearer","type":"Extension","url":"learning-lab/index.html#x13-s03","excerpt":"Improve labels, validation messages, confirmation feedback and keyboard accessibility in the existing forms.","body":"Your design challenge Help people complete forms confidently and understand what happened afterwards. Focus on clear labels, useful error messages, sensible spacing and visible success feedback rather than decorative effects.      Where to begin Starting point: Use your completed Module 09 Book Reviews app. This challenge improves its existing interface; it does not require new controllers, migrations or external front-end libraries. Inspect resources/views/books/partials/form.blade.php and resources/views/reviews/partials/form.blade.php , then look at the create/edit pages that include them. Keep existing @csrf , @method and validation logic.      Checkpoint 1 · Make a field understandable Every input needs a visible label. Link its label to the field with matching for and id values. Use old() and @error so the visitor can recover from a failed submission without starting again.       Book title @error('title') {{ $message }} @enderror resources/views/books/partials/form.blade.php · field example     <div class=\"space-y-2\">\n    <label for=\"title\" class=\"block text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900\">\n        Book title\n    </label>\n    <input id=\"title\" name=\"title\" type=\"text\" required\n           value=\"{{ old('title', $book->title ?? '') }}\"\n           aria-describedby=\"title-error\"\n           class=\"w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 focus:border-indigo-600 focus:ring-indigo-600\">\n    @error('title')\n        <p id=\"title-error\" class=\"text-sm text-red-700\">{{ $message }}</p>\n    @enderror\n</div> Checkpoint 2 · Explain the result After saving, a short success message can reassure users that the action worked. Check whether your controller already flashes a message with with(\"success\", ...) . If so, render it in a common layout or on the destination page. Do not accidentally show a success message before the database action completes.       @if (session('success')) {{ session('success') }} @endif Blade view or shared layout · success message     @if (session('success'))\n    <p role=\"status\" class=\"mb-5 rounded-lg border border-green-200 bg-green-50 p-4 text-green-900\">\n        {{ session('success') }}\n    </p>\n@endif Checkpoint 3 · Review accessibility and deletion Check focus states on inputs and buttons, errors after validation, and a clear distinction between Save and Delete actions. For destructive operations, consider a confirmation step and ensure your existing DELETE method and CSRF protection remain in place.      Progressive hints       Check your implementation Labels are visible and associated with the right fields. Invalid submissions show useful errors and preserve entered values. A successful action provides clear feedback and delete actions are identifiable. The forms work without JavaScript, and all buttons have visible focus.      Stretch goal Add a helpful empty state for the personal reviews page, with a link to browse books when the user has not reviewed anything yet.      Improve labels, validation messages, confirmation feedback and keyboard accessibility in the existing forms.    Clear feedback and retained input make forms easier to use without changing the underlying Laravel workflow.  ","label":"X13-S03"},{"title":"Putting it all together","type":"Concepts","url":"request-trace.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 08 / 08 7 MIN READ THE COMPLETE PICTURE Putting it all together The most useful mental model is a complete journey. Follow an edit request and explain why each part of your application is invo","body":"CONCEPT 08 / 08 7 MIN READ THE COMPLETE PICTURE Putting it all together The most useful mental model is a complete journey. Follow an edit request and explain why each part of your application is involved. 01 / TRACE IT A user edits a book. 01 Browser PATCH /books/7 02 Route books.update 03 Middleware Checks access 04 Controller Validates input 05 Model Updates record 06 Response Redirects Your exact request may pass through different middleware and authorisation rules. Check your own routes and controller code. 02 / FIND EACH STEP Here is a simplified example. routes/web.php Copy Route::resource('books', BookController::class); BookController.php · update() (simplified) Copy public function update(Request $request, Book $book)\n{\n    $data = $request->validate([\n        'title' => ['required', 'string'],\n    ]);\n\n    $book->update($data);\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n} The real controller may handle authors, cover images and permissions too. Terminal · inspect your app Copy php artisan route:list --path=books\nphp artisan test 03 / CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING Can you follow the request through your own code? TRY THIS OUT Choose a form in your own application. Find its route, the controller method it calls, any validation, the model operation and the response returned to the browser. Open each file as you work through the request. Check that every step connects to the next. ? Why should a controller validate input before saving it? Reveal answer Validation checks submitted values against the application’s rules before they are saved, helping prevent missing or incorrect data. TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Return to these guided lessons when you're ready to practise.","label":""},{"title":"MVC and the request lifecycle","type":"Concepts","url":"mvc-request.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 02 / 08 7 MIN READ THE BIG PICTURE MVC and the request lifecycle When someone visits a URL, Laravel connects several small pieces of code to produce the result. MVC is a way of separating thei","body":"CONCEPT 02 / 08 7 MIN READ THE BIG PICTURE MVC and the request lifecycle When someone visits a URL, Laravel connects several small pieces of code to produce the result. MVC is a way of separating their responsibilities. 01 / ONE REQUEST What happens when you visit /books? 01 Browser GET /books 02 Route Finds index() 03 Controller Coordinates the work 04 Model Fetches books 05 View Builds HTML The controller gives data to the view, and Laravel sends the finished HTML back to the browser. Middleware may check access before the controller runs. 02 / FOUR PIECES Look at the same page in four places. routes/web.php Copy Route::get('/books', [BookController::class, 'index'])\n    ->name('books.index'); app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php Copy public function index()\n{\n    $books = Book::all();\n\n    return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n} app/Models/Book.php Copy class Book extends Model\n{\n    // Eloquent represents a book record.\n} resources/views/books/index.blade.php Copy @foreach ($books as $book)\n    <p>{{ $book->title }}</p>\n@endforeach 03 / HTTP REQUESTS The browser tells Laravel what it wants. Method Typical meaning Book Reviews example GET Read a page Show the catalogue POST Create something Save a new book PATCH Change something Update a book DELETE Remove something Delete a book ? A user submits a form to update a book. Which part receives the request first: the route, controller or model? Reveal answer Laravel matches the route first. The route then directs the request to the appropriate controller action (after relevant middleware). TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Return to these guided lessons when you're ready to practise.","label":""},{"title":"Blade templates, explained","type":"Concepts","url":"blade-templates.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 03 / 08 6 MIN READ HTML + A LITTLE LOGIC Blade templates, explained Blade files look like HTML, but contain unfamiliar symbols such as {{ }} , @if and @foreach . They are simply Laravel’s way ","body":"CONCEPT 03 / 08 6 MIN READ HTML + A LITTLE LOGIC Blade templates, explained Blade files look like HTML, but contain unfamiliar symbols such as {{ }} , @if and @foreach . They are simply Laravel’s way of putting information and small decisions into a page. 01 / FIRST, THE IDEA HTML is still the foundation. A Blade template is a file ending in .blade.php , usually inside resources/views . Laravel processes the Blade instructions on the server and sends ordinary HTML to the browser. Controller Gets the data and passes it to the view. Blade view Displays the data and chooses which HTML to show. BookController.php · pass data to a view Copy public function show(Book $book)\n{\n    return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n} The view now has a $book variable available. books/show.blade.php · display the title Copy <h1>{{ $book->title }}</h1> {{ }} means “display this value”. Blade escapes special HTML characters by default, helping prevent unsafe HTML from being injected. 02 / LEARN THE SYMBOLS Four patterns you will see repeatedly. Blade · display a variable Copy <p>{{ $book->title }}</p> Print the title of the book. Blade · show something only when a condition is true Copy @if ($book->cover_image)\n    <img src=\"{{ $book->cover_image }}\" alt=\"Book cover\">\n@else\n    <p>No cover available</p>\n@endif @if checks a condition. @else provides a fallback. @endif ends the block. Blade · repeat HTML for each record Copy @foreach ($books as $book)\n    <li>{{ $book->title }}</li>\n@endforeach @foreach repeats the list item for every book. The singular $book represents the current item. Blade · handle an empty list Copy @forelse ($books as $book)\n    <p>{{ $book->title }}</p>\n@empty\n    <p>No books yet.</p>\n@endforelse @forelse works like a loop, but also handles an empty collection. 03 / REUSING PAGE PIECES Components, includes and forms. Blade · reuse a template fragment Copy @include('books.partials.card', ['book' => $book]) This inserts resources/views/books/partials/card.blade.php and passes it the selected book. Blade · Breeze layout and form directives Copy <x-app-layout>\n    <h1>Edit {{ $book->title }}</h1>\n    <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.update', $book) }}\">\n        @csrf\n        @method('PUT')\n        <input name=\"title\" value=\"{{ old('title', $book->title) }}\">\n        <button>Save</button>\n    </form>\n</x-app-layout> The <x-app-layout> component supplies a shared layout. route() builds a URL, @csrf adds a security token, @method specifies the intended method, and old() can restore submitted form values. A Blade file may mix plain HTML, components and directives. Read the plain HTML first, then work out what each Blade expression adds. 04 / CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING Read a template one line at a time. ? What does {{ $book->title }} do? Reveal answer It displays the book’s title as escaped text in the HTML sent to the browser. ? Which directive would you use to display one card for every book? Reveal answer @foreach (or @forelse if you also want an empty-list message). ? Does the browser receive the literal @if and @foreach text? Reveal answer No. Laravel processes those instructions on the server and sends the resulting HTML to the browser. SEE IT IN THE PROJECT Find Blade in the Learning Lab. Compare these short examples with the complete views as you create them.","label":""},{"title":"Laravel and its tools","type":"Concepts","url":"laravel-tools.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 01 / 08 5 MIN READ THE TOOLKIT Laravel and its tools Laravel gives a PHP web application a sensible structure and tools for common jobs. You focus on what makes your application useful. 01 / W","body":"CONCEPT 01 / 08 5 MIN READ THE TOOLKIT Laravel and its tools Laravel gives a PHP web application a sensible structure and tools for common jobs. You focus on what makes your application useful. 01 / WHY A FRAMEWORK A framework gives you a starting structure. Without a framework You would have to decide how to handle every request, connect to the database and organise the project. With Laravel Routing, database access, templates, validation and testing fit into established patterns. 02 / THE TOOLS Different tools do different jobs. Composer Installs and manages PHP packages, including Laravel itself. Artisan Laravel’s command-line helper for generating files, running migrations and tests. Tinker A small interactive console for trying PHP and Eloquent expressions. Blade A template language for combining HTML with data from Laravel. Breeze Starter code for registration, login and account management. Docker Runs the project’s development services in a predictable environment. Terminal Copy composer install\nphp artisan route:list\nphp artisan tinker Run Artisan commands in the Laravel project directory, inside the appropriate development container. resources/views/books/index.blade.php Copy <h1>Books</h1>\n<p>{{ $book->title }}</p> Blade escapes output between {{ and }} by default. If {{ }} or @foreach looks unfamiliar, see the Blade templates concept page for a gentle introduction. 03 / CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING Which tool would you use? ? Which tool do you use to run Laravel commands from the terminal? Reveal answer Artisan. For example, php artisan route:list shows your routes. ? Which tool adds PHP packages to your project: Composer or Blade? Reveal answer Composer installs packages. Blade is used to build page templates. TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Return to these guided lessons when you're ready to practise.","label":""},{"title":"Roles and authorisation","type":"Concepts","url":"authorisation.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 06 / 08 7 MIN READ WHO CAN DO WHAT? Roles and authorisation Logging in tells Laravel who you are. Authorisation decides what you are allowed to do. Different checks answer different questions:","body":"CONCEPT 06 / 08 7 MIN READ WHO CAN DO WHAT? Roles and authorisation Logging in tells Laravel who you are. Authorisation decides what you are allowed to do. Different checks answer different questions: validation asks whether submitted information is acceptable; authorisation asks whether this user may perform the action. Both matter. 01 / TWO DIFFERENT CHECKS Being signed in is not enough. Authentication Are you signed in? The auth middleware checks this. Role-based access Are you an administrator? Our role:admin middleware checks this. Record ownership Did you write this review? Check the review's user_id . 02 / ROLES AND ROUTES Only administrators manage books. An application can store a role such as user or admin on each account. Laravel runs middleware before a protected controller action. routes/web.php · a simplified example Copy Route::middleware(['auth', 'role:admin'])->group(function () {\n    Route::resource('books', BookController::class)\n        ->only(['create', 'store', 'edit', 'update', 'destroy']);\n}); auth means logged in. role:admin checks the role. If a signed-in non-admin tries these actions, Laravel returns 403 Forbidden . The name role here is a middleware alias : a short label registered in bootstrap/app.php for our own middleware class. The class contains the actual role check. Middleware · the key check Copy if ($request->user()?->role !== 'admin') {\n    abort(403);\n} The check stops non-admin users even if they enter a URL manually. This is an illustrative extract, not the complete middleware file. 03 / OWNERSHIP AND INTERFACE You can edit your own review, not someone else's. Roles are not the only kind of permission. A user might be allowed to write reviews but only change reviews they created. ReviewController.php · record ownership check Copy if ($review->user_id !== auth()->id()) {\n    abort(403);\n} $review->user_id is the review's owner; auth()->id() is the signed-in user. Different IDs mean no permission. In Blade, the interface can show actions conditionally: books/index.blade.php · show Add Book to admins Copy @if (auth()->user()?->role === 'admin')\n    <a href=\"{{ route('books.create') }}\">Add Book</a>\n@endif This improves the interface; middleware still provides the real protection. CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING What happens if a signed-in ordinary user manually visits /books/create ? Reveal answer Laravel should return 403 Forbidden . Hiding the Add Book link alone would not be enough. TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Use two accounts to compare administrator actions and review ownership.","label":""},{"title":"Build your own Laravel application.","type":"Assignment","url":"assignment.html","excerpt":"CC-Y2-AWD / 2026 ASSIGNMENT Build your own Laravel application. Choose a case study, design the data, build its features, test your work and explain how it works. Book Reviews is your worked example, ","body":"CC-Y2-AWD / 2026 ASSIGNMENT Build your own Laravel application. Choose a case study, design the data, build its features, test your work and explain how it works. Book Reviews is your worked example, not your submission. Download original PDF ↓ View requirements ↓ 100% continuous assessment Two assessment points 12-week project ASSESSMENT AT A GLANCE ASSESSMENT POINT 1 · WORK TO DATE 30% Friday 30 October 2026 Code + screencast due 23:59 Viva: Tuesday 3 November 2026 Requirements and rubric → ASSESSMENT POINT 2 · FINAL SUBMISSION 70% Friday 11 December 2026 Code + screencast due 23:59 Viva: Tuesday 15 December 2026 Requirements and rubric → 01 / THE ASSIGNMENT Your own case study You will design and build a Laravel web application for a case study of your own choice, working on it across the whole 12 weeks of the module. The Book Reviews application and its tutorial notes are your worked example. They show you, step by step, how to build each part of a Laravel application. You do not submit Book Reviews. Apply each technique from the tutorial to your own case study. Module aim and learning outcomes The aim of this module is to improve your knowledge and skills in relation to the development and testing of web applications, and provide you with the understanding, skills and competencies to use server-side web application frameworks. LO1 — Explain the principles of server-side Web Application Frameworks LO2 — Design, develop, test and debug a web application using a server-side web application framework LO3 — Document, explain and defend the design and implementation of web applications Your case study must include A main entity , the “thing” your application is about (equivalent to a Book in Book Reviews) At least one one-to-many (1:M) relationship, e.g. one Book has many Reviews At least one many-to-many (M:N) relationship, e.g. Books and Authors At least two roles , with some pages accessible only to users with a given role Requirement Book Reviews example Main entity Book 1:M relationship A Book has many Reviews M:N relationship A Book has many Authors, and an Author has many Books ( author_book join table) Roles Only admins can create, edit and delete Books and Authors. Users can only edit and delete their own Reviews. Extensions Once your core application is working, you can earn further marks at Assessment Point 2 by adding features beyond what the Book Reviews tutorial covers. The examples below are indicative, not an exhaustive list . You can explore other extension ideas in the Learning Lab’s Advanced Extensions section after completing the core application. Image uploads with resizing or validation Pagination and sorting on index pages An admin dashboard showing summary statistics A further relationship or entity Email notifications Discuss any extension with your lecturer before you build it, so you both agree it is feasible and worthwhile. Finish the core requirements first: an extension will not make up for missing core functionality. 02 / TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS What your app must use Laravel 12 with Blade views , no Livewire, Inertia, React or Vue Laravel Breeze (Blade stack) for registration and login MySQL in the Docker environment provided with Book Reviews Eloquent models and relationships, no raw SQL Migrations, factories and seeders; php artisan migrate:fresh --seed must build realistic demo data Resource controllers and route model binding Laravel validation for all form input Passing PHPUnit tests using php artisan test Using AI tools You may use AI tools to understand errors and fix bugs. We recommend writing your own code so you develop the skills and understanding to build the application independently. If you use AI-generated code, you must be able to explain every line, and record your AI use for the final design document. IADT guidance on AI and academic integrity ↗ 03 / ASSESSMENT How your work is assessed Your application code in your classroom50.org repository ; include only your own case study Your Git commit history , with regular meaningful commits throughout the module A recorded screencast , uploaded to the OneDrive folder created for you Your attendance and observed progress in class A viva , a short one-to-one discussion of your code, which gates your marks The viva During scheduled class hours, you will trace one or two requests through your application, for example submitting an edit form. Explain what happens at each stage: Route → Middleware → Controller → Validation → Model / relationships → Database → Response You will have your code open. You do not need to memorise it, but you do need to understand it. A time slot will be published in advance. Viva outcome Effect on mark Confirmed Your rubric marks stand. Partly confirmed Marks for criteria depending on code you could not explain are capped at 40%. Not confirmed, or absent The assessment point is graded as a Fail. If you cannot attend for reasons covered by the Institute's extenuating circumstances procedures, contact your lecturer promptly to rearrange it. 04 / ASSESSMENT POINT 1 · 30% Work to date Code + screencast: 23:59, Friday 30 October 2026. Viva: Tuesday 3 November 2026. What we expect By this point, we expect the equivalent of Lesson 3 of Book Reviews in your own case study: full CRUD on the main entity. This is an expectation, not a requirement. The minimum to pass is working index and show pages (equivalent of Lesson 2). Deliverables Your classroom50.org repository with regular commits, runnable Laravel application, migrations, models and relationships, factories and seeders, controllers and Blade views (at least index and show), passing PHPUnit tests, and a README.md explaining the case study and relationships with an ERD A screencast of maximum 5 minutes in your OneDrive folder showing the application running with seeded data, php artisan test passing, and explaining the tables and 1:M/M:N relationships Marking rubric Scroll horizontally on smaller screens. Criterion LOs Fail (0–39%) Pass (40–59%) Merit (60–79%) Distinction (80–100%) Data design & models (25%) LO2, LO3 Migrations are missing, or the 1:M or M:N relationship is missing. Migrations create all tables, including a 1:M and an M:N relationship with foreign keys. Models define these relationships. A seeder creates some data. README describes the case study. Factories and seeders produce realistic data for every entity, including the join table. Columns use appropriate data types and constraints. README includes a clear ERD. The schema enforces the case study's rules in the database (e.g. unique and composite indexes, sensible on delete behaviour), and the README explains why. Application functionality (30%) LO1, LO2 The application does not run, or the main entity has no working index and show pages. Working index and show pages for the main entity, using route model binding. show displays related data from at least one relationship. Full CRUD on the main entity, with validation, a shared form partial for create and edit, and form repopulation with old() after a validation error. All of Merit, plus work beyond Lesson 3 (e.g. CRUD on a second entity), with clean, consistent, well-organised code. Testing (15%) LO2 No tests, or tests do not pass. Unit tests for at least one model that create a row and check a relationship. All tests pass. Unit tests for every model, and feature tests for the index and show pages. All tests pass. Feature tests for the full CRUD flow, including validation failures. All tests pass. Screencast (10%) LO3 No screencast, or it does not show the application. Shows the application running and tests passing. Also explains the database design and relationships clearly. A clear, confident explanation that links what the viewer sees on screen to the code that produces it. Progress & engagement (20%) LO1, LO2, LO3 Little or no evidence of regular work. Few commits; poor attendance. Attends class but shows limited progress. Commits are infrequent or unclear. Regular commits with clear messages. Attends and works consistently in class. Consistently works ahead, shows initiative in solving problems, and engages fully in every class. 05 / ASSESSMENT POINT 2 · 70% Final submission Code + screencast: 23:59, Friday 11 December 2026. Viva: Tuesday 15 December 2026. What we expect A complete application covering Lessons 1–6 of Book Reviews, plus role-based authorisation, applied to your own case study. Deliverables Your classroom50.org repository showing regular commits across all 12 weeks, including full CRUD on your main entity, CRUD on other entities where needed, M:N relationships managed through forms, search on the main entity, role-based authorisation for at least two roles, any agreed extensions, passing tests, and a final design document in docs/ A screencast of maximum 10 minutes in OneDrive demonstrating all features as each role, passing tests and main design decisions We suggest using role middleware, as demonstrated in class. You may use a different approach if you explain and justify your choice in your design document. Design document Keep this concise. Include the following nine sections as a PDF or Markdown file in docs/ . Write this document in your own words . Describe your own work, decisions and understanding rather than copying explanations from the tutorial, documentation or AI-generated text. The word counts are maximums per section , not targets. Aim for approximately 900 words or fewer overall, excluding your ERD, route table and code snippets. Case study max 75 words : what your application does and who uses it. Database design max 125 words : include an ERD and explain the main tables, relationships, constraints and design choices. Routes max 50 words : provide a table showing HTTP method, URI, controller action and middleware, with any brief notes needed. How a request is handled max 125 words : trace one real request through your application, from route to response. Authorisation max 100 words : identify your roles, permissions, enforcement and why you chose this approach. Testing max 100 words : what you tested, why, and anything you deliberately did not test. Extensions max 75 words : describe any additional features and how they work. If none, say so briefly. AI Agent Use max 100 words : name tools used, give specific examples of prompts and responses, explain what you did with the output, and reflect on when AI helped or misled you. If you did not use AI, state this. Reflection max 150 words : what went well, what you would improve and any known problems. Use diagrams, route tables and short code excerpts where useful. Focus on explaining your own decisions , not describing Laravel in general. Marking rubric Scroll horizontally on smaller screens. Criterion LOs Fail (0–39%) Pass (40–59%) Merit (60–79%) Distinction (80–100%) Application functionality (20%) LO1, LO2 The application does not run, or core functionality is broken. Full CRUD on the main entity. Users can register and log in. Also: CRUD for the other entities the case study needs; the M:N relationship is managed through forms; search works; database constraint errors are shown as friendly messages. All of Merit, plus relationships are eager-loaded (no N+1 queries), error handling is robust, and the code is clean and consistent throughout. Authentication & authorisation (10%) LO1, LO2 Little or no authorisation — fewer than two roles, or no pages restricted by role. At least two roles, and some pages that can only be accessed by users with a given role. Authorisation checks suit the case study and are applied to every page and action that needs them — e.g. in Book Reviews, only admins can create, edit and delete Books and Authors, and users can only edit and delete their own Reviews. All of Merit, plus the interface only shows the actions a user is allowed to perform, and the authorisation checks are tested. Data design (10%) LO2, LO3 Required relationships are missing or broken. Schema includes the required 1:M and M:N relationships, with migrations, factories and seeders. Appropriate data types and constraints. Migrations correct mistakes by adding new migrations, not by editing old ones. The schema enforces the case study's rules in the database, and the design is clearly justified in the design document. Testing (15%) LO2 Few or no tests, or tests do not pass. Tests for every model and for the main entity's CRUD. All tests pass. Feature tests for every controller, including validation failures, search (matching and non-matching) and authorisation. All tests pass. A comprehensive, well- organised suite, including tests for what each role can and cannot do, and a test that proves N+1 queries are fixed. All tests pass. Extensions (10%) LO2 No agreed extension attempted, or it does not work. One small extension works. One substantial extension, or several small ones, working and tested. Substantial extensions, well integrated, tested, and explained in the design document. Design document (15%) LO1, LO3 No design document, or it is missing most sections. Includes most sections, with limited explanation. AI use is mentioned without detail. All sections complete, with an ERD and a clear request trace. AI use is described with specific examples. A clear, professional document that justifies its design decisions, explains the framework's role accurately, and reflects critically on AI use. Screencast (5%) LO3 No screencast, or it does not show the application. Shows the main features and tests passing. Demonstrates all features and roles, and explains the main design decisions. A clear, confident demonstration that explains and defends the design. Progress & engagement (15%) LO1, LO2, LO3 Little or no evidence of continued work since Assessment Point 1. Some progress, but commits are infrequent and attendance is inconsistent. Regular commits throughout, with clear messages. Attends and works consistently in class. Sustained effort across all 12 weeks, with initiative shown beyond the core requirements. 06 / BEFORE YOU SUBMIT Submission checklist Commit and push all work to your classroom50.org repository php artisan migrate:fresh --seed runs without errors php artisan test runs and all tests pass Upload your screencast to OneDrive and respect the time limit Assessment Point 1: your README describes your case study and includes an ERD Assessment Point 2: your design document is in the docs/ folder Prefer the original wording? Download the original assignment brief (PDF) ↓","label":""},{"title":"Testing and debugging","type":"Concepts","url":"testing-debugging.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 07 / 08 6 MIN READ QUALITY & CONFIDENCE Testing and debugging Automated tests give you a repeatable way to check behaviour. Debugging helps you explain and fix problems when the behaviour is w","body":"CONCEPT 07 / 08 6 MIN READ QUALITY & CONFIDENCE Testing and debugging Automated tests give you a repeatable way to check behaviour. Debugging helps you explain and fix problems when the behaviour is wrong. 01 / TWO TYPES OF TEST What are you checking? Model / unit-style test Check a model rule, a relationship or a small piece of logic. Feature test Send a simulated HTTP request and check the response, data or permissions. Terminal Copy php artisan test\nphp artisan test --filter=BookTest 02 / A FEATURE TEST Arrange, act, assert. tests/Feature/BookTest.php · illustrative test Copy public function test_books_page_requires_login(): void\n{\n    // Act\n    $response = $this->get(route('books.index'));\n\n    // Assert\n    $response->assertRedirect(route('login'));\n} This example assumes the catalogue is behind auth middleware, as in the guided app. Arrange Create the records or users needed for the test. Act Make the request or call the method. Assert Check the result matches what you expected. 03 / WHEN IT FAILS Read the error before changing code. Symptom First thing to check View [reviews.create] not found Is the Blade file in resources/views/reviews/create.blade.php ? Class ... Request does not exist Does the controller import Illuminate\\Http\\Request ? 404 on a route What does php artisan route:list show? Test fails unexpectedly What did the test expect? What actually happened? ? Your test expected a redirect but received a 200 response. What should you investigate? Reveal answer Check the route and middleware, whether the test user is authenticated, and what response the controller actually returns. TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Return to these guided lessons when you're ready to practise.","label":""},{"title":"CRUD: reading and changing data","type":"Concepts","url":"crud.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 05 / 08 12 MIN READ COMMON ACTIONS CRUD: reading and changing data Most web apps let people create, read, update and delete records. Laravel resource controllers name these common operations c","body":"CONCEPT 05 / 08 12 MIN READ COMMON ACTIONS CRUD: reading and changing data Most web apps let people create, read, update and delete records. Laravel resource controllers name these common operations consistently. 01 / THE CRUD MAP Four operations, seven controller methods. Operation Actions What the user experiences Read index() , show() Browse all books, open one book Create create() , store() Display a blank form, save its contents Update edit() , update() Display a populated form, save changes Delete destroy() Remove a record 02 / READ Show a list, or open one record. index() loads a collection of books for a listing page. show() displays one specific book. Both are handled by GET requests, so they should not change the database. BookController.php · simplified read actions Copy // List books on the index page.\npublic function index()\n{\n    $books = Book::all();\n    return view('books.index', compact('books'));\n}\n\n// Show one book. Laravel resolves $book from the URL.\npublic function show(Book $book)\n{\n    return view('books.show', compact('book'));\n} Book::all() returns all books. compact('books') passes the variable to the Blade template. For larger collections, use pagination as in the Learning Lab. 03 / CREATE Show a blank form, then save a new book. create() shows the empty form using a GET request. When the user submits it, a POST request goes to store() , which validates the values and creates the new database record. BookController.php · simplified create actions Copy // Show an empty form.\npublic function create()\n{\n    return view('books.create');\n}\n\n// Process the submitted form.\npublic function store(Request $request)\n{\n    $data = $request->validate([\n        'title' => ['required', 'string'],\n    ]);\n\n    Book::create($data);\n    return redirect()->route('books.index');\n} books/create.blade.php · form Copy <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.store') }}\">\n    @csrf\n    <input name=\"title\" required>\n    <button type=\"submit\">Create book</button>\n</form> @csrf adds a hidden security token to the form. When you submit it, Laravel checks that the token matches your session. This helps prevent another website from secretly making your browser submit a form to your application while you are signed in. It does not check whether the input is valid or whether you have permission to create books; those are separate checks. 04 / UPDATE Load existing values, then save the edits. edit() shows a form containing the selected book’s current details. update() validates the submission and changes that existing record , instead of inserting a new one. BookController.php · simplified update actions Copy // Display the edit form with the existing book.\npublic function edit(Book $book)\n{\n    return view('books.edit', compact('book'));\n}\n\n// Validate and save the changes.\npublic function update(Request $request, Book $book)\n{\n    $data = $request->validate([\n        'title' => ['required', 'string'],\n    ]);\n\n    $book->update($data);\n    return redirect()->route('books.show', $book);\n} books/edit.blade.php · form Copy <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.update', $book) }}\">\n    @csrf\n    @method('PUT')\n    <input name=\"title\" value=\"{{ old('title', $book->title) }}\">\n    <button type=\"submit\">Save changes</button>\n</form> old('title', $book->title) keeps submitted input after a validation error, otherwise it shows the current title. HTML forms don’t directly support PUT, so @method('PUT') tells Laravel which request method to treat it as. 05 / DELETE Remove the selected record. destroy() deletes a book identified by its route parameter. A form sends a DELETE request to that action; a normal link should not delete data. BookController.php · simplified delete action Copy // Delete a specific book, then go back to the list.\npublic function destroy(Book $book)\n{\n    $book->delete();\n    return redirect()->route('books.index');\n} books/show.blade.php · delete form Copy <form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ route('books.destroy', $book) }}\">\n    @csrf\n    @method('DELETE')\n    <button type=\"submit\">Delete book</button>\n</form> In a real app, only authorised users should be able to delete books. You should also consider how related reviews and other records are handled. 06 / FOLLOW THE ACTION What happens when you press Save? 01 Form POST /books 02 Route books.store 03 Controller store() 04 Model Book::create() 05 Response Redirect ? Which method should save edits to an existing book: edit(), update(), or store()? Reveal answer update() saves changes. edit() displays the edit form, while store() creates a new record. ? Which method displays an empty form, and which method saves a new book? Reveal answer create() shows the form; store() validates and saves its submitted data. TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Return to these guided lessons when you're ready to practise.","label":""},{"title":"Advanced Web Development.","type":"Module home","url":"index.html","excerpt":"BSc (Hons) Creative Computing / CC2 · Semester 1 Advanced Web Development. Learn how to build complete, database-driven web applications with Laravel, from the first route to a working, tested project","body":"BSc (Hons) Creative Computing / CC2 · Semester 1 Advanced Web Development. Learn how to build complete, database-driven web applications with Laravel, from the first route to a working, tested project you can explain and defend. Open the Learning Lab ↗ Explore module resources ↓ Laravel 12 PHP · Blade · MySQL Build · Test · Explain YOUR APPLICATION / THE BIG PICTURE 01 Browser What the user sees and does ◉ 02 Laravel Routes · controllers · views { } 03 Database Models · relationships · data ▤ One application. Connected pieces. 01 / THE PURPOSE From web pages to web applications. Earlier in Creative Computing, you've worked with programming, websites and databases. This module brings those skills together. Instead of building individual pages, you'll create an application where users can sign in, work with data and interact with information stored in a database. You'll learn the structure of a server-side web framework and why developers use one: it helps organise code, manage common tasks and build more reliable software. The aim isn't just to make something work. It's to understand how a request travels through your application , how to test what you've built, and how to make decisions you can explain. Those are skills you'll continue to use in software projects throughout the degree. 02 / WHAT YOU'LL PRACTISE Learn by building. Work through a complete example, then apply those ideas to a project of your own. 01 Understand the framework See how routes, controllers, Blade views and models work together to handle a web request. 02 Work with data Design tables and relationships, use migrations and Eloquent, and prepare realistic sample data. 03 Build real features Create forms, validate input, handle authentication, manage permissions and make information searchable. 04 Test and explain Use automated tests and Git, then demonstrate and explain how your application works. 03 / MATERIALS Everything in one place. Start with the Learning Lab, refer to the original notes and code, and use the brief to guide your own project. 01 / DEVELOPMENT ↗ { } Book Reviews Learning Lab The guided, step-by-step Laravel build. Start here for practical activities and optional advanced challenges. Open learning resource → 02 / REFERENCE MATERIAL ↗ ⌘ Original notes & source code Use the original tutorial notes and the completed GitHub repository as references while working through the example. Tutorial site ↗ GitHub repository ↗ 03 / THEORY & CONCEPTS ↗ ≡ Understanding Laravel Eight short concept pages with clear explanations, diagrams, code examples and quick checks. Explore concept notes → 04 / ASSESSMENT ↗ ↗ Assignment brief Your own Laravel case study, technical requirements, assessment criteria, submission instructions and viva expectations. Read assignment brief → 04 / YOUR OWN PROJECT The tutorial is your worked example. Your assignment is different: you choose a case study and build your own Laravel application using the techniques you learn. The Book Reviews application shows you how to approach the work. It is not the application you submit. Your own project needs a database design, related models, working functionality, tests and role-based access, as described in the brief. Read the full requirements and rubric ↗ 05 / OFFICIAL MODULE LEARNING OUTCOMES What you should be able to do. Learning outcomes describe what you should know, understand and be able to do by the end of this module. They help you understand what you're working towards and how your learning will be assessed. LO1 Explain the principles of server-side Web Application Frameworks LO2 Design, develop, test and debug a web application using a server-side web application framework LO3 Document, explain and defend the design and implementation of web applications READY TO START? Build something you understand. Start with the Book Reviews example. Then take what you've learned and make it your own. Go to the Learning Lab ↗","label":""},{"title":"Working with data","type":"Concepts","url":"data-eloquent.html","excerpt":"CONCEPT 04 / 08 7 MIN READ DATA & RELATIONSHIPS Working with data A database stores the records. Eloquent lets you work with those records using PHP objects, without writing SQL for each operation. 01","body":"CONCEPT 04 / 08 7 MIN READ DATA & RELATIONSHIPS Working with data A database stores the records. Eloquent lets you work with those records using PHP objects, without writing SQL for each operation. 01 / SEPARATE THE JOBS Four related ideas, four different purposes. Migration Describes a database change: tables, columns and keys. Model A PHP class used to query and change database records. Factory Defines how to generate realistic sample model data. Seeder Chooses which records to insert into a development database. Terminal Copy php artisan migrate\nphp artisan db:seed\nphp artisan tinker These commands perform different tasks: update schema, populate records and explore data. 02 / RELATIONSHIPS Books, authors and reviews are connected. Book has many Reviews 1 : many Review belongs to a Book Book has many Authors many : many Author has many Books app/Models/Book.php Copy public function reviews()\n{\n    return $this->hasMany(Review::class);\n}\n\npublic function authors()\n{\n    return $this->belongsToMany(Author::class);\n} Why do we need an author_book table? A book may have more than one author, and an author may write more than one book. Neither table can store all those links neatly in a single column. Instead, Laravel uses a pivot table called author_book . Each row connects one book to one author using their IDs. author_book book_id author_id Link 1 1 3 Link 2 1 8 Link 3 2 3 Here, Book 1 has Authors 3 and 8. Author 3 is also linked to Book 2. These are example IDs, not actual book records. The belongsToMany() relationship tells Eloquent to use that intermediate table. You can then access a book’s authors with $book->authors rather than manually joining tables. 03 / ASK FOR DATA Eloquent queries read like PHP. Tinker Copy // Count all books in the database.\nBook::count();\n\n// Get the first book, including its related authors.\nBook::with('authors')->first();\n\n// Find every book whose title contains 'Dune'.\nBook::where('title', 'like', '%Dune%')->get(); ? You added a new database column. Should you change a factory or create a migration? Reveal answer Create a migration to change the database structure. You may then update a factory if the new field should appear in sample records. TRY IT IN THE LEARNING LAB See it in the project. Return to these guided lessons when you're ready to practise.","label":""},{"title":"Theory & Concepts overview","type":"Concepts","url":"theory.html","excerpt":"THEORY / CONCEPT NOTES · EIGHT SHORT READS Understand what you're building. The Learning Lab shows you how to build the application. These notes explain what the code is doing and why , using small ex","body":"THEORY / CONCEPT NOTES · EIGHT SHORT READS Understand what you're building. The Learning Lab shows you how to build the application. These notes explain what the code is doing and why , using small examples from Book Reviews. CHOOSE A TOPIC Eight ideas. One application. Read in order or jump to whatever you're working on. LEARN IT, THEN USE IT Keep the code close. Use these pages alongside your own project and the Book Reviews walkthrough. Your goal is to understand what each part of an application contributes. Open Learning Lab ↗","label":""}];
